[[:db/cas 17592186055233 :question/problem "![](images/b7/b75586450ddee4f682f02539e02462d0aa5f88830b2989cc7cc2f4f6df635ade.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(5+7x)^{3}(f(x)-x)^{4}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above." "![](images/b7/b75586450ddee4f682f02539e02462d0aa5f88830b2989cc7cc2f4f6df635ade.png)\n\nFind $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-1}(5+7x)^{3}(f(x)-x)^{4}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above."] [:db/cas 17592186064753 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2$ and $f(-2)=2,$ then what is the value of\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-2)}({(f(x))}^{2}+4)\\,?\n$$" "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2$ and $f(-2)=2,$ then what is the value of\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-2)}({(f(x))}^{2}+4)\\,?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186069218 :question/problem "![](images/7b/7b3544694cc41819941bdc05e2e1c9f388c5c538dbc5a58dbf02f6ae0bc685f7.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of $f(x)$, then$\\lim_{x \\to  1^{-}}f(x) =$\n{{field-1}} ,\n$\\lim_{x \\to  1^{+}}f(x) =$\n{{field-2}}." "![](images/7b/7b3544694cc41819941bdc05e2e1c9f388c5c538dbc5a58dbf02f6ae0bc685f7.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of $f(x)$, then$\\lim_{x \\to  1^{-}}f(x) =$\n{{field-1}} ,\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to  1^{+}}f(x) =\n$$\n{{field-2}}."] [:db/cas 17592186069232 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{({x}^{2}-1)^{2}-1}{{x}^{2}}=$" "$\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{({x}^{2}-1)^{2}-1}{{x}^{2}}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186069247 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)}=$" "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186069248 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}=$" "$\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186069311 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=2$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=-1,$ then what is ${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(f(x)+f(2))?$" "If $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=2$ and $\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=-1,$ then what is $\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(f(x)+f(2))?$"] [:db/cas 17592186069312 :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “The function is left-continuous at $x=0$” is false. The function is not left-continuous at $x=0,$ since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}g(x)=0\\ne -2=g(0)\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function is right-continuous at $x=0$” is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=0,$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}g(x)=-2=g(0)\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function is continuous at $x=0$” is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=0,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not left-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement “The function is right-continuous at $x=0$” is true." "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “The function is left-continuous at $x=0$” is false. The function is not left-continuous at $x=0,$ since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}g(x)=0\\ne -2=g(0)\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function is right-continuous at $x=0$” is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=0,$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}g(x)=-2=g(0)\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function is continuous at $x=0$” is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=0,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not left-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement “The function is right-continuous at $x=0$” is true."] [:db/cas 17592186069313 :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “The function is left-continuous at $x=-1$” is false. The function is not left-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=1\\ne 2=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function is right-continuous at $x=-1$” is false. The function is not right-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the right-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=3\\ne 2=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function is continuous at $x=-1$” is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=-1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is neither left-continuous nor right-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, none of the statements are true." "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “The function is left-continuous at $x=-1$” is false. The function is not left-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=1\\ne 2=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function is right-continuous at $x=-1$” is false. The function is not right-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the right-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=3\\ne 2=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function is continuous at $x=-1$” is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=-1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is neither left-continuous nor right-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, none of the statements are true."] [:db/cas 17592186069366 :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement “${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)$ is finite” is false. We get ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty .$\n\n- Statement “${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)$ is finite” is true. We get ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)=3.$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$  has a discontinuity at $x=2$” is true. The function contains a break exactly at $x=2$ and it's impossible to draw its graph without taking our pencil off of the paper." "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement “$\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)$ is finite” is false. We get $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty .$\n\n- Statement “$\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)$ is finite” is true. We get $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)=3.$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$  has a discontinuity at $x=2$” is true. The function contains a break exactly at $x=2$ and it's impossible to draw its graph without taking our pencil off of the paper."] [:db/cas 17592186078923 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+4}-2}{x}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+4}-2}{x}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186078924 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{x-1}{\\sqrt{x+3}-2}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{x-1}{\\sqrt{x+3}-2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186078926 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 4}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-2}{x-4}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to 4}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-2}{x-4}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186078933 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{x}{7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=$" "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{x}{7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078963 :question/problem "![](images/d9/d9e89a9b811bdc6b766b01eecf817df48968e3ea604b89bc55bc9660331194e0.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above? \n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=4$\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=1$" "![](images/d9/d9e89a9b811bdc6b766b01eecf817df48968e3ea604b89bc55bc9660331194e0.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above? \n\nI. $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=4$\n\nII. $\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$\n\nIII. $\\lim_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=1$"] [:db/cas 17592186078963 :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We see from the graph that \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{+}}f(x)=3\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=3.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Both limits ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)$ do not exist because the function $f(x)$ is not given in the interval $(0,2].$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\\,.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. We see from the graph that \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{+}}f(x)=1\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements II and III are true." "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We see from the graph that \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{+}}f(x)=3\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=3.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Both limits $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)$ and $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)$ do not exist because the function $f(x)$ is not given in the interval $(0,2].$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\\,.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. We see from the graph that \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{3}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,{3}^{+}}f(x)=1\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lim_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements II and III are true."] [:db/cas 17592186078983 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186078983 :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${x}^{2}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}=0.\n$$" "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${x}^{2}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}=0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078985 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186078985 :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{x}≫x$ for large values of $x,$ the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}=\\infty .\n$$" "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{x}≫x$ for large values of $x,$ the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}=\\infty .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078986 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{9}}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{9}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186078986 :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{x}≫{x}^{9}$ for large values of $x,$ the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{9}}=\\infty .\n$$" "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{x}≫{x}^{9}$ for large values of $x,$ the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{9}}=\\infty .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186097262 :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{(2+\\frac{5k}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}].\n$$" "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{(2+\\frac{5k}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186097262 :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=2.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{5}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{n}=\\frac{b-2}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=7.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=2+\\frac{5k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})={x}_{k}^{2}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{7}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{(2+\\frac{5k}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)={x}^{2}$ and $b=7.$" "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=2.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{5}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{n}=\\frac{b-2}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=7.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=2+\\frac{5k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})={x}_{k}^{2}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{7}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{(2+\\frac{5k}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)={x}^{2}$ and $b=7.$"] [:db/cas 17592186101224 :question/problem "The functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are differentiable on an open interval containing $x=7.$ The values of $f,$ $g,$ and their respective derivatives are shown in the following table:\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $7$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to 7}\\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{2}g(x)}.$" "The functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are differentiable on an open interval containing $x=7.$ The values of $f,$ $g,$ and their respective derivatives are shown in the following table:\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $7$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFind $\\lim_{x\\to 7}\\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{2}g(x)}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186101225 :question/problem "The functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are twice-differentiable on an open interval containing $x=10.$ The values of $f,$ $g,$ and their respective derivatives are given below:\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | ${f}^{″}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ | ${g}^{″}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $10$ | $-5$ | $0$ | $3$ | $-20$ | $-2$ | $-6$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to 10}\\frac{f(x)+5}{2x+g(x)}.$" "The functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are twice-differentiable on an open interval containing $x=10.$ The values of $f,$ $g,$ and their respective derivatives are given below:\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | ${f}^{″}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ | ${g}^{″}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $10$ | $-5$ | $0$ | $3$ | $-20$ | $-2$ | $-6$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFind $\\lim_{x\\to 10}\\frac{f(x)+5}{2x+g(x)}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186101455 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡n}{{n}^{2}}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡n}{{n}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges,  and its limit is $0.$" "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡n}{{n}^{2}}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡n}{{n}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges,  and its limit is $0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186101456 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{n}}{n}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{n}}{n}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence diverges." "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{n}}{n}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{n}}{n}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence diverges."] [:db/cas 17592186106285 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{4}\\right).$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{4}\\right).$"] [:db/cas 17592186106288 :question/problem "![](images/67/67dc6176a774b0dcb803a8e0935e2cc7c4241f635b18b7466fc530da8320cfe0.png)\n\nThe figure shows the graph of $y=f(x).$ Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{(xf(x))}^{3}.$" "![](images/67/67dc6176a774b0dcb803a8e0935e2cc7c4241f635b18b7466fc530da8320cfe0.png)\n\nThe figure shows the graph of $y=f(x).$ Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{(xf(x))}^{3}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186115754 :question/problem "Find the limit  $\\,{lim}_{t\\to 2}f(t)$, if $$\n\\displaystyle f(t)={t}^{2}\\,i+\\frac{t-4}{2}\\,j+\\frac{1}{t}\\,k.\n$$" "Find the limit  $\\,\\lim_{t\\to 2}f(t)$, if $$\n\\displaystyle f(t)={t}^{2}\\,i+\\frac{t-4}{2}\\,j+\\frac{1}{t}\\,k.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115802 :question/problem "Consider a vector-valued function $f(u)$ that is continuous over all real numbers and the real-valued function $u(t) = \\sqrt{9 - t^{2}}$. Then,\n$\\lim_{t \\to  0}f(u(t)) =$" "Consider a vector-valued function $f(u)$ that is continuous over all real numbers and the real-valued function $u(t) = \\sqrt{9 - t^{2}}$. Then,\n$$\n\\lim_{t \\to  0}f(u(t)) =\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115804 :question/problem "Find $g(2)$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are two continuous vector-valued functions for which $f(2)=⟨1,1,2⟩$ and \n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 2}[f(t)+2g(t)]=⟨-1,7,0⟩\\,.\n$$" "Find $g(2)$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are two continuous vector-valued functions for which $f(2)=⟨1,1,2⟩$ and \n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to 2}[f(t)+2g(t)]=⟨-1,7,0⟩\\,.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186124806 :question/worked-solution "The partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ is given by the limit \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{f(x+h,y)-f(x,y)}{h}\\right).\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)=3{x}^{2}y$,  we have that \n\n$$\nf(x+h,y)=3(x+h)^{2}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{3(x+h)^{2}y-3{x}^{2}y}{h}\\right).\n$$" "The partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ is given by the limit \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{f(x+h,y)-f(x,y)}{h}\\right).\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)=3{x}^{2}y$,  we have that \n\n$$\nf(x+h,y)=3(x+h)^{2}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{3(x+h)^{2}y-3{x}^{2}y}{h}\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186124937 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}.$" "Find $\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186124938 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{xy-x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{xy-x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186124953 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to (-2,3)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy}=$" "$\\lim_{x\\to (-2,3)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186125089 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{4}-6}{{x}^{2}-x+2}.$" "Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{4}-6}{{x}^{2}-x+2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186125160 :question/problem "Compute ${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}}.$" "Compute $\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186125160 :question/worked-solution "We can sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}}$ as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$\n\n2\\. Translate it to the right by $1$ unit. This gives $y=\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/e4/e4a6bad4e26a2f078930ca95d56999b315cfe55328367bbb1508333891a10618.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to ({1}^{-})}\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}}=\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to ({1}^{+})}\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}}=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}}=\\infty .\n$$" "We can sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}}$ as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$\n\n2\\. Translate it to the right by $1$ unit. This gives $y=\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/e4/e4a6bad4e26a2f078930ca95d56999b315cfe55328367bbb1508333891a10618.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to ({1}^{-})}\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}}=\\infty ,\\,\\lim_{x\\to ({1}^{+})}\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}}=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}}=\\infty .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186125201 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to \\pi /3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2x).$" "Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2x).$"] [:db/cas 17592186125220 :question/problem "Find  ${lim}_{x\\to (-1,2,2)}\\frac{{z}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{z}^{3}-{y}^{2}z}.$" "Find  $\\lim_{x\\to (-1,2,2)}\\frac{{z}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{z}^{3}-{y}^{2}z}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186125221 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to (0,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-x}{\\sqrt{1+xy}-1}=$" "$\\lim_{x\\to (0,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-x}{\\sqrt{1+xy}-1}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186125222 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2{y}^{2}x+y{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}}=$" "$\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2{y}^{2}x+y{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186125223 :question/problem "Find  ${lim}_{x\\to (2,2,-1)}\\frac{xyz-z{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}.$" "Find  $\\lim_{x\\to (2,2,-1)}\\frac{xyz-z{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186125224 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to (4,4)}\\frac{x-y}{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}}=$" "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (4,4)}\\frac{x-y}{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129705 :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=-1.$ Note that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}f(x)=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=-1.$\n\nThere are also discontinuities at $x=-2$ and $x=0,$ but they are not discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes." "The function has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=-1.$ Note that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}f(x)=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=-1.$\n\nThere are also discontinuities at $x=-2$ and $x=0,$ but they are not discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes."] [:db/cas 17592186129796 :question/worked-solution "The function $q(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $q(3)=0\\,$  exists  $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}q(x)=1$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}q(x)\\ne q(3)$  $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=3$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\n![](images/d7/d7fc2426919c72ff934d19e98eb7465b8a42852407809d242213da9c26a4ed4a.png)\n\n   \n\nNotice that the functions $h(x)$ and $p(x)$ are continuous at $x=3,$ while $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ both have a discontinuity at $x=3$ but they are *not* removable." "The function $q(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $q(3)=0\\,$  exists  $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 3}q(x)=1$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 3}q(x)\\ne q(3)$  $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=3$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\n![](images/d7/d7fc2426919c72ff934d19e98eb7465b8a42852407809d242213da9c26a4ed4a.png)\n\n   \n\nNotice that the functions $h(x)$ and $p(x)$ are continuous at $x=3,$ while $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ both have a discontinuity at $x=3$ but they are *not* removable."] [:db/cas 17592186129799 :question/worked-solution "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(-3)=1$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -3}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}f(x)=-1,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-3$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nIn the same way, the function has a jump discontinuity at $x=1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(1)=0$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=-1,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=0.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=1$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there is also a discontinuity at $x=0,$ but this is not a jump discontinuity." "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(-3)=1$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -3}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}f(x)=-1,\\,\\,\\lim_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-3$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nIn the same way, the function has a jump discontinuity at $x=1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(1)=0$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=-1,\\,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=0.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=1$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there is also a discontinuity at $x=0,$ but this is not a jump discontinuity."] [:db/cas 17592186129802 :question/worked-solution "The function $y=f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=-1.$ Checking our conditions, we see that\n\n- $f(-1)=2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}f(x)=2,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}f(x)=4.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-1$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\n![](images/89/8961fba5e89b2c6e85879f448806b0cc348cf9d271e18606ad318c1b8bf9e13c.png)\n\nNotice that the functions $h(x)$ and $p(x)$ are continuous at $x=-1,$ while $g(x)$ and $q(x)$ both have a discontinuity at $x=-1,$ but they are *not* jump discontinuities." "The function $y=f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=-1.$ Checking our conditions, we see that\n\n- $f(-1)=2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -1}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}f(x)=2,\\,\\,\\lim_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}f(x)=4.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-1$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\n![](images/89/8961fba5e89b2c6e85879f448806b0cc348cf9d271e18606ad318c1b8bf9e13c.png)\n\nNotice that the functions $h(x)$ and $p(x)$ are continuous at $x=-1,$ while $g(x)$ and $q(x)$ both have a discontinuity at $x=-1,$ but they are *not* jump discontinuities."] [:db/cas 17592186152391 :question/problem "${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{nx/2}=$" "$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{nx/2}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186157752 :question/problem "Consider the following statement. \n\nIf $f(x)=3{e}^{2x}+2x,$ then $f=O({e}^{2x})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nA proof of the statement is given below\n\n $\\text{L1}:\\,f=O(3{e}^{2x}+2x)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{2x}{3{e}^{2x}}\\right)=0$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,2x=O(3{e}^{2x})$ \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,f=O(3{e}^{2x})$  \n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,f=O({e}^{2x})$ \n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L1}$ follows from {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from lines \n{{field-2}} and \n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from line {{field-4}}  and \n{{field-5}}." "Consider the following statement. \n\nIf $f(x)=3{e}^{2x}+2x,$ then $f=O({e}^{2x})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nA proof of the statement is given below\n\n $\\text{L1}:\\,f=O(3{e}^{2x}+2x)$\n\n$$\n\\text{L2}:\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{2x}{3{e}^{2x}}\\right)=0\n$$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,2x=O(3{e}^{2x})$ \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,f=O(3{e}^{2x})$  \n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,f=O({e}^{2x})$ \n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L1}$ follows from {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from lines \n{{field-2}} and \n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from line {{field-4}}  and \n{{field-5}}."] [:db/cas 17592186176305 :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation for the function $y=y(t):$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=1\\,000y-10{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=40\n$$\n\nWhat is ${lim}_{t\\to \\infty }y(t)?$" "Consider the following differential equation for the function $y=y(t):$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=1\\,000y-10{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=40\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }y(t)?$"] [:db/cas 17592186176305 :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nA diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's factor the right-hand side of the given equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=10y(100-y),\\,y(0)=40.\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=100.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $100.$\n\nFor a given logistic growth model, $y\\to a$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to \\infty }y(t)=100.\n$$" "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nA diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's factor the right-hand side of the given equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=10y(100-y),\\,y(0)=40.\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=100.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $100.$\n\nFor a given logistic growth model, $y\\to a$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }y(t)=100.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186184516 :question/problem "What is the value of ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-1)?$" "What is the value of $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-1)?$"] [:db/cas 17592186184516 :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-1):$\n\n![](images/9e/9e2e705cf17a5dca362c23a23e4d10067ff4390a3f9464dddb6745d78986c02a.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-1)$ is $x\\in [0,1].$ This means that $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-1)$ is undefined outside this interval.\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-1)$ is undefined." "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-1):$\n\n![](images/9e/9e2e705cf17a5dca362c23a23e4d10067ff4390a3f9464dddb6745d78986c02a.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-1)$ is $x\\in [0,1].$ This means that $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-1)$ is undefined outside this interval.\n\nTherefore, $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-1)$ is undefined."] [:db/cas 17592186188566 :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0$\n\nIII. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\pi /2$\n\n*Hint: Use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*" "Consider the polar curve $r=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0$\n\nII. $\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0$\n\nIII. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\pi /2$\n\n*Hint: Use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*"] [:db/cas 17592186198759 :question/problem "The notation\n$\\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\frac{2}{3n + 1} = 0$\nmeans the following:\n\nFor any real\n{{field-1}} {{field-2}}\nnatural number $N$ such that\n{{field-3}}\nnatural number\n{{field-4}} ,\nwe have\n$\\mid \\frac{2}{3n + 1} \\mid$ {{field-5}}." "The notation\n$$\n\\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\frac{2}{3n + 1} = 0\n$$\nmeans the following:\n\nFor any real\n{{field-1}} {{field-2}}\nnatural number $N$ such that\n{{field-3}}\nnatural number\n{{field-4}} ,\nwe have\n$\\mid \\frac{2}{3n + 1} \\mid$ {{field-5}}."] [:db/cas 17592186198766 :question/problem "The notation\n$\\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{n}} = 0$\nmeans the following:\n\n{{field-1}}\nreal $ε > 0$\nthere exists a\n{{field-2}}\nnumber $N$ such that for every\n{{field-3}}\nnumber $n$, if\n{{field-4}} ,\nthen $\\mid \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{n}} \\mid$ {{field-5}} {{field-6}}." "The notation\n$$\n\\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{n}} = 0\n$$\nmeans the following:\n\n{{field-1}}\nreal $ε > 0$\nthere exists a\n{{field-2}}\nnumber $N$ such that for every\n{{field-3}}\nnumber $n$, if\n{{field-4}} ,\nthen $\\mid \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{n}} \\mid$ {{field-5}} {{field-6}}."] [:db/cas 17592186198819 :question/problem "The notation\n$\\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(3n + 1) = ∞$\nmeans the following:\n\nFor any real\n{{field-1}} {{field-2}}\nnatural number $N$ such that\n{{field-3}}\nnatural number\n{{field-4}} ,\nwe have\n$3n + 1$ {{field-5}}." "The notation\n$$\n\\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(3n + 1) = ∞\n$$\nmeans the following:\n\nFor any real\n{{field-1}} {{field-2}}\nnatural number $N$ such that\n{{field-3}}\nnatural number\n{{field-4}} ,\nwe have\n$3n + 1$ {{field-5}}."] [:db/cas 17592186198820 :question/problem "The notation\n$\\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\left(\\frac{1 - 2n^{2}}{n}\\right) = - ∞$\nmeans the following:\n\n{{field-1}}\nreal $M < 0$\nthere exists a\n{{field-2}}\nnumber $N$ such that for every\n{{field-3}}\nnumber $n$, if\n{{field-4}} ,\nthen $<$ {{field-5}} {{field-6}}" "The notation\n$$\n\\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\left(\\frac{1 - 2n^{2}}{n}\\right) = - ∞\n$$\nmeans the following:\n\n{{field-1}}\nreal $M < 0$\nthere exists a\n{{field-2}}\nnumber $N$ such that for every\n{{field-3}}\nnumber $n$, if\n{{field-4}} ,\nthen $<$ {{field-5}} {{field-6}}"] [:db/cas 17592186222025 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-3,3).$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/18/186106373f9848cfefefb23197e7508497ff3d570f326da2ca342d078682128d.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{true}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}-3x=-3(-3)=9\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}{x}^{2}=(3)^{2}=9.\n$$\n\nSo, ${lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{true}$ since statements I, II, and III are all true.\n\n**Note:** The $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/89/89c3a597ce1114fee9e775f291cf89e8ca04f61eb39cf2b4c896dd4be8022f09.png)" "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-3,3).$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/18/186106373f9848cfefefb23197e7508497ff3d570f326da2ca342d078682128d.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{true}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}-3x=-3(-3)=9\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}{x}^{2}=(3)^{2}=9.\n$$\n\nSo, $\\lim_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{true}$ since statements I, II, and III are all true.\n\n**Note:** The $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/89/89c3a597ce1114fee9e775f291cf89e8ca04f61eb39cf2b4c896dd4be8022f09.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186222026 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/2e/2eb2120db83d33d37f1d076f7e3664a21cd1bb97ccd04aeb792cd8e510edf846.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-1,1).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{false}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}x=-1\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}1=1.\n$$\n\nSo, ${lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}F(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statements I and III are false.\n\n**Note:** The $2$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/c9/c91cf3eea948fdce9a992c77d95bc3c8bc5165ade9e6f17ae5b7e699b0a9be9d.png)" "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/2e/2eb2120db83d33d37f1d076f7e3664a21cd1bb97ccd04aeb792cd8e510edf846.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-1,1).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{false}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}x=-1\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}1=1.\n$$\n\nSo, $\\lim_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}F(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statements I and III are false.\n\n**Note:** The $2$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/c9/c91cf3eea948fdce9a992c77d95bc3c8bc5165ade9e6f17ae5b7e699b0a9be9d.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186229731 :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous on $x\\in [a,b]$ if\n\n- it is continuous on $[a,b]$ except at finitely many points, and\n\n- at each point of discontinuity, the one-sided limits exist and are finite.\n\nIn other words, there can only be finitely many discontinuities, and each of them must be either a point or a jump discontinuity.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-1,1]$ since the one-sided limits at $x=0$ are infinite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=+\\infty \n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-1,1]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [-1,1]$ except at the point $x=0,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}g(x)=0,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}g(x)=1\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-1,1]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$g$ and $h$ only.\"" "A function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous on $x\\in [a,b]$ if\n\n- it is continuous on $[a,b]$ except at finitely many points, and\n\n- at each point of discontinuity, the one-sided limits exist and are finite.\n\nIn other words, there can only be finitely many discontinuities, and each of them must be either a point or a jump discontinuity.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-1,1]$ since the one-sided limits at $x=0$ are infinite:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=+\\infty \n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-1,1]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [-1,1]$ except at the point $x=0,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}g(x)=0,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}g(x)=1\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-1,1]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$g$ and $h$ only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186229736 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $I⊆R.$ A function $f:I\\to R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]⊂I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=1,2,3,…,$ and any closed interval $[a,b]⊂[0,\\infty )$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](images/08/084c469b225d2a4cc4bc148fa3a42fb1501141d1adcd91d8d37999409a8d313f.png)\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ except at the point $x=2,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}g(x)=0,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}g(x)=1\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ Notice that the one-sided limits at $x=\\pi $ are infinite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}h(x)=+\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}h(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f$ and $g$ only.\"" "Suppose $I⊆R.$ A function $f:I\\to R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]⊂I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=1,2,3,…,$ and any closed interval $[a,b]⊂[0,\\infty )$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](images/08/084c469b225d2a4cc4bc148fa3a42fb1501141d1adcd91d8d37999409a8d313f.png)\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ except at the point $x=2,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}g(x)=0,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}g(x)=1\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ Notice that the one-sided limits at $x=\\pi $ are infinite:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}h(x)=+\\infty ,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}h(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f$ and $g$ only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186233020 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{(2{x}^{4}+4)^{2}}{(x-2)^{3}}.$" "Find $\\lim_{x\\to -1}\\frac{(2{x}^{4}+4)^{2}}{(x-2)^{3}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186233021 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{y\\to -2}{({y}^{3}+2)}^{3}(2y+3).$" "Find $\\lim_{y\\to -2}{({y}^{3}+2)}^{3}(2y+3).$"] [:db/cas 17592186233022 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to 4}(\\sqrt[3]{({x}^{2}-3x-5)^{5}}-\\sqrt{(2x-7)^{3}}).$" "Find $\\lim_{x\\to 4}(\\sqrt[3]{({x}^{2}-3x-5)^{5}}-\\sqrt{(2x-7)^{3}}).$"] [:db/cas 17592186233023 :question/problem "![](images/fd/fda731fa3c9a3401e9b9e2a7d4c103829d3fe3b14ac9d6f2872b76a319f6ae91.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x))^{3}(2{x}^{4}-3)^{2}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above." "![](images/fd/fda731fa3c9a3401e9b9e2a7d4c103829d3fe3b14ac9d6f2872b76a319f6ae91.png)\n\nFind $\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x))^{3}(2{x}^{4}-3)^{2}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above."] [:db/cas 17592186233024 :question/problem "![](images/e8/e8aac08a40b3a318cb682afca1783a8d9563bf67f0b51bdff2ead8a9bf28117c.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{3}+4)^{2}(4-2f(x))^{5}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above." "![](images/e8/e8aac08a40b3a318cb682afca1783a8d9563bf67f0b51bdff2ead8a9bf28117c.png)\n\nFind $\\lim_{x\\to -2}({x}^{3}+4)^{2}(4-2f(x))^{5}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above."] [:db/cas 17592186233025 :question/problem "![](images/dd/ddf4b2d129022e27627afa1b0946f18ef7e98ab2ffe20dea6d609bedce65f84d.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(1-3x)^{3}(3f(x)-4x)^{2}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above." "![](images/dd/ddf4b2d129022e27627afa1b0946f18ef7e98ab2ffe20dea6d609bedce65f84d.png)\n\nFind $\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}(1-3x)^{3}(3f(x)-4x)^{2}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above."] [:db/cas 17592186233026 :question/problem "![](images/a4/a4f8b046dd85d258dd897e595b5dba1116e5df763e2e74a196c6b79e2a8a42a5.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(3-x)^{3}(f(x)-4{x}^{2})^{5}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above." "![](images/a4/a4f8b046dd85d258dd897e595b5dba1116e5df763e2e74a196c6b79e2a8a42a5.png)\n\nFind $\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}(3-x)^{3}(f(x)-4{x}^{2})^{5}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above."] [:db/cas 17592186233029 :question/problem "![](images/b9/b9a95898f98ad5289313a0f0fdea24fa8828d508564c8ce60cec986919f16f50.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{3f(x)+1}{\\sqrt{f(x)+3}}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above." "![](images/b9/b9a95898f98ad5289313a0f0fdea24fa8828d508564c8ce60cec986919f16f50.png)\n\nFind $\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{3f(x)+1}{\\sqrt{f(x)+3}}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above."] [:db/cas 17592186233047 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt{4x-3}-3}{x-3}=$" "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt{4x-3}-3}{x-3}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233048 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{5x+6}-4}{x-2}=$" "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{5x+6}-4}{x-2}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233049 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3x+4}-2}=$" "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3x+4}-2}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233081 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=\\sqrt{3}$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {\\sqrt{3}}^{\\,-}}f(x)=9,$ then what is $f(\\sqrt{3})?$" "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\lim_{x\\to {\\sqrt{3}}^{\\,-}}f(x)=9,$ then what is $f(\\sqrt{3})?$"] [:db/cas 17592186233081 :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=\\sqrt{3}.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to \\sqrt{3}}f(x)$ exists, and\n\n$$\nf(\\sqrt{3})={lim}_{x\\to \\sqrt{3}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {\\sqrt{3}}^{\\,-}}f(x)=9.\n$$" "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=\\sqrt{3}.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that $\\lim_{x\\to \\sqrt{3}}f(x)$ exists, and\n\n$$\nf(\\sqrt{3})=\\lim_{x\\to \\sqrt{3}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {\\sqrt{3}}^{\\,-}}f(x)=9.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233082 :question/worked-solution "To determine whether $f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=c,$ we need to check whether the left-sided limit is equal to the function value.\n\nLet's check this for $x=0$, at $x=2$ and at  $x=3.$\n\n- At $x=0$ the function is not left-continuous because \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=3\\ne 2=f(0).\n$$\n\n- At $x=2$ the function is not left-continuous because \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=0\\ne -2=f(2).\n$$\n\n- At $x=3$ the function is left-continuous because\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{-}}f(x)=1=f(3).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=3$ only." "To determine whether $f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=c,$ we need to check whether the left-sided limit is equal to the function value.\n\nLet's check this for $x=0$, at $x=2$ and at  $x=3.$\n\n- At $x=0$ the function is not left-continuous because \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=3\\ne 2=f(0).\n$$\n\n- At $x=2$ the function is not left-continuous because \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=0\\ne -2=f(2).\n$$\n\n- At $x=3$ the function is left-continuous because\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{3}^{-}}f(x)=1=f(3).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=3$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186233083 :question/worked-solution "To determine whether $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=c,$ we need to check whether the right-sided limit is equal to the function value.\n\nThe function has three discontinuities: $x=-2$, $x=0$ and $x=2.$\n\n- At $x=-2$  the function is neither left- nor right-continuous because it's undefined at this point.\n\n- At $x=0$ the function is not right-continuous because\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=0\\ne -1=f(0).\n$$\n\n- At $x=2$ the function is right-continuous because\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)=-2=f(2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=2$ only." "To determine whether $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=c,$ we need to check whether the right-sided limit is equal to the function value.\n\nThe function has three discontinuities: $x=-2$, $x=0$ and $x=2.$\n\n- At $x=-2$  the function is neither left- nor right-continuous because it's undefined at this point.\n\n- At $x=0$ the function is not right-continuous because\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=0\\ne -1=f(0).\n$$\n\n- At $x=2$ the function is right-continuous because\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)=-2=f(2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=2$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186233090 :question/problem "![](images/f3/f367a4318a1b9d3c2e582f515a2c57058067ed4546f588dc30282f7dd34aac89.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above? \n\nI. - ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=-1$\nII. - ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=3$\nIII. - ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$" "![](images/f3/f367a4318a1b9d3c2e582f515a2c57058067ed4546f588dc30282f7dd34aac89.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above? \n\nI. - $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=-1$\nII. - $\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=3$\nIII. - $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$"] [:db/cas 17592186233090 :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As $x$ approaches $-2,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=1.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. As $x$ approaches $0,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=3.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=3.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ approaches $-1,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=2,$ and consequently \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\n \nSimilarly, as $x$ approaches $2,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=1,$ and consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n As a result,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x).\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true." "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As $x$ approaches $-2,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=1.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. As $x$ approaches $0,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=3.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=3.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ approaches $-1,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=2,$ and consequently \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\n \nSimilarly, as $x$ approaches $2,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=1,$ and consequently, \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n As a result,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x).\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true."] [:db/cas 17592186233091 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}(4x)}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}(4x)}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186233091 :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since $(x+1)^{2}≫\\operatorname{ln}(4x)$ for large values of $x,$ the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}(4x)}=\\infty .\n$$" "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since $(x+1)^{2}≫\\operatorname{ln}(4x)$ for large values of $x,$ the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}(4x)}=\\infty .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233092 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}(x+2)}-20)=$" "$\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}(x+2)}-20)=$"] [:db/cas 17592186233092 :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${x}^{3}≫\\operatorname{ln}(x+2)$ for large values of $x,$ the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}(x+2)}=\\infty \n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}(x+2)}-20)=\\infty \n$$" "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${x}^{3}≫\\operatorname{ln}(x+2)$ for large values of $x,$ the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}(x+2)}=\\infty \n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}(x+2)}-20)=\\infty \n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233093 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{\\sqrt{x-1}-2}{x-5}=$" "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 5}\\frac{\\sqrt{x-1}-2}{x-5}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233097 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3x-2}{4-{x}^{2}},$ which of the following statements is correct?\n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty $\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)=\\infty $\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty $" "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3x-2}{4-{x}^{2}},$ which of the following statements is correct?\n\nI. $\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty $\n\nII. $\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)=\\infty $\n\nIII. $\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty $"] [:db/cas 17592186233211 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-6x+9}{8\\sqrt{x+1}-2x-10}=$" "$\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-6x+9}{8\\sqrt{x+1}-2x-10}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186233221 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{ln}^{2}x}{x}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{ln}^{2}x}{x}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186233223 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}2x}{\\operatorname{cot}x}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}2x}{\\operatorname{cot}x}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186233378 :question/problem "${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{n})}^{n/x}=$" "$\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{n})}^{n/x}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186233387 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}g(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 2}g(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}g(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2}g(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186233387 :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we see that as we approach $x=2$ from the left, the $y$-value approaches $0.$ So the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}g(x)=0.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, when we approach $x=2$ from the right, the $y$-value again approaches $0.$ So the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$\n\nSince both the left and right-hand limits exist and are equal to $0,$ we conclude that\n\n \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}g(x)=0.\n$$" "From the graph, we see that as we approach $x=2$ from the left, the $y$-value approaches $0.$ So the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}g(x)=0.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, when we approach $x=2$ from the right, the $y$-value again approaches $0.$ So the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$\n\nSince both the left and right-hand limits exist and are equal to $0,$ we conclude that\n\n \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}g(x)=0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233388 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x).$ If a limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x)=$\n{{field-2}}" "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)$ and $\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x).$ If a limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x)=\n$$\n{{field-2}}"] [:db/cas 17592186233388 :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $-1$ from the left, the function $g(x)$ approaches $2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)=2.\n$$\n\nLikewise, as $x$ approaches $-1$ from the right, the function $g(x)$ approaches $0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)=2,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$" "As $x$ approaches $-1$ from the left, the function $g(x)$ approaches $2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)=2.\n$$\n\nLikewise, as $x$ approaches $-1$ from the right, the function $g(x)$ approaches $0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)=2,\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233389 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }g(x).$ If a limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }g(x)=$\n{{field-2}}" "Find $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)$ and $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }g(x).$ If a limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }g(x)=\n$$\n{{field-2}}"] [:db/cas 17592186233389 :question/worked-solution "On the left side of the graph, we see that as the values of $x$ decrease and approach $-\\infty ,$ the graph of the function $g(x)$ moves closer and closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=4.$\n\nConsequently, $g(x)$ approaches the value $4$ as $x$ approaches $-\\infty ,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)=4.\n$$\n\nOn the right side of the graph, as the values of $x$ increase and approach $\\infty ,$ the graph of the function grows without bound to $\\infty $ as well. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }g(x)=\\infty .\n$$" "On the left side of the graph, we see that as the values of $x$ decrease and approach $-\\infty ,$ the graph of the function $g(x)$ moves closer and closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=4.$\n\nConsequently, $g(x)$ approaches the value $4$ as $x$ approaches $-\\infty ,$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)=4.\n$$\n\nOn the right side of the graph, as the values of $x$ increase and approach $\\infty ,$ the graph of the function grows without bound to $\\infty $ as well. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }g(x)=\\infty .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233390 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(2f(x)-3g(x)+{x}^{2}).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(2f(x)-3g(x)+{x}^{2})=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}(2f(x)-3g(x)+{x}^{2}).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}(2f(x)-3g(x)+{x}^{2})=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186233391 :question/problem "Given that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}Cf(x)=6,$ where $C$ is a real constant, what is the value of $C?$\n\n$C=$\n{{field-1}}" "Given that $\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}Cf(x)=6,$ where $C$ is a real constant, what is the value of $C?$\n\n$C=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186233393 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186233393 :question/worked-solution "In part 1, we showed that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)=2,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$\n\nSince ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x),$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$" "In part 1, we showed that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)=2,\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$\n\nSince $\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x),$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233414 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi }f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi }f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186233415 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186233415 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=f(x)=6\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right).$\n\n![](images/72/72a0459ee5db59562a19961d0304785fb8d46db8be24ad7839d4b8690b0dc835.png)\n\nAs $x$ decreases to $-\\infty $, the graph oscillates between the values $y=-6$ and $y=6$ indefinitely. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }f(x)={li