}^{4}(x)\\cdot {q}^{3}(x))=\n$$\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186233762 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{log}_{6}(4x-2)}{x+3}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{log}_{6}(4x-2)}{x+3}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186233763 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}{log}_{3}(-4x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}{log}_{3}(-4x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186233763 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{3}(-4x).$\n\n![](images/bb/bb8d326b427d481c69ad021a831efadf77cb90540dd79d0c3edd29f68cae41ec.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\ge 0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{log}_{3}(-4x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x<0.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}{log}_{3}(-4x)=-\\infty .\n$$" "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{3}(-4x).$\n\n![](images/bb/bb8d326b427d481c69ad021a831efadf77cb90540dd79d0c3edd29f68cae41ec.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\ge 0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{log}_{3}(-4x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x<0.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}{log}_{3}(-4x)=-\\infty .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233764 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\operatorname{csc}(2x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\operatorname{csc}(2x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186233766 :question/problem "Given that ${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\sqrt{5x-m}=\\sqrt{5},$ what is the value of constant $m?$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" "Given that $\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\sqrt{5x-m}=\\sqrt{5},$ what is the value of constant $m?$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186233779 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{x+1}{3{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+4}=$" "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -1}\\frac{x+1}{3{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+4}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233780 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{4\\sqrt{x+4}-x-8}{{x}^{2}}=$" "$\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{4\\sqrt{x+4}-x-8}{{x}^{2}}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186233800 :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{\\,1+4k/n}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{\\,1+4k/n}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}"] [:db/cas 17592186233800 :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=1.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{4}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{n}=\\frac{b-1}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=5.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=1+\\frac{4k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})={e}^{\\,{x}_{k}}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{5}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{\\,1+4k/n}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)={e}^{x}\\,\\text{and}\\,b=5.\n$$" "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=1.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{4}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{n}=\\frac{b-1}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=5.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=1+\\frac{4k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})={e}^{\\,{x}_{k}}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{5}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{\\,1+4k/n}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)={e}^{x}\\,\\text{and}\\,b=5.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233858 :question/problem "Given that $p(x)=\\frac{-6}{{x}^{5}}$ and $q(x)=\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}},$ evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Given that $p(x)=\\frac{-6}{{x}^{5}}$ and $q(x)=\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}},$ evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186233859 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6({x}^{5}-4)}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6({x}^{5}-4)}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186233859 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite our limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6({x}^{5}-4)}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}-4}\n$$\n\nFor any non-constant polynomial $P(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\pm \\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{P(x)}\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${x}^{5}-4$ is a non-constant polynomial, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}-4}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the original limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}-4}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}\\cdot 0=0\n$$" "First, we rewrite our limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6({x}^{5}-4)}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}-4}\n$$\n\nFor any non-constant polynomial $P(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\pm \\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{P(x)}\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${x}^{5}-4$ is a non-constant polynomial, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}-4}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the 