n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}(2x){e}^{-3(x+2)})=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186233888 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(2x)}{{e}^{3x}}-3)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(2x)}{{e}^{3x}}-3)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186233888 :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{3x}≫\\operatorname{ln}(2x)$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(2x)}{{e}^{3x}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(2x)}{{e}^{3x}}-3)=0-3=-3.\n$$" "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{3x}≫\\operatorname{ln}(2x)$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(2x)}{{e}^{3x}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(2x)}{{e}^{3x}}-3)=0-3=-3.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233890 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\frac{8}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{arctan}(5-x))=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\frac{8}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{arctan}(5-x))=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186233890 :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}(5-x):$ \n\n![](images/ce/ce2078de99b2c037b0ab6d5c5dcd4dab1e8aa7005417d87ca83176bf197fd5dc.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}(5-x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}(5-x)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\frac{8}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{arctan}(5-x))=\\frac{8}{\\pi }\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=4.\n$$" "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}(5-x):$ \n\n![](images/ce/ce2078de99b2c037b0ab6d5c5dcd4dab1e8aa7005417d87ca83176bf197fd5dc.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}(5-x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}(5-x)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\frac{8}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{arctan}(5-x))=\\frac{8}{\\pi }\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=4.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233892 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}sin\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}(x))=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}sin\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}(x))=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186233893 :question/problem "If $g(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=3$ and $g(3)=16,$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}(4g(x)-3x\\sqrt{g(3)})=$\n{{field-1}}." "If $g(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=3$ and $g(3)=16,$ then\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}(4g(x)-3x\\sqrt{g(3)})=\n$$\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186233894 :question/problem "Find the left- and right-sided limits at $x=\\pi $ of the piecewise function $f(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}(x-\\pi +\\sqrt{3})^{2} & \\text{ for }\\,x<\\pi  \\\\ 5+2\\operatorname{cos}(x) & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge \\pi .\\end{cases}\n$$\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}},$\\,$\n$\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}f(x)=$\n{{field-2}}" "Find the left- and right-sided limits at $x=\\pi $ of the piecewise function $f(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}(x-\\pi +\\sqrt{3})^{2} & \\text{ for }\\,x<\\pi  \\\\ 5+2\\operatorname{cos}(x) & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge \\pi .\\end{cases}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}},$\\,$\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-2}}"] [:db/cas 17592186233895 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 2}{log}_{3}(x+7)\\cdot {log}_{3}(5-x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2}{log}_{3}(x+7)\\cdot {log}_{3}(5-x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186233896 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{log}_{4}(-6x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{log}_{4}(-6x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186233896 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{4}(-6x).$\n\n![](images/8a/8a4a1bc79f9d096c3ebe2b4ff37e8682f74ab04583101dfe1d7f1d5f2e8df8c1.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ decreases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{log}_{4}(-6x)=\\infty \n$$" "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{4}(-6x).$\n\n![](images/8a/8a4a1bc79f9d096c3ebe2b4ff37e8682f74ab04583101dfe1d7f1d5f2e8df8c1.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ decreases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{log}_{4}(-6x)=\\infty \n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233898 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)$ and $\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}(x-2)^{2}+3x-5 & \\text{ for }\\,x<1 \\\\ sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}x)+2 & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 4.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,$\n$\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x)=$\n{{field-2}}" "Find $\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)$ and $\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}(x-2)^{2}+3x-5 & \\text{ for }\\,x<1 \\\\ sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}x)+2 & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 4.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,$\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-2}}"] [:db/cas 17592186233904 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{log}_{4}(9x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{log}_{4}(9x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186233904 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{4}(9x).$\n\n![](images/1e/1ead64d0b64a005122fa34c65677d5e7980c2a76245bbaca572325b8681ae958.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\le 0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}{log}_{4}(9x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x>0.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{log}_{4}(9x)=-\\infty .\n$$" "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{4}(9x).$\n\n![](images/1e/1ead64d0b64a005122fa34c65677d5e7980c2a76245bbaca572325b8681ae958.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\le 0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}{log}_{4}(9x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x>0.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{log}_{4}(9x)=-\\infty .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233923 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=5,$ then\n\n$\\frac{10-f(0)}{(f(0))^{2}}=$\n{{field-1}}." "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$ and $\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=5,$ then\n\n$\\frac{10-f(0)}{(f(0))^{2}}=$\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186233929 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=3$ and $f(3)=7,$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 3}{(4{x}^{2}-5f(x))}^{3}=$\n{{field-1}}." "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=3$ and $f(3)=7,$ then\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 3}{(4{x}^{2}-5f(x))}^{3}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186233930 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=4$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}f(x)=-3,$ then\n\n${[(f(4))^{2}-11]}^{4}=$\n{{field-1}}." "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=4$ and $\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}f(x)=-3,$ then\n\n${[(f(4))^{2}-11]}^{4}=$\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186233940 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\frac{5}{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+7))=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\frac{5}{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+7))=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186233940 :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+7):$\n\n![](images/37/371cbb841c67494626038fc93747a1d0a2b2245f422364911f1a859f4711868d.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+7)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+7)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\frac{5}{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+7))=\\frac{5}{2\\pi }\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=-\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$" "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+7):$\n\n![](images/37/371cbb841c67494626038fc93747a1d0a2b2245f422364911f1a859f4711868d.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+7)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+7)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\frac{5}{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+7))=\\frac{5}{2\\pi }\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=-\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233948 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(\\operatorname{sin}(x)+4\\operatorname{cot}(x))=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(\\operatorname{sin}(x)+4\\operatorname{cot}(x))=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186233953 :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+1):$ \n\n![](images/4d/4d81187377609f2cc79f2f972b1416a00f854d8c7a72731eb306aaa17d9f7339.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+1)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+1)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+1))=\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))=2.\n$$" "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+1):$ \n\n![](images/4d/4d81187377609f2cc79f2f972b1416a00f854d8c7a72731eb306aaa17d9f7339.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+1)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+1)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+1))=\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))=2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233954 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}tan\\,(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}(x))=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}tan\\,(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}(x))=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186233955 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }(8\\operatorname{arctan}(3x-1))=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }(8\\operatorname{arctan}(3x-1))=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186233955 :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}(3x-1):$\n\n![](images/56/56e73e41a60732857504125b12eb3d3265b2633b0a9d1502058096725630793b.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}(3x-1)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}(3x-1)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }(8\\operatorname{arctan}(3x-1))=(8\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})=4\\pi .\n$$" "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}(3x-1):$\n\n![](images/56/56e73e41a60732857504125b12eb3d3265b2633b0a9d1502058096725630793b.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}(3x-1)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}(3x-1)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }(8\\operatorname{arctan}(3x-1))=(8\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})=4\\pi .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233960 :question/problem "Express $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{10}{x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ as the limit of a left Riemann sum.\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{10}{x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[(\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{\\,1/2}\\cdot \\frac{9}{n}]$" "Express $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{10}{x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ as the limit of a left Riemann sum.\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{10}{x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[(\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{\\,1/2}\\cdot \\frac{9}{n}]$"] [:db/cas 17592186233961 :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3}7{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3}7{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[7(\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{\\,-2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]$" "What is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3}7{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3}7{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[7(\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{\\,-2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]$"] [:db/cas 17592186233992 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{y\\to 1}{(2{y}^{2}+1)}^{3}(4y-1)^{2}.$" "Find $\\lim_{y\\to 1}{(2{y}^{2}+1)}^{3}(4y-1)^{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186233993 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{z\\to (-4)}\\frac{{({z}^{2}-10)}^{2}}{(z+6)^{5}}.$" "Calculate $\\lim_{z\\to (-4)}\\frac{{({z}^{2}-10)}^{2}}{(z+6)^{5}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186233994 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to -1}\\sqrt[5]{(7x+11)^{3}}.$" "Find $\\lim_{x\\to -1}\\sqrt[5]{(7x+11)^{3}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186233995 :question/problem "![](images/95/95e092490e087b5245f05b352b4738beb1ce3d9d649ea289ded881d0728129e5.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to 1/2}\\sqrt{4(f(x))^{4}+10x}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above." "![](images/95/95e092490e087b5245f05b352b4738beb1ce3d9d649ea289ded881d0728129e5.png)\n\nFind $\\lim_{x\\to 1/2}\\sqrt{4(f(x))^{4}+10x}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above."] [:db/cas 17592186233997 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{x}{-2}\\right)}^{3}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{x}{-2}\\right)}^{3}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186233998 :question/problem "![](images/e6/e647ebf6e8332e9be5abd75e0f5ac5102c89c4a40590cef21a8ddfc65a17701d.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to -1}(3{x}^{2}-1)^{2}(2-f(x))^{4}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above." "![](images/e6/e647ebf6e8332e9be5abd75e0f5ac5102c89c4a40590cef21a8ddfc65a17701d.png)\n\nFind $\\lim_{x\\to -1}(3{x}^{2}-1)^{2}(2-f(x))^{4}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above."] [:db/cas 17592186233999 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{y\\to 128}{y}^{-1/7}.$" "Calculate $\\lim_{y\\to 128}{y}^{-1/7}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186234000 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-7{x}^{3}+\\sqrt{2}{x}^{2}+6x-3).$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(-7{x}^{3}+\\sqrt{2}{x}^{2}+6x-3).$"] [:db/cas 17592186234010 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{3x}-1}{x}=$" "$\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{3x}-1}{x}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186234057 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+3x+4}+6x}{4x-2}.$" "Find $\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+3x+4}+6x}{4x-2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186234058 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {5}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x-1}-3}{x-5}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to {5}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x-1}-3}{x-5}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186234063 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{x-2}{2x-\\sqrt{8x}}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{x-2}{2x-\\sqrt{8x}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186234064 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\frac{x-8}{4-\\sqrt{2x}}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\frac{x-8}{4-\\sqrt{2x}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186234065 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 25}\\frac{x-25}{\\sqrt{x}-5}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to 25}\\frac{x-25}{\\sqrt{x}-5}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186234066 :question/problem "Given that $p(x)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}}$ and $q(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},$ what is ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)}?$" "Given that $p(x)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}}$ and $q(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},$ what is $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186234068 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to \\pi /3}\\operatorname{csc}(3x).$" "Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /3}\\operatorname{csc}(3x).$"] [:db/cas 17592186234069 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\operatorname{csc}x.$" "Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\operatorname{csc}x.$"] [:db/cas 17592186234070 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\operatorname{cot}8x=$" "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\operatorname{cot}8x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186234076 :question/problem "${lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2+h}-{e}^{2}}{h}=$" "$\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2+h}-{e}^{2}}{h}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186234080 :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=0,$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=0=f(0)\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function is not right-continuous at $x=0,$ since the right-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=1\\ne 0=f(0)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=0,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not right-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true." "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=0,$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=0=f(0)\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function is not right-continuous at $x=0,$ since the right-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=1\\ne 0=f(0)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=0,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not right-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true."] [:db/cas 17592186234087 :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$” is true. Indeed, $f(2)=1.$\n\n- Statement “${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)$ exists” is true. Indeed, \n${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=3.$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$” is false. The graph of $f(x)$ breaks at $x=2$,  it's impossible to draw the graph without taking our pencil off of the paper." "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$” is true. Indeed, $f(2)=1.$\n\n- Statement “$\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)$ exists” is true. Indeed, \n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=3.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$” is false. The graph of $f(x)$ breaks at $x=2$,  it's impossible to draw the graph without taking our pencil off of the paper."] [:db/cas 17592186234088 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}x}{{e}^{x}}+7).$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}x}{{e}^{x}}+7).$"] [:db/cas 17592186234088 :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{x}≫\\operatorname{ln}x$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}x}{{e}^{x}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}x}{{e}^{x}}+7)=0+7=7.\n$$" "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{x}≫\\operatorname{ln}x$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}x}{{e}^{x}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}x}{{e}^{x}}+7)=0+7=7.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186234089 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}x}{3x}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}x}{3x}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186234089 :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since $3x≫\\operatorname{ln}x$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}x}{3x}=0.\n$$" "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since $3x≫\\operatorname{ln}x$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}x}{3x}=0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186234090 :question/worked-solution "To determine whether $f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=c,$ we need to check whether the left-sided limit is equal to the function value.\n\nLet's check this for $x=-2$, at $x=1$ and at  $x=2.$\n\n- At $x=-2$ the function is not left-continuous because it's undefined at this point.\n\n- At $x=1$ the function is left-continuous because\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=4=f(1).\n$$\n\n- At $x=2$ the function is not left-continuous because \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=0\\ne 2=f(2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=1$ only." "To determine whether $f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=c,$ we need to check whether the left-sided limit is equal to the function value.\n\nLet's check this for $x=-2$, at $x=1$ and at  $x=2.$\n\n- At $x=-2$ the function is not left-continuous because it's undefined at this point.\n\n- At $x=1$ the function is left-continuous because\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=4=f(1).\n$$\n\n- At $x=2$ the function is not left-continuous because \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=0\\ne 2=f(2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=1$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186234091 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }x{e}^{-2x}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }x{e}^{-2x}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186234091 :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can rewrite the given expression as\n\n$$\nx{e}^{-2x}=\\frac{x}{{e}^{2x}}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{2x}≫x$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }x{e}^{-2x}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{{e}^{2x}}=0.\n$$" "Notice that we can rewrite the given expression as\n\n$$\nx{e}^{-2x}=\\frac{x}{{e}^{2x}}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{2x}≫x$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }x{e}^{-2x}=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{{e}^{2x}}=0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186234102 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{3{x}^{2}+x}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{3{x}^{2}+x}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186234102 :question/worked-solution "The numerator here is a bounded, oscillating function with\n\n$$\n|\\operatorname{cos}x|\\le 1.\n$$\n\nHowever, the denominator $3{x}^{2}+x$ grows without bound as $x\\to \\infty .$ So the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{3{x}^{2}+x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see this from the graph of $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{3{x}^{2}+x},$ shown below:\n\n![](images/80/80dcb33426c9981efdb4eddb4bbd33e75aeb5267cd776acfbda99758e010128a.png)" "The numerator here is a bounded, oscillating function with\n\n$$\n|\\operatorname{cos}x|\\le 1.\n$$\n\nHowever, the denominator $3{x}^{2}+x$ grows without bound as $x\\to \\infty .$ So the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{3{x}^{2}+x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see this from the graph of $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{3{x}^{2}+x},$ shown below:\n\n![](images/80/80dcb33426c9981efdb4eddb4bbd33e75aeb5267cd776acfbda99758e010128a.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186234103 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x+4}{6{x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x+4}{6{x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186234103 :question/worked-solution "The function $\\operatorname{cos}(x)$ is a bounded, oscillating function with\n\n$$\n|\\operatorname{cos}x|\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSo, the numerator here is a bounded, oscillating function with\n\n$$\n3\\le \\operatorname{cos}x+4\\le 5.\n$$\n\nHowever, the denominator $6{x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}$ grows without bound as $x\\to \\infty .$ So the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x+4}{6{x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see this from the graph of $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x+4}{6{x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}},$ shown below:\n\n![](images/2c/2c4c732472ffcfa4a42e7a0dd7e1ff55a9b4d2f0d5b0b652b875efa284d9274d.png)" "The function $\\operatorname{cos}(x)$ is a bounded, oscillating function with\n\n$$\n|\\operatorname{cos}x|\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSo, the numerator here is a bounded, oscillating function with\n\n$$\n3\\le \\operatorname{cos}x+4\\le 5.\n$$\n\nHowever, the denominator $6{x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}$ grows without bound as $x\\to \\infty .$ So the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x+4}{6{x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see this from the graph of $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x+4}{6{x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}},$ shown below:\n\n![](images/2c/2c4c732472ffcfa4a42e7a0dd7e1ff55a9b4d2f0d5b0b652b875efa284d9274d.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186234108 :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{cos}^{2}⁡(-\\pi +\\frac{\\pi k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}].\n$$" "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{cos}^{2}⁡(-\\pi +\\frac{\\pi k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186234108 :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=-\\pi .$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{\\pi }{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{n}=\\frac{b-(-\\pi )}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=0.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=-\\pi +\\frac{\\pi k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})={cos}^{2}⁡{x}_{k}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}{cos}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{cos}^{2}⁡(-\\pi +\\frac{\\pi k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)={cos}^{2}⁡x$ and $b=0.$" "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=-\\pi .$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{\\pi }{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{n}=\\frac{b-(-\\pi )}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=0.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=-\\pi +\\frac{\\pi k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})={cos}^{2}⁡{x}_{k}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}{cos}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{cos}^{2}⁡(-\\pi +\\frac{\\pi k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)={cos}^{2}⁡x$ and $b=0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186234125 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}8x-1}{2x}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}8x-1}{2x}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186234146 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{log}(2-7x)=$" "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{log}(2-7x)=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186234146 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{log}(2-7x).$\n\n![](images/b4/b4fcf2946431788963444f22bcd51b2a617b53dfc4bbfdd0d8b90bd9fd9304d2.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ decreases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{log}(2-7x)=\\infty \n$$" "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{log}(2-7x).$\n\n![](images/b4/b4fcf2946431788963444f22bcd51b2a617b53dfc4bbfdd0d8b90bd9fd9304d2.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ decreases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{log}(2-7x)=\\infty \n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186234147 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(3x-\\operatorname{ln}(2x))=$" "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(3x-\\operatorname{ln}(2x))=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186234147 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{ln}(2x).$\n\n![](images/cb/cb507d2c8846ff1a55d2ff32c2db9264be34c008fff61098a3e3ad81d8c1c4de.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\le 0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{ln}(2x)=\\text{DNE}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(3x-\\operatorname{ln}(2x))=\\text{DNE}\n$$" "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{ln}(2x).$\n\n![](images/cb/cb507d2c8846ff1a55d2ff32c2db9264be34c008fff61098a3e3ad81d8c1c4de.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\le 0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{ln}(2x)=\\text{DNE}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(3x-\\operatorname{ln}(2x))=\\text{DNE}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186234238 :question/problem "Given that $p(x)=\\frac{8}{{x}^{5}}$ and $q(x)=-\\frac{2}{3{x}^{3}}$, what is ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)}{q(x)}?$" "Given that $p(x)=\\frac{8}{{x}^{5}}$ and $q(x)=-\\frac{2}{3{x}^{3}}$, what is $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)}{q(x)}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186234239 :question/problem "Compute ${lim}_{x\\to -2}\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{6}}.$" "Compute $\\lim_{x\\to -2}\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{6}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186234239 :question/worked-solution "We can sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{6}}$ as follows:\n\n- Take the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}.$\n\n- Translate it to the left by $2$ units. This gives $y=\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{6}},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/b5/b5b7cefccb376e47581c1e858d8e1afaa707cfa6b87518da42a826ce58dd341c.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-{2}^{-})}\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{6}}=\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to (-{2}^{+})}\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{6}}=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -2}\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{6}}=\\infty .\n$$" "We can sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{6}}$ as follows:\n\n- Take the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}.$\n\n- Translate it to the left by $2$ units. This gives $y=\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{6}},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/b5/b5b7cefccb376e47581c1e858d8e1afaa707cfa6b87518da42a826ce58dd341c.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-{2}^{-})}\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{6}}=\\infty ,\\,\\lim_{x\\to (-{2}^{+})}\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{6}}=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -2}\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{6}}=\\infty .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186234240 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to 3}{log}_{3}(x-3).$" "Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to 3}{log}_{3}(x-3).$"] [:db/cas 17592186234240 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{3}(x-3).$\n\n![](images/91/91e0a0ebd65df128534d58d181e62be18aa1c398cf526da7838d1a56e62f7d08.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\le 3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}{log}_{3}(x-3)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x>3.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}{log}_{3}(x-3)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are not equal, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 3}{log}_{3}(x-3)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$" "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{3}(x-3).$\n\n![](images/91/91e0a0ebd65df128534d58d181e62be18aa1c398cf526da7838d1a56e62f7d08.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\le 3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}{log}_{3}(x-3)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x>3.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}{log}_{3}(x-3)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are not equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 3}{log}_{3}(x-3)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186234241 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\sqrt{x+3}=$" "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\sqrt{x+3}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186234241 :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\sqrt{x+3}.$ Let's sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/b0/b03d3bc42d076a63937ab41d939c6d39ceca8bdb879c9ed58d583ab8a694a979.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=\\infty .\n$$" "Let $f(x)=\\sqrt{x+3}.$ Let's sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/b0/b03d3bc42d076a63937ab41d939c6d39ceca8bdb879c9ed58d583ab8a694a979.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=\\infty .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186234242 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{z\\to -1}\\frac{(z-2)^{4}}{(z+2)^{3}}.$" "Find $\\lim_{z\\to -1}\\frac{(z-2)^{4}}{(z+2)^{3}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186234243 :question/problem "![](images/90/90f51ea4561fc1c1b892863daedc15bb813fbf18a03e1c9366d54f1a883d0db4.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /2}(4x)^{2}(f(x)-2)^{4}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above." "![](images/90/90f51ea4561fc1c1b892863daedc15bb813fbf18a03e1c9366d54f1a883d0db4.png)\n\nFind $\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi /2}(4x)^{2}(f(x)-2)^{4}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above."] [:db/cas 17592186234244 :question/problem "![](images/d8/d81155f8c9b683e85a7f00ef41ac9ddcf36e303b40ca51c6de3e5d74833d5825.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,3}(3x-10)^{3}(f(x)-x)^{2}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above." "![](images/d8/d81155f8c9b683e85a7f00ef41ac9ddcf36e303b40ca51c6de3e5d74833d5825.png)\n\nFind $\\lim_{x\\to \\,3}(3x-10)^{3}(f(x)-x)^{2}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above."] [:db/cas 17592186234245 :question/problem "![](images/17/1786cfa3ea3b08a97e59ec148a8fe710d6e4b11fc1cbc3582c4bf925a41bf19f.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}\\sqrt{17-2f(x)}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above." "![](images/17/1786cfa3ea3b08a97e59ec148a8fe710d6e4b11fc1cbc3582c4bf925a41bf19f.png)\n\nFind $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-2}\\sqrt{17-2f(x)}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above."] [:db/cas 17592186234247 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5x}{\\operatorname{ln}(7x)}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5x}{\\operatorname{ln}(7x)}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186234247 :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since $5x≫\\operatorname{ln}(7x)$ for large values of $x,$ the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5x}{\\operatorname{ln}(7x)}=\\infty .\n$$" "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since $5x≫\\operatorname{ln}(7x)$ for large values of $x,$ the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5x}{\\operatorname{ln}(7x)}=\\infty .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186234321 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}({x}^{2})}{{x}^{3}}=$" "$\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}({x}^{2})}{{x}^{3}}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186234323 :question/problem "The functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are twice-differentiable on an open interval containing $x=1.$ The values of $f,$ $g,$ and their respective derivatives are given below:\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | ${f}^{″}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ | ${g}^{″}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $4$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-2$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{f(x)+x}{g(x)-x}.$" "The functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are twice-differentiable on an open interval containing $x=1.$ The values of $f,$ $g,$ and their respective derivatives are given below:\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | ${f}^{″}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ | ${g}^{″}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $4$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-2$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFind $\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{f(x)+x}{g(x)-x}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186234333 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=-2$ and ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)=-3,$ then what is \n${lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}(3f(x)-xf(-2))?$" "If $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=-2$ and $\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)=-3,$ then what is \n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}(3f(x)-xf(-2))?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186234335 :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=1=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function is not right-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the right-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=2\\ne 1=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=-1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not right-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true." "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=1=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function is not right-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the right-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=2\\ne 1=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=-1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not right-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true."] [:db/cas 17592186234336 :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The function is not left-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=0\\ne -3=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=-3=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=-1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not left-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true." "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The function is not left-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=0\\ne -3=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=-3=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=-1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not left-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true."] [:db/cas 17592186234342 :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{sec}^{2}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi l}{2n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2n}].\n$$" "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{sec}^{2}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi l}{2n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2n}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186234342 :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{l})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{l}=a+lΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{\\pi }{2n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2n}=\\frac{(b-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right))}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{l}=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi l}{2n}\n$$ and $f({x}_{l})={sec}^{2}⁡{x}_{l}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{sec}^{2}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi l}{2n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{l})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{l}=a+lΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{\\pi }{2n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2n}=\\frac{(b-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right))}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{l}=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi l}{2n}\n$$ and $f({x}_{l})={sec}^{2}⁡{x}_{l}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{sec}^{2}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi l}{2n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186234393 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(2x+1)^{3}}{(x-2)^{5}}.$" "Find $\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(2x+1)^{3}}{(x-2)^{5}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186234394 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to 4}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}-1}{3x-2}.$" "Find $\\lim_{x\\to 4}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}-1}{3x-2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186234395 :question/problem "![](images/44/44196cce68dab39addc447b332273371d5becb94bc0068e5200eeda596239ffa.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(2f(x)+3)^{8}}{(f(x)+4)^{3}}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above." "![](images/44/44196cce68dab39addc447b332273371d5becb94bc0068e5200eeda596239ffa.png)\n\nFind $\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(2f(x)+3)^{8}}{(f(x)+4)^{3}}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above."] [:db/cas 17592186234396 :question/problem "![](images/4b/4b3ef4ce6c3c2d40c60639b8467649155fb7e8a3c018dff359bf766a86cf8054.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(3f(x)-1)^{2}}{(f(x)-1)^{3}}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above." "![](images/4b/4b3ef4ce6c3c2d40c60639b8467649155fb7e8a3c018dff359bf766a86cf8054.png)\n\nFind $\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(3f(x)-1)^{2}}{(f(x)-1)^{3}}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above."] [:db/cas 17592186234397 :question/problem "![](images/b1/b1d539ebf048585018c1f3ed0314d04eda6fc0250e1012e7b61de6c3f3f0dfe8.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}\\frac{\\sqrt{8f(x)+1}}{2(f(x))^{5}}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above." "![](images/b1/b1d539ebf048585018c1f3ed0314d04eda6fc0250e1012e7b61de6c3f3f0dfe8.png)\n\nFind $\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}\\frac{\\sqrt{8f(x)+1}}{2(f(x))^{5}}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above."] [:db/cas 17592186234399 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{2{e}^{x/5}}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{2{e}^{x/5}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186234399 :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since $2{e}^{x/5}≫x$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{2{e}^{x/5}}=0.\n$$" "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since $2{e}^{x/5}≫x$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{2{e}^{x/5}}=0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186234401 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}{log}_{4}(2-x).$" "Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}{log}_{4}(2-x).$"] [:db/cas 17592186234401 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{4}(2-x).$\n\n![](images/26/26fc43bf5d0d715faa38704ef48813392e9e55e84b519174c96fb4cfa8550350.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\ge 2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}{log}_{4}(2-x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x<2.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}{log}_{4}(2-x)=-\\infty .\n$$" "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{4}(2-x).$\n\n![](images/26/26fc43bf5d0d715faa38704ef48813392e9e55e84b519174c96fb4cfa8550350.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\ge 2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}{log}_{4}(2-x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x<2.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}{log}_{4}(2-x)=-\\infty .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186234508 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}\\right)=$" "$\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}\\right)=$"] [:db/cas 17592186234509 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\left(-\\frac{6}{{x}^{9}}\\right)=$" "$\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\left(-\\frac{6}{{x}^{9}}\\right)=$"] [:db/cas 17592186234510 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}\\frac{1}{x+1}=$" "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}\\frac{1}{x+1}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186234510 :question/worked-solution "We can sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{x+1}$ as follows:\n\n- Take the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n- Translate it to the left by $1$ unit. This gives $y=\\frac{1}{x+1},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/0e/0e8333166f36375ee91ce2fe958683f6f2ab01d02a94fe33ca5ecfec9349a146.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}\\frac{1}{x+1}=-\\infty .\n$$" "We can sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{x+1}$ as follows:\n\n- Take the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n- Translate it to the left by $1$ unit. This gives $y=\\frac{1}{x+1},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/0e/0e8333166f36375ee91ce2fe958683f6f2ab01d02a94fe33ca5ecfec9349a146.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}\\frac{1}{x+1}=-\\infty .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186234511 :question/problem "Compute ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\,\\frac{3}{7(x-1)^{4}}.$" "Compute $\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\,\\frac{3}{7(x-1)^{4}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186234513 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}{log}_{7}(x-3).$" "Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}{log}_{7}(x-3).$"] [:db/cas 17592186234513 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{7}(x-3).$\n\n![](images/9a/9a25c28ba2bb1c47a32b56620bfb8038d0ec76a51494ee40818c3c18fff94101.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\le 3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}{log}_{7}(x-3)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x>3.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}{log}_{7}(x-3)=-\\infty .\n$$" "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{7}(x-3).$\n\n![](images/9a/9a25c28ba2bb1c47a32b56620bfb8038d0ec76a51494ee40818c3c18fff94101.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\le 3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}{log}_{7}(x-3)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x>3.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}{log}_{7}(x-3)=-\\infty .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186234514 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}{log}_{7}(-x-1)=$" "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}{log}_{7}(-x-1)=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186234514 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{7}(-x-1).$\n\n![](images/3a/3ab8924d6d0f1be64e7b664f0a68e5d75c7721f7ac804241ecb311c8a7a4f095.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\ge -1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}{log}_{7}(-x-1)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x<-1.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}{log}_{7}(-x-1)=-\\infty .\n$$" "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{7}(-x-1).$\n\n![](images/3a/3ab8924d6d0f1be64e7b664f0a68e5d75c7721f7ac804241ecb311c8a7a4f095.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\ge -1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}{log}_{7}(-x-1)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x<-1.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}{log}_{7}(-x-1)=-\\infty .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186234515 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to 2}{log}_{7}(x-2).$" "Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to 2}{log}_{7}(x-2).$"] [:db/cas 17592186234515 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{7}(x-2).$\n\n![](images/2f/2f6ced8942365f93dcff7c4d2b5b902906b913c1959137aeedbe30f907745666.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\le 2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}{log}_{7}(x-2)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x>2.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}{log}_{7}(x-2)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are not equal, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 2}{log}_{7}(x-2)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$" "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{7}(x-2).$\n\n![](images/2f/2f6ced8942365f93dcff7c4d2b5b902906b913c1959137aeedbe30f907745666.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\le 2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}{log}_{7}(x-2)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x>2.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}{log}_{7}(x-2)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are not equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2}{log}_{7}(x-2)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186234517 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\sqrt{4x-5}.$" "Find $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\sqrt{4x-5}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186234517 :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\sqrt{4x-5}.$ Let's sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/24/24d694316a99dc9455c772a525c03700dc866b12662d2dc0a90399a7ca44a374.png)\n\nWe see that the function is not defined for $x<\\frac{5}{4}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$" "Let $f(x)=\\sqrt{4x-5}.$ Let's sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/24/24d694316a99dc9455c772a525c03700dc866b12662d2dc0a90399a7ca44a374.png)\n\nWe see that the function is not defined for $x<\\frac{5}{4}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186234520 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to -1}\\sqrt[3]{4x+5}.$" "Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to -1}\\sqrt[3]{4x+5}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186234522 :question/problem "![](images/43/4317726b2c9fbe22707377e1c713e8e8271ae95f0d818ac658da02cc81d64202.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to 0}({x}^{3}-5f(x))$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above." "![](images/43/4317726b2c9fbe22707377e1c713e8e8271ae95f0d818ac658da02cc81d64202.png)\n\nFind $\\lim_{x\\to 0}({x}^{3}-5f(x))$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above."] [:db/cas 17592186234523 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\operatorname{csc}(x+\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$" "Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\operatorname{csc}(x+\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$"] [:db/cas 17592186235350 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{y(x)-2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)}=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{y(x)-2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)}=\n$$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186235374 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x).$ If a limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x)=$\n{{field-2}}" "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)$ and $\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x).$ If a limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x)=\n$$\n{{field-2}}"] [:db/cas 17592186235374 :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $-1$ from the left, the function $g(x)$ approaches $2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)=2.\n$$\n\nLikewise, as $x$ approaches $-1$ from the right, the function $g(x)$ approaches $0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)=2,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$" "As $x$ approaches $-1$ from the left, the function $g(x)$ approaches $2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)=2.\n$$\n\nLikewise, as $x$ approaches $-1$ from the right, the function $g(x)$ approaches $0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)=2,\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186235375 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186235375 :question/worked-solution "In part 1, we showed that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)=2,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$\n\nSince ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x),$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$" "In part 1, we showed that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)=2,\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$\n\nSince $\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x),$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186235376 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}g(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 2}g(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}g(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2}g(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186235376 :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we see that as we approach $x=2$ from the left, the $y$-value approaches $-1.$ So the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}g(x)=-1.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, when we approach $x=2$ from the right, the $y$-value again approaches $-1.$ So the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}g(x)=-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince both the left and right-hand limits exist and are equal to $-1,$ we conclude that\n\n \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}g(x)=-1.\n$$" "From the graph, we see that as we approach $x=2$ from the left, the $y$-value approaches $-1.$ So the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}g(x)=-1.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, when we approach $x=2$ from the right, the $y$-value again approaches $-1.$ So the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}g(x)=-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince both the left and right-hand limits exist and are equal to $-1,$ we conclude that\n\n \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}g(x)=-1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186235377 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }g(x).$ If a limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }g(x)=$\n{{field-2}}" "Find $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)$ and $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }g(x).$ If a limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }g(x)=\n$$\n{{field-2}}"] [:db/cas 17592186235377 :question/worked-solution "On the left side of the graph, we see that as the values of $x$ decrease and approach $-\\infty ,$ the graph of the function $g(x)$ moves closer and closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=3.$\n\nConsequently, $g(x)$ approaches the value $3$ as $x$ approaches $-\\infty ,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)=3.\n$$\n\nOn the right side of the graph, as the values of $x$ increase and approach $\\infty ,$ the graph of the function decrease without bound to $-\\infty .$   Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }g(x)=-\\infty .\n$$" "On the left side of the graph, we see that as the values of $x$ decrease and approach $-\\infty ,$ the graph of the function $g(x)$ moves closer and closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=3.$\n\nConsequently, $g(x)$ approaches the value $3$ as $x$ approaches $-\\infty ,$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)=3.\n$$\n\nOn the right side of the graph, as the values of $x$ increase and approach $\\infty ,$ the graph of the function decrease without bound to $-\\infty .$   Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }g(x)=-\\infty .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186235378 :question/problem "Given that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}Cf(x)=\\frac{4}{3},$ where $C$ is a real constant, what is the value of $C?$\n\n$C=$\n{{field-1}}" "Given that $\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}Cf(x)=\\frac{4}{3},$ where $C$ is a real constant, what is the value of $C?$\n\n$C=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186235379 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(7f(