b/cas 17592186239262 :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)$ does not exist" "$\\lim_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)$ does not exist"] [:db/cas 17592186239476 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)\\ne 3" "\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)\\ne 3"] [:db/cas 17592186239737 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)" "\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)"] [:db/cas 17592186239975 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)\\ne f(3)" "\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)\\ne f(3)"] [:db/cas 17592186240118 :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist" "$\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist"] [:db/cas 17592186240120 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)\\ne f(-2)" "\\lim_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)\\ne f(-2)"] [:db/cas 17592186240178 :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})}f(x)$ does not exist" "$\\lim_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})}f(x)$ does not exist"] [:db/cas 17592186240788 :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is **not** defined at $x=0$” is true. From the graph we can see that no value has been assigned to $f(x)$ at $x=0.$\n\n- Statement “$\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)$ exists” is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x) & =3, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x) & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n$\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=3.$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$” is false. The function is not even defined at $x=0.$ The graph contains a break at $x=0.$ It's impossible to draw the graph of $f(x)$ without taking our pencil off of the paper." "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is **not** defined at $x=0$” is true. From the graph we can see that no value has been assigned to $f(x)$ at $x=0.$\n\n- Statement “$\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)$ exists” is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x) & =3, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x) & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=3.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$” is false. The function is not even defined at $x=0.$ The graph contains a break at $x=0.$ It's impossible to draw the graph of $f(x)$ without taking our pencil off of the paper."] [:db/cas 17592186240811 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=5$ and $f(5)=2,$ then what is the value of\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 5}\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-25f(x)}\\,?\n$$" "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=5$ and $f(5)=2,$ then what is the value of\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 5}\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-25f(x)}\\,?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186240812 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=4$ and $f(4)=-6,$ then what is ${lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{(f(x))^{2}}{6x}?$" "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=4$ and $f(4)=-6,$ then what is $\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{(f(x))^{2}}{6x}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186240813 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=7$ and $f(7)=13,$ then what is ${lim}_{x\\to {7}^{-}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-f(x))?$" "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=7$ and $f(7)=13,$ then what is $\\lim_{x\\to {7}^{-}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-f(x))?$"] [:db/cas 17592186240840 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=-5$ and ${lim}_{x\\to (-5)^{+}}f(x)=3,$ then what is ${lim}_{x\\to (-5)^{+}}(f(x)-2f(-5))?$" "If $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=-5$ and $\\lim_{x\\to (-5)^{+}}f(x)=3,$ then what is $\\lim_{x\\to (-5)^{+}}(f(x)-2f(-5))?$"] [:db/cas 17592186240841 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=-9$ and $f(-9)=4,$ then what is \n${lim}_{x\\to (-9)^{-}}(f(x)-x)^{2}?$" "If $f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=-9$ and $f(-9)=4,$ then what is \n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-9)^{-}}(f(x)-x)^{2}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186240842 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=-1$ and ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=-6,$ then what is \n${lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}(2f(x)-xf(-1))?$" "If $f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=-1$ and $\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=-6,$ then what is \n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}(2f(x)-xf(-1))?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186240855 :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “The function is left-continuous at $x=-1$” is false. The function is not left-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=1\\ne 0=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function is right-continuous at $x=-1$” is false. The function is not right-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the right-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=-1\\ne 0=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function is continuous at $x=-1$” is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=-1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is neither left-continuous nor right-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, none of the statements are true." "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “The function is left-continuous at $x=-1$” is false. The function is not left-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=1\\ne 0=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function is right-continuous at $x=-1$” is false. The function is not right-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the right-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=-1\\ne 0=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function is continuous at $x=-1$” is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=-1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is neither left-continuous nor right-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, none of the statements are true."] [:db/cas 17592186240856 :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “The function is left-continuous at $x=1$” is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=1,$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=4=f(1)\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function is right-continuous at $x=1$” is false.  The function is not right-continuous at $x=1,$ since the right-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=2\\ne 4=f(1)\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function is continuous at $x=1$” is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not right-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement  “The function is left-continuous at $x=1$” is true." "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “The function is left-continuous at $x=1$” is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=1,$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=4=f(1)\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function is right-continuous at $x=1$” is false.  The function is not right-continuous at $x=1,$ since the right-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=2\\ne 4=f(1)\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function is continuous at $x=1$” is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not right-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement  “The function is left-continuous at $x=1$” is true."] [:db/cas 17592186240864 :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(-1)=-3$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=1$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)\\ne f(-1)$ $\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=-1$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\n![](images/34/340443e20bc285d2e711b81608a60eb81ff33537ece23860b11871f5373dfde2.png)\n\nNotice that the functions $p(x)$ and $q(x)$ are continuous at $x=-1,$ while $g(x)$ and $h(x)$ both have a discontinuity at $x=-1$ but they are *not* removable." "The function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(-1)=-3$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=1$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)\\ne f(-1)$ $\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=-1$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\n![](images/34/340443e20bc285d2e711b81608a60eb81ff33537ece23860b11871f5373dfde2.png)\n\nNotice that the functions $p(x)$ and $q(x)$ are continuous at $x=-1,$ while $g(x)$ and $h(x)$ both have a discontinuity at $x=-1$ but they are *not* removable."] [:db/cas 17592186240868 :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(2)=-4$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)\\ne f(2)$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=2$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nNote that the function also has a discontinuity at $x=-2,$ but it is not a point removable discontinuity. Checking the conditions, we find that\n\n- $f(-2)=-2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo this time, the second condition failed, and therefore $x=-2$ is not a point removable discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, the correct answer is $x=2$ only." "The function has a discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(2)=-4$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)=2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)\\ne f(2)$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=2$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nNote that the function also has a discontinuity at $x=-2,$ but it is not a point removable discontinuity. Checking the conditions, we find that\n\n- $f(-2)=-2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo this time, the second condition failed, and therefore $x=-2$ is not a point removable discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, the correct answer is $x=2$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186240873 :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity at $x=-1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(-1)=2$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=-3$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)\\ne f(-1)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=-1$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nThe function also has a discontinuity at $x=3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(3)=-2$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=1$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)\\ne f(3)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=3$ is also a removable discontinuity.\n\nFinally,  the function has a discontinuity at $x=5.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(5)$ does not exists $\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the first condition that failed, which tells us that $x=5$ is not a removable discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, the correct answer is $x=-1$ and $x=3$ only." "The function has a discontinuity at $x=-1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(-1)=2$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=-3$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)\\ne f(-1)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=-1$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nThe function also has a discontinuity at $x=3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(3)=-2$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)=1$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)\\ne f(3)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=3$ is also a removable discontinuity.\n\nFinally,  the function has a discontinuity at $x=5.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(5)$ does not exists $\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the first condition that failed, which tells us that $x=5$ is not a removable discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, the correct answer is $x=-1$ and $x=3$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186240875 :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(2)=-1$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=0$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)\\ne f(2)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=2$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nThe function also has a discontinuity at $x=4.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(4)=-3$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x)=-1$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x)\\ne f(4)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=4$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nThe function has a discontinuity at $x=-1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(1)=3$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\times $\n\nSo this time, the second condition failed, and therefore $x=-1$ is not a removable discontinuity.\n\nFinally, the function has a discontinuity at $x=3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(3)=2$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\times $\n\nSo this time, the second condition failed, and therefore $x=3$ is not a removable discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, the correct answer is $x=2$ and $x=4$ only." "The function has a discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(2)=-1$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)=0$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)\\ne f(2)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=2$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nThe function also has a discontinuity at $x=4.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(4)=-3$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 4}f(x)=-1$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 4}f(x)\\ne f(4)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=4$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nThe function has a discontinuity at $x=-1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(1)=3$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\times $\n\nSo this time, the second condition failed, and therefore $x=-1$ is not a removable discontinuity.\n\nFinally, the function has a discontinuity at $x=3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(3)=2$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\times $\n\nSo this time, the second condition failed, and therefore $x=3$ is not a removable discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, the correct answer is $x=2$ and $x=4$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186240900 :question/worked-solution "The function $y=g(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=-3.$ Checking our conditions, we see that\n\n- $g(-3)=-2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-3)}g(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}g(x)=-2,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}g(x)=1.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-3$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\n![](images/38/3811fcaaf0bbf2158a046dca5c8dc232fd268bce664c810df9fe114a404296cf.png)\n\nNotice that $h(x)$ and $p(x)$ are continuous at $x=-3,$ while $f(x)$ and $q(x)$ both have a discontinuity at $x=-3,$ but they are *not* jump discontinuities." "The function $y=g(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=-3.$ Checking our conditions, we see that\n\n- $g(-3)=-2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to (-3)}g(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}g(x)=-2,\\,\\,\\lim_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}g(x)=1.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-3$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\n![](images/38/3811fcaaf0bbf2158a046dca5c8dc232fd268bce664c810df9fe114a404296cf.png)\n\nNotice that $h(x)$ and $p(x)$ are continuous at $x=-3,$ while $f(x)$ and $q(x)$ both have a discontinuity at $x=-3,$ but they are *not* jump discontinuities."] [:db/cas 17592186240910 :question/worked-solution "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(-1)=2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}f(x)=2,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to ,(-1)^{+}}f(x)=-1.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-1$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nIn the same way, the function has a jump discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(2)=-1$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=-1.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=2$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there is also a discontinuity at $x=4,$ but this is not a jump discontinuity." "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(-1)=2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}f(x)=2,\\,\\,\\lim_{x\\to ,(-1)^{+}}f(x)=-1.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-1$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nIn the same way, the function has a jump discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(2)=-1$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=-1.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=2$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there is also a discontinuity at $x=4,$ but this is not a jump discontinuity."] [:db/cas 17592186240911 :question/worked-solution "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(2)=4$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)=4,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=2$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there is also a discontinuity at $x=0,$ but it is not a jump discontinuity.\n\nHence, the correct answer is $x=2$ only." "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(2)=4$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)=4,\\,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=2$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there is also a discontinuity at $x=0,$ but it is not a jump discontinuity.\n\nHence, the correct answer is $x=2$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186241048 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{4{sin}^{2}⁡x}{5{x}^{2}}=$" "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{4{sin}^{2}⁡x}{5{x}^{2}}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186243922 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{2x-{x}^{5}}{{x}^{3}+5}=$" "$\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{2x-{x}^{5}}{{x}^{3}+5}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186243935 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{(x-2)^{3}}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{(x-2)^{3}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186243935 :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since $(x-2)^{3}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{(x-2)^{3}}=0.\n$$" "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since $(x-2)^{3}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{(x-2)^{3}}=0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186243936 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2-{x}^{2}}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2-{x}^{2}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186243936 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the limit as\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2-{x}^{2}}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}-2}.\n$$\n\nThe numerator here is a bounded, oscillating function with\n\n$$\n|-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|\\le 3.\n$$\n\nHowever, the denominator ${x}^{2}-2$ grows without bound as $x\\to \\infty .$ So the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2-{x}^{2}}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}-2}=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see this from the graph of $y=\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2-{x}^{2}},$ shown below:\n\n![](images/9f/9fade66030e2e416f92138d787999d3ca3a7dd4788ad39cf29dd07ff944038da.png)" "First, we rewrite the limit as\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3\