ecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ except at the points $x=2$ and $x=5,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}h(x)=0,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}h(x)=1\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {5}^{-}}h(x)=1,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {5}^{+}}h(x)=2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$h$ only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186317209 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-\\frac{3}{5}$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {(-3/5)}^{-}}f(x)=7,$ then\n\n$f\\left(-\\frac{3}{5}\\right)=$\n{{field-1}}." "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-\\frac{3}{5}$ and $\\lim_{x\\to {(-3/5)}^{-}}f(x)=7,$ then\n\n$f\\left(-\\frac{3}{5}\\right)=$\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186317209 :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-\\frac{3}{5}.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to (-3/5)}f(x)$ exists, and\n\n$$\nf\\left(-\\frac{3}{5}\\right)={lim}_{x\\to (-3/5)}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {(-3/5)}^{-}}f(x)=7.\n$$" "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-\\frac{3}{5}.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that $\\lim_{x\\to (-3/5)}f(x)$ exists, and\n\n$$\nf\\left(-\\frac{3}{5}\\right)=\\lim_{x\\to (-3/5)}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {(-3/5)}^{-}}f(x)=7.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317223 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=3$ and $f(3)=-3,$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{x+(f(x))^{3}}{2}=$\n{{field-1}}." "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=3$ and $f(3)=-3,$ then\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{x+(f(x))^{3}}{2}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186317224 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=6$ and $f(6)=14,$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {6}^{+}}\\sqrt{f(x)+{x}^{2}-1}=$\n{{field-1}}." "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=6$ and $f(6)=14,$ then\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {6}^{+}}\\sqrt{f(x)+{x}^{2}-1}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186317225 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2$ and $f(-2)=2,$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}({x}^{3}+2(f(x))^{2})=$\n{{field-1}}." "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2$ and $f(-2)=2,$ then\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}({x}^{3}+2(f(x))^{2})=\n$$\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186317227 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=7$ and $f(7)=5,$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 7}\\frac{(f(x)+1)^{2}}{x-f(x)}=$\n{{field-1}}." "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=7$ and $f(7)=5,$ then\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 7}\\frac{(f(x)+1)^{2}}{x-f(x)}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186317256 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/7c/7cfd6da222fb36d06be1153ac0e398e567f8b9fb8cd1cd9e21f1bb35ed131f8d.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{false}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}(x+1)=-2+1=-1\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}(-2)=-2.\n$$\n\nSo, ${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statements I and III are false.\n\n**Note:** The $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/a5/a59fad69c9790a2b899c21fd730f779c28bc9b70f44a67851ad5ca92bff21951.png)" "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/7c/7cfd6da222fb36d06be1153ac0e398e567f8b9fb8cd1cd9e21f1bb35ed131f8d.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{false}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}(x+1)=-2+1=-1\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}(-2)=-2.\n$$\n\nSo, $\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statements I and III are false.\n\n**Note:** The $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/a5/a59fad69c9790a2b899c21fd730f779c28bc9b70f44a67851ad5ca92bff21951.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186317271 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/38/385029e7709a8d4eda45b101aba6f553c79ae3baef5b97080844e307a0b71cc7.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{true}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}(2x+3)=2(-3)+3=-3\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}((x-3)^{2}-3)=(3-3)^{2}-3=-3.\n$$\n\nSo, ${lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statement I is false.\n\n**Note:** The $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/f4/f4bfe80cbcc0821e93012a859ccc91b3614fa7db2df8e25399fbadc3ef7a3355.png)" "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/38/385029e7709a8d4eda45b101aba6f553c79ae3baef5b97080844e307a0b71cc7.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{true}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}(2x+3)=2(-3)+3=-3\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}((x-3)^{2}-3)=(3-3)^{2}-3=-3.\n$$\n\nSo, $\\lim_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statement I is false.\n\n**Note:** The $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/f4/f4bfe80cbcc0821e93012a859ccc91b3614fa7db2df8e25399fbadc3ef7a3355.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186317343 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ is not continuous on $x\\in (-3,3)$ because $\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to {0}^{+}.$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/77/7736417b068541a1609efa46e9824cd3f65cd34856c1d2f5d0ec7258ec883b5c.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ is not piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3)$ because ${F}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{false}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}(2x+1)=2(-3)+1=-5\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{9}.\n$$\n\nSo, ${lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statements I and II are false.\n\n**Note:** The $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/ac/ac4d89fcccca99bebaeab4b44baac9c5c03651c38cef7e4d14cfe57978d066b7.png)" "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ is not continuous on $x\\in (-3,3)$ because $\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to {0}^{+}.$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/77/7736417b068541a1609efa46e9824cd3f65cd34856c1d2f5d0ec7258ec883b5c.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ is not piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3)$ because ${F}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{false}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}(2x+1)=2(-3)+1=-5\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{9}.\n$$\n\nSo, $\\lim_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statements I and II are false.\n\n**Note:** The $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/ac/ac4d89fcccca99bebaeab4b44baac9c5c03651c38cef7e4d14cfe57978d066b7.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186317367 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\frac{7}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6-x))=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\frac{7}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6-x))=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186317367 :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6-x):$ \n\n![](images/04/04dd8257ef32291b930aa29f5bed4de2c41761692fd48d08ed2479f6cbd78659.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6-x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6-x)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\frac{7}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6-x))=\\frac{7}{\\pi }\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=-\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$" "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6-x):$ \n\n![](images/04/04dd8257ef32291b930aa29f5bed4de2c41761692fd48d08ed2479f6cbd78659.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6-x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6-x)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\frac{7}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6-x))=\\frac{7}{\\pi }\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=-\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317370 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }(10\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+3))=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }(10\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+3))=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186317370 :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+3):$ \n\n![](images/bb/bb49c052cabe12e8869270ab61b1de9e7f45b6acd6eaa9bc0d4c6892446a7cb6.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+3)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+3)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }(10\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+3))=(10\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})=5\\pi .\n$$" "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+3):$ \n\n![](images/bb/bb49c052cabe12e8869270ab61b1de9e7f45b6acd6eaa9bc0d4c6892446a7cb6.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+3)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+3)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }(10\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+3))=(10\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})=5\\pi .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317467 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186317470 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)-\\sqrt{3}}{2}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)-\\sqrt{3}}{2}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186319070 :question/problem "Express $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{3}+2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ as the limit of a left Riemann sum.\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{3}+2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[((\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{3}+2($\n{{field-2}}\n$)+1)\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]$" "Express $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{3}+2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ as the limit of a left Riemann sum.\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{3}+2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[((\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{3}+2($\n{{field-2}}\n$)+1)\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]$"] [:db/cas 17592186319071 :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[({2}^{\\,2+3k/n}+1)\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[({2}^{\\,2+3k/n}+1)\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}"] [:db/cas 17592186319071 :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=2.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{3}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{n}=\\frac{b-2}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=5.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=2+\\frac{3k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})={2}^{\\,{x}_{k}}+1.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{5}({2}^{x}+1)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[({2}^{\\,2+3k/n}+1)\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)={2}^{x}+1\\,\\text{and}\\,b=5.\n$$" "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=2.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{3}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{n}=\\frac{b-2}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=5.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=2+\\frac{3k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})={2}^{\\,{x}_{k}}+1.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{5}({2}^{x}+1)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[({2}^{\\,2+3k/n}+1)\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)={2}^{x}+1\\,\\text{and}\\,b=5.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186319077 :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[5{(2+\\frac{4k}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[5{(2+\\frac{4k}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}"] [:db/cas 17592186319077 :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=2.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{4}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{n}=\\frac{b-2}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=6.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=2+\\frac{4k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=5{x}_{k}^{3}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{6}5{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[5{(2+\\frac{4k}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)=5{x}^{3}\\,\\text{and}\\,b=6.\n$$" "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=2.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{4}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{n}=\\frac{b-2}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=6.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=2+\\frac{4k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=5{x}_{k}^{3}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{6}5{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[5{(2+\\frac{4k}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)=5{x}^{3}\\,\\text{and}\\,b=6.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186319081 :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{((-1+\\frac{3k}{n})+8)}^{1/3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{((-1+\\frac{3k}{n})+8)}^{1/3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}"] [:db/cas 17592186319081 :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=-1.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{3}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{n}=\\frac{b-(-1)}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=2.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=-1+\\frac{3k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=({x}_{k}+8)^{1/3}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{2}(x+8)^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{((-1+\\frac{3k}{n})+8)}^{1/3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+8)^{1/3}\\,\\text{and}\\,b=2.\n$$" "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=-1.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{3}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{n}=\\frac{b-(-1)}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=2.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=-1+\\frac{3k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=({x}_{k}+8)^{1/3}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{2}(x+8)^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{((-1+\\frac{3k}{n})+8)}^{1/3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+8)^{1/3}\\,\\text{and}\\,b=2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186319082 :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{2+\\frac{3k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{2+\\frac{3k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}"] [:db/cas 17592186319082 :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=2.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{3}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{n}=\\frac{b-2}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=5.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=2+\\frac{3k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=\\sqrt{{x}_{k}}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{2+\\frac{3k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{and}\\,b=5.\n$$" "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=2.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{3}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{n}=\\frac{b-2}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=5.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=2+\\frac{3k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=\\sqrt{{x}_{k}}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{2+\\frac{3k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{and}\\,b=5.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186319085 :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]$" "What is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]$"] [:db/cas 17592186319214 :question/problem "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{-8n+7}{4n}$ for $n\\ge 1?$\n\n${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{-8n+7}{4n}$ for $n\\ge 1?$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186319215 :question/problem "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{5{n}^{2}+7}{3{n}^{3}-2}$ f