}{2}$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{+}}f(x)=4,$ then what is $f\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)?$" "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\lim_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{+}}f(x)=4,$ then what is $f\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)?$"] [:db/cas 17592186323960 :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)$ exists, and\n\n$$\nf\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)={lim}_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{+}}f(x)=4.\n$$" "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that $\\lim_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)$ exists, and\n\n$$\nf\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\lim_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{+}}f(x)=4.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186326681 :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “The function is left-continuous at $x=0$” is false.  The function is not left-continuous at $x=0,$ since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=3\\ne 0=f(0)\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function is right-continuous at $x=0$” is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=0,$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=0=f(0)\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function is continuous at $x=0$” is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=0,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not left-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement “The function is right-continuous at $x=0$” is true." "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “The function is left-continuous at $x=0$” is false.  The function is not left-continuous at $x=0,$ since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=3\\ne 0=f(0)\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function is right-continuous at $x=0$” is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=0,$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=0=f(0)\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function is continuous at $x=0$” is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=0,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not left-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement “The function is right-continuous at $x=0$” is true."] [:db/cas 17592186326691 :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “The function is left-continuous at $x=-1$” is false. The function is not left-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=-1\\ne 1=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function is right-continuous at $x=-1$” is false. The function is not right-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the right-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=-1\\ne 1=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function is continuous at $x=-1$” is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=-1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is neither left-continuous nor right-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, none of the statements are true." "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “The function is left-continuous at $x=-1$” is false. The function is not left-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=-1\\ne 1=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function is right-continuous at $x=-1$” is false. The function is not right-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the right-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=-1\\ne 1=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function is continuous at $x=-1$” is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=-1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is neither left-continuous nor right-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, none of the statements are true."] [:db/cas 17592186326710 :question/worked-solution "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our conditions, we see that\n\n- $f(2)=2$ exists, $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)=2,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=3.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=2$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, “It has a jump discontinuity at $x=2.$” is true, and “It has a point discontinuity at $x=2.$” and “It is continuous at $x=2.$” are false." "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our conditions, we see that\n\n- $f(2)=2$ exists, $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)=2,\\,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=3.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=2$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, “It has a jump discontinuity at $x=2.$” is true, and “It has a point discontinuity at $x=2.$” and “It is continuous at $x=2.$” are false."] [:db/cas 17592186327014 :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist" "$\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist"] [:db/cas 17592186327019 :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)$ does not exist" "$\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)$ does not exist"] [:db/cas 17592186327169 :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist" "$\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist"] [:db/cas 17592186327175 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)" "\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)"] [:db/cas 17592186328017 :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist" "$\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist"] [:db/cas 17592186328277 :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)$ does not exist" "$\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)$ does not exist"] [:db/cas 17592186328550 :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist" "$\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist"] [:db/cas 17592186328685 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)\\ne f(2)" "\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)\\ne f(2)"] [:db/cas 17592186328835 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)\\ne f(3)" "\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)\\ne f(3)"] [:db/cas 17592186329077 :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)$ does not exist" "$\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)$ does not exist"] [:db/cas 17592186329758 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x+5}-\\sqrt{5}}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x+5}-\\sqrt{5}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186329759 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-2}{{x}^{2}-9}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-2}{{x}^{2}-9}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186329761 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{x-1}{x-\\sqrt{x}}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{x-1}{x-\\sqrt{x}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186329795 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{x-4}{\\sqrt{3x-3}-3}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{x-4}{\\sqrt{3x-3}-3}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186329796 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}-2}{{x}^{2}-4}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}-2}{{x}^{2}-4}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186329797 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to (64)^{-}}\\frac{x-8\\sqrt{x}}{x-64}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to (64)^{-}}\\frac{x-8\\sqrt{x}}{x-64}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186329798 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {25}^{-}}\\frac{2x-10\\sqrt{x}}{x-25}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to {25}^{-}}\\frac{2x-10\\sqrt{x}}{x-25}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186329802 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to \\pi }\\operatorname{cot}⁡x.$" "Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to \\pi }\\operatorname{cot}⁡x.$"] [:db/cas 17592186329854 :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=1$” is false. From the graph we can see that no value has been assigned to $f(x)$ at $x=1.$\n\n- Statement “$\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)$ exists” is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x) & =1, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n$\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=1.$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$” is false. The function is not even defined at $x=1.$ The graph contains a break at $x=1.$ It's impossible to draw the graph of $f(x)$ without taking our pencil off of the paper." "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=1$” is false. From the graph we can see that no value has been assigned to $f(x)$ at $x=1.$\n\n- Statement “$\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)$ exists” is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x) & =1, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$” is false. The function is not even defined at $x=1.$ The graph contains a break at $x=1.$ It's impossible to draw the graph of $f(x)$ without taking our pencil off of the paper."] [:db/cas 17592186329857 :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement “${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)$ is finite” is true. We get ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=1.$\n\n- Statement “${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)$ is **not** finite” is false. We get ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)=1.$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$  is  continuous at $x=-1$” is true. The graph of the function doesn't have a break at $x=-1$ and we can draw the graph of $f(x)$ around $x=-1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper." "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement “$\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)$ is finite” is true. We get $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=1.$\n\n- Statement “$\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)$ is **not** finite” is false. We get $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)=1.$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$  is  continuous at $x=-1$” is true. The graph of the function doesn't have a break at $x=-1$ and we can draw the graph of $f(x)$ around $x=-1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper."] [:db/cas 17592186329864 :question/worked-solution "The function $p(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $p(2)=2$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}p(x)=1$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}p(x)\\ne p(2)$ $\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=2$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\n![](images/fb/fbc65d4168ce2f6747e75a606b3ccc175f9a68d98f718e473255f085d929c334.png)\n\n  \n\nNotice that the functions $h(x)$ and $q(x)$ are continuous at $x=2,$ while $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ both have a discontinuity at $x=2$ but they are *not* removable." "The function $p(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $p(2)=2$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 2}p(x)=1$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 2}p(x)\\ne p(2)$ $\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=2$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\n![](images/fb/fbc65d4168ce2f6747e75a606b3ccc175f9a68d98f718e473255f085d929c334.png)\n\n  \n\nNotice that the functions $h(x)$ and $q(x)$ are continuous at $x=2,$ while $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ both have a discontinuity at $x=2$ but they are *not* removable."] [:db/cas 17592186329868 :question/worked-solution "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(1)=1$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to f(x)}$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=2,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=1$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there are also discontinuities at $x=-5$ and $x=-1,$ but they are not jump discontinuities.\n\nHence, the correct answer is $x=1$ only." "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(1)=1$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to f(x)}$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=2,\\,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=1$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there are also discontinuities at $x=-5$ and $x=-1,$ but they are not jump discontinuities.\n\nHence, the correct answer is $x=1$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186329870 :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity at $x=-2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(-2)=0$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=1$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)\\ne f(-2)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=-2$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nNote that the function also has a discontinuity at $x=2.$ but it is not a point discontinuity. Checking the conditions, we find that\n\n- $f(2)=0$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\times $\n\nSo this time, the second condition failed, and therefore $x=2$ is not a point discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, the correct answer is $x=-2$ only." "The function has a discontinuity at $x=-2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(-2)=0$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=1$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)\\ne f(-2)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=-2$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nNote that the function also has a discontinuity at $x=2.$ but it is not a point discontinuity. Checking the conditions, we find that\n\n- $f(2)=0$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\times $\n\nSo this time, the second condition failed, and therefore $x=2$ is not a point discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, the correct answer is $x=-2$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186329872 :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity at $x=3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(3)=1$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=0$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)\\ne f(3)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=3$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nNote that the function also has a discontinuity at $x=-3,$ but it is not a point discontinuity. Checking the conditions, we find that\n\n- $f(-3)$ does not exist. $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo this time, the first condition failed, and therefore $x=-3$ is not a point discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, the correct answer is $x=3$ only." "The function has a discontinuity at $x=3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(3)=1$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)=0$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)\\ne f(3)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=3$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nNote that the function also has a discontinuity at $x=-3,$ but it is not a point discontinuity. Checking the conditions, we find that\n\n- $f(-3)$ does not exist. $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo this time, the first condition failed, and therefore $x=-3$ is not a point discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, the correct answer is $x=3$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186329873 :question/worked-solution "For $x=1,$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $f(1)=1-{1}^{3}=0$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}({x}^{2}+3)=({1}^{2}+3)=4\n$$\n\nwhile \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(1-{x}^{3})=(1-{1}^{3})=0.\n$$\n\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal. $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=1.$\n\nHence, “It has a jump discontinuity at $x=1.$” is true, while “It has a removable discontinuity at $x=1.$” and “It's continuous at $x=1.$” are false." "For $x=1,$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $f(1)=1-{1}^{3}=0$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}({x}^{2}+3)=({1}^{2}+3)=4\n$$\n\nwhile \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(1-{x}^{3})=(1-{1}^{3})=0.\n$$\n\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal. $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=1.$\n\nHence, “It has a jump discontinuity at $x=1.$” is true, while “It has a removable discontinuity at $x=1.$” and “It's continuous at $x=1.$” are false."] [:db/cas 17592186329874 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10x)}{2{x}^{3}}-1)=$" "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10x)}{2{x}^{3}}-1)=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329874 :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since $2{x}^{3}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10x)$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10x)}{2{x}^{3}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10x)}{2{x}^{3}}-1)=0-1=-1.\n$$" "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since $2{x}^{3}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10x)$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10x)}{2{x}^{3}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10x)}{2{x}^{3}}-1)=0-1=-1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329875 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{2}{e}^{-x}-2).$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{2}{e}^{-x}-2).$"] [:db/cas 17592186329876 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x){e}^{-x}+5).$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x){e}^{-x}+5).$"] [:db/cas 17592186329877 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186329877 :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since $x≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}=0.\n$$" "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since $x≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}=0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329879 :question/worked-solution "To determine whether a function $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=2,$ we need to check whether the right-sided limit is equal to the function value.\n\nThe function plotted below has a discontinuity at $x=2,$ but is right-continuous at $x=2.$\n\n![](images/b0/b0c5521372b10882b6f5357cb678aef1274df935e7626bec930e3e084674ba62.png)\n\nIndeed, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)=1=f(2).\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=2.$" "To determine whether a function $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=2,$ we need to check whether the right-sided limit is equal to the function value.\n\nThe function plotted below has a discontinuity at $x=2,$ but is right-continuous at $x=2.$\n\n![](images/b0/b0c5521372b10882b6f5357cb678aef1274df935e7626bec930e3e084674ba62.png)\n\nIndeed, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)=1=f(2).\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186329912 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{|7x-4|}{2x-6}=$" "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{|7x-4|}{2x-6}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329929 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{|6x-2|}{8x+5}=$" "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{|6x-2|}{8x+5}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329932 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}+1}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}+1}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186329933 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{|10x+3|}{6-5x}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{|10x+3|}{6-5x}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186329940 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{|6x-4|}{3x+4}=$" "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{|6x-4|}{3x+4}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186330053 :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[5{(4+\\frac{i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]" "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[5{(4+\\frac{i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]"] [:db/cas 17592186330142 :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[5{(3+\\frac{i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[5{(3+\\frac{i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]"] [:db/cas 17592186330319 :answer/value "{lim}_{a\\to {0}^{-}}({\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x)" "\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{-}}({\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x)"] [:db/cas 17592186330402 :answer/value "{lim}_{a\\to (-1)^{+}}({\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x)" "\\lim_{a\\to (-1)^{+}}({\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x)"] [:db/cas 17592186330426 :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[{(2+\\frac{2m}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\f