)}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[{(4+\\frac{m}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]"] [:db/cas 17592186332117 :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[3\\sqrt{2+\\frac{3j}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]" "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[3\\sqrt{2+\\frac{3j}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]"] [:db/cas 17592186332681 :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{e}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+\\frac{ie}{n})\\cdot \\frac{e}{n}].\n$$" "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{e}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+\\frac{ie}{n})\\cdot \\frac{e}{n}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332681 :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=e.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{e}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{e}{n}=\\frac{b-e}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=2e.\n$$\n\nConsequently,  ${x}_{i}=e+\\frac{ie}{n}$ and $f({x}_{i})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{e}^{2e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+\\frac{ie}{n})\\cdot \\frac{e}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and $b=2e.$" "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=e.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{e}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{e}{n}=\\frac{b-e}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=2e.\n$$\n\nConsequently,  ${x}_{i}=e+\\frac{ie}{n}$ and $f({x}_{i})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{e}^{2e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+\\frac{ie}{n})\\cdot \\frac{e}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and $b=2e.$"] [:db/cas 17592186332769 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches at infinity,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡n}{n+2}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡n}{n+2}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges,  and its limit is $0.$" "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches at infinity,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡n}{n+2}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡n}{n+2}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges,  and its limit is $0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186332770 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nTo find the limit of each sequence, we must evaluate them for $n\\to \\infty .$\n\nFor ${a}_{n}=\\frac{n\\sqrt{n}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n)},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nFor ${b}_{n}=\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(2n)}{{e}^{n}},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{b}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nFor ${c}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{5}}{{e}^{n}},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{c}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"${a}_{n}$ only.\"" "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nTo find the limit of each sequence, we must evaluate them for $n\\to \\infty .$\n\nFor ${a}_{n}=\\frac{n\\sqrt{n}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n)},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nFor ${b}_{n}=\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(2n)}{{e}^{n}},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{b}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nFor ${c}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{5}}{{e}^{n}},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{c}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"${a}_{n}$ only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186332842 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-1}{x}.$" 