:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 49}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-7}{x-49}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 49}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-7}{x-49}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186363695 :question/problem "Calculate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{2n(x+y)}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Calculate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{2n(x+y)}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186363698 :question/problem "Calculate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{3n}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Calculate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{3n}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186363941 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{9n}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{9n}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186363944 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n/5}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n/5}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186363947 :question/problem "Calculate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{7nyz}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Calculate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{7nyz}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186363950 :question/problem "Calculate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{-n}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Calculate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{-n}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186363953 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -3}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x+22}-4}{x+3}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -3}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x+22}-4}{x+3}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186364199 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-3}{x-8}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-3}{x-8}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186364206 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 49}\\frac{x-49}{7\\sqrt{x}-x}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to 49}\\frac{x-49}{7\\sqrt{x}-x}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186364282 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 81}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-9}{x-81}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to 81}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-9}{x-81}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186364289 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 27}\\frac{x-27}{\\sqrt{3x}-9}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to 27}\\frac{x-27}{\\sqrt{3x}-9}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186364859 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{x}-x-1}=$" "$\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{x}-x-1}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186365568 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2\\sqrt{x}+9x-1}{3x+5}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2\\sqrt{x}+9x-1}{3x+5}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186365571 :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{8}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x+1)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $m$ and $n.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{8}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x+1)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\n$$\n{{field-1}}$)\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}]$" "What is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{8}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x+1)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $m$ and $n.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{8}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x+1)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\n$$\n{{field-1}}$)\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}]$"] [:db/cas 17592186366158 :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{4}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(4+\\frac{3i}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\n$$" "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{4}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(4+\\frac{3i}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186366158 :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=4.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{3}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{n}=\\frac{b-4}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=7.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{i}=4+\\frac{3i}{n}$ and $f({x}_{i})={x}_{i}^{3}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{4}^{7}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(4+\\frac{3i}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)={x}^{3}$ and $b=7.$" "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=4.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{3}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{n}=\\frac{b-4}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=7.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{i}=4+\\frac{3i}{n}$ and $f({x}_{i})={x}_{i}^{3}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{4}^{7}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(4+\\frac{3i}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)={x}^{3}$ and $b=7.$"] [:db/cas 17592186367247 :question/problem "Find the limit  $\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t),$ if $f(t)=(1-{t}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{1}{t-1}\\,j+\\sqrt{1-t}\\,k.$" "Find the limit  $\\,\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t),$ if $f(t)=(1-{t}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{1}{t-1}\\,j+\\sqrt{1-t}\\,k.$"] [:db/cas 17592186367275 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{s\\to 2}||f(s)||,$ if $f(s)=⟨{e}^{s-2},\\,\\sqrt{6-s},\\,\\frac{3}{s+2}⟩.$" "Calculate $\\lim_{s\\to 2}||f(s)||,$ if $f(s)=⟨{e}^{s-2},\\,\\sqrt{6-s},\\,\\frac{3}{s+2}⟩.$"] [:db/cas 17592186367282 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{s\\to 2}||f(s)||,$ if $f(s)=⟨\\frac{5}{{e}^{s-2}},\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4-2s),\\,{s}^{3}-5⟩.$" "Calculate $\\lim_{s\\to 2}||f(s)||,$ if $f(s)=⟨\\frac{5}{{e}^{s-2}},\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4-2s),\\,{s}^{3}-5⟩.$"] [:db/cas 17592186367985 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{s\\to 0}||f(s)||$, if $f(s)=⟨{e}^{s},\\frac{{s}^{2}-2}{s+6},{4}^{s}⟩.$" "Calculate $\\lim_{s\\to 0}||f(s)||$, if $f(s)=⟨{e}^{s},\\frac{{s}^{2}-2}{s+6},{4}^{s}⟩.$"] [:db/cas 17592186367992 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s)$, if $$\n\\displaystyle f(s)=⟨4{s}^{7}+13,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡s,\\,\\frac{{s}^{2}+13}{{e}^{s}}⟩.\n$$" "Calculate $\\lim_{s\\to 0}f(s)$, if $$\n\\displaystyle f(s)=⟨4{s}^{7}+13,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡s,\\,\\frac{{s}^{2}+13}{{e}^{s}}⟩.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186369553 :question/problem "Find $g(1)$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are two continuous vector-valued functions for which $f(1)=⟨0,1,-2⟩$ and \n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 1}[2f(t)+g(t)]=⟨-1,4,2⟩\\,.\n$$" "Find $g(1)$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are two continuous vector-valued functions for which $f(1)=⟨0,1,-2⟩$ and \n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to 1}[2f(t)+g(t)]=⟨-1,4,2⟩\\,.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186369560 :question/problem "Given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are two continuous vector-valued functions for which $f(4)=⟨1,2,-2⟩$ and $g(4)=⟨0,2,3⟩,$ determine\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 4}[3f(t)-2g(t)].\n$$" "Given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are two continuous vector-valued functions for which $f(4)=⟨1,2,-2⟩$ and $g(4)=⟨0,2,3⟩,$ determine\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to 4}[3f(t)-2g(t)].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186371000 :question/problem "Consider a vector-valued function $f(u)$ that is continuous over all real numbers and the real-valued function $u(t)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-t).$ Then ${lim}_{t\\to 0}f(u(t))=$" "Consider a vector-valued function $f(u)$ that is continuous over all real numbers and the real-valued function $u(t)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-t).$ Then $\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(u(t))=$"] [:db/cas 17592186371014 :question/problem "Find $g(1)$ given that $g(t)$ is a continuous vector-valued function and $u(t)$ is continuous real-valued function for which $u(1)=5$ and \n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 1}[u(t)g(t)]=⟨5,-10,20⟩\\,.\n$$" "Find $g(1)$ given that $g(t)$ is a continuous vector-valued function and $u(t)$ is continuous real-valued function for which $u(1)=5$ and \n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to 1}[u(t)g(t)]=⟨5,-10,20⟩\\,.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186371028 :question/problem "Given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are two continuous vector-valued functions for which $f(-2)=⟨0,3,-9⟩$ and $g(-2)=⟨2,0,1⟩,$ determine\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to -2}[\\frac{f(t)}{3}+2g(t)].\n$$" "Given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are two continuous vector-valued functions for which $f(-2)=⟨0,3,-9⟩$ and $g(-2)=⟨2,0,1⟩,$ determine\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to -2}[\\frac{f(t)}{3}+2g(t)].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186376171 :question/problem "The notation\n${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+3)^{2}}=0$\nmeans the following:\n\n$\\,$ \n{{field-1}} \n$ε>0,$\n{{field-2}} \n$N\\in N$ $,$ \n{{field-3}} \n$n\\in N,$\n{{field-4}} \n$\\,⇒\\,|\\frac{1}{(n+3)^{2}}|$ \n{{field-5}}" "The notation\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+3)^{2}}=0\n$$\nmeans the following:\n\n$\\,$ \n{{field-1}} \n$ε>0,$\n{{field-2}} \n$N\\in N$ $,$ \n{{field-3}} \n$n\\in N,$\n{{field-4}} \n$\\,⇒\\,|\\frac{1}{(n+3)^{2}}|$ \n{{field-5}}"] [:db/cas 17592186376171 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that ${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+3)^{2}}=0,$ means the following: \n\nFor any real $ε>0$ there exists a natural number $N$ such that, for every natural number $n\\ge N,$ we have \n\n$$\n|\\frac{1}{(n+3)^{2}}|<ε.\n$$\n\nThis can also be expressed as follows:\n\nFor any real $ε>0$ there exists a natural number $N$ such that, for every natural number $n,$ if $n\\ge N,$ then \n\n$$\n|\\frac{1}{(n+3)^{2}}|<ε.\n$$\n\nTranslating this into symbolic form, we get\n\n$$\n∀ε>0,\\,∃N\\in N,∀n\\in N,\\,n\\ge N\\,⇒\\,|\\frac{1}{(n+3)^{2}}|<ε.\n$$" "First, recall that $\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+3)^{2}}=0,$ means the following: \n\nFor any real $ε>0$ there exists a natural number $N$ such that, for every natural number $n\\ge N,$ we have \n\n$$\n|\\frac{1}{(n+3)^{2}}|<ε.\n$$\n\nThis can also be expressed as follows:\n\nFor any real $ε>0$ there exists a natural number $N$ such that, for every natural number $n,$ if $n\\ge N,$ then \n\n$$\n|\\frac{1}{(n+3)^{2}}|<ε.\n$$\n\nTranslating this into symbolic form, we get\n\n$$\n∀ε>0,\\,∃N\\in N,∀n\\in N,\\,n\\ge N\\,⇒\\,|\\frac{1}{(n+3)^{2}}|<ε.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186376552 :question/problem "Using the formal definition of a limit, prove that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$" "Using the formal definition of a limit, prove that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186377695 :question/problem "Find  ${lim}_{x\\to (1,-3)}\\frac{xy-y}{x-1}.$" "Find  $\\lim_{x\\to (1,-3)}\\frac{xy-y}{x-1}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186377708 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}=$" "$\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186378230 :question/worked-solution "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=3.$ Checking our conditions, we see that\n\n- $f(3)=-1$ exists, $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)=-1,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)=3.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=3$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, “It has a jump discontinuity at $x=3$” is true, and “It has a removable discontinuity at $x=3$” and “It is continuous at $x=3$” are false." "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=3.$ Checking our conditions, we see that\n\n- $f(3)=-1$ exists, $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)=-1,\\,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)=3.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=3$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, “It has a jump discontinuity at $x=3$” is true, and “It has a removable discontinuity at $x=3$” and “It is continuous at $x=3$” are false."] [:db/cas 17592186378237 :question/worked-solution "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(-3)=4$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-3)}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-3)^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}f(x)=4.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-3$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nThe function also has a jump discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(2)=-3$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)=0,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=-3.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=2$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there is also a discontinuity at $x=-1$ and $x=4,$ but they are not jump discontinuities.\n\nHence, the correct answer is $x=-3$ and $x=2$ only." "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(