nsequently, the correct answer is $x=2$ and $x=5$ only." "The function has a discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(2)=3$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)=0$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)\\ne f(2)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=2$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nThe function also has a discontinuity at $x=5.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(5)=-1$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 5}f(x)=2$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 5}f(x)\\ne f(5)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=5$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nFinally,  the function has a discontinuity at $x=-1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(-1)=3$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)$ does not exists $\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the second condition that failed, which tells us that $x=-1$ is not a removable discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, the correct answer is $x=2$ and $x=5$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186386719 :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{3i/n}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3n}]" "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{3i/n}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3n}]"] [:db/cas 17592186386720 :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{3i/n}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]" "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{3i/n}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]"] [:db/cas 17592186386721 :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{i/n}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{i/n}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]"] [:db/cas 17592186386722 :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{3i/n}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{3i/n}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]"] [:db/cas 17592186386723 :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{i/n}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]" "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{i/n}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]"] [:db/cas