s 17592186393274 :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{2+\\frac{4j}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]" "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{2+\\frac{4j}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]"] [:db/cas 17592186393275 :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[2\\sqrt{6+\\frac{4j}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]" "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[2\\sqrt{6+\\frac{4j}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]"] [:db/cas 17592186393276 :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6+\\frac{4j}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]" "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6+\\frac{4j}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]"] [:db/cas 17592186393277 :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+\\frac{4j}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]" "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+\\frac{4j}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]"] [:db/cas 17592186396620 :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{6i/n}\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}].\n$$" "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{6i/n}\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186396620 :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=0.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{6}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{n}=\\frac{b-0}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=6.\n$$\n\nConsequently,  ${x}_{i}=\\frac{6i}{n}$ and $f({x}_{i})={e}^{{x}_{i}}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{6}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{6i/n}\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)={e}^{x}$ and $b=6.$" "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=0.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{6}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{n}=\\frac{b-0}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=6.\n$$\n\nConsequently,  ${x}_{i}=\\frac{6i}{n}$ and $f({x}_{i})={e}^{{x}_{i}}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{6}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{6i/n}\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)={e}^{x}$ and $b=6.$"] [:db/cas 17592186396634 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nTo find the limit of each sequence, we must evaluate them for $n\\to \\infty .$\n\nFor ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(3n)}{{e}^{2n}},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nFor ${b}_{n}=\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡n}{\\sqrt{n}},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{b}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nFor ${c}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}}{{e}^{n}},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{c}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"${a}_{n},$ ${b}_{n},$ and ${c}_{n}.$\"" "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nTo find the limit of each sequence, we must evaluate them for $n\\to \\infty .$\n\nFor ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(3n)}{{e}^{2n}},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nFor ${b}_{n}=\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡n}{\\sqrt{n}},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{b}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nFor ${c}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}}{{e}^{n}},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{c}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"${a}_{n},$ ${b}_{n},$ and ${c}_{n}.$\""] [:db/cas 17592186396654 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $I⊆R.$ A function $f:I\\to R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]⊂I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ The function is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=0,1,2,…,$ and any closed interval $[a,b]⊂[0,\\infty )$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](images/d5/d5a497337426dad07096702a3e211f525a959add8cb1fbaaef05fccac26d634b.png)\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ except at the points $x=1,$ $x=\\frac{5}{2},$ and $x=4,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}h(x)=0,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}h(x)=1\n$$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 5/{2}^{-}}h(x)=1,\\,{lim}_{x\\to 5/{2}^{+}}h(x)=2\n$$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}h(x)=2,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}h(x)=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f,g,$ and $h.$\"" "Suppose $I⊆R.$ A function $f:I\\to R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]⊂I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ The function is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=0,1,2,…,$ and any closed interval $[a,b]⊂[0,\\infty )$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](images/d5/d5a497337426dad07096702a3e211f525a959add8cb1fbaaef05fccac26d634b.png)\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ except at the points $x=1,$ $x=\\frac{5}{2},$ and $x=4,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}h(x)=0,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}h(x)=1\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 5/{2}^{-}}h(x)=1,\\,\\lim_{x\\to 5/{2}^{+}}h(x)=2\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}h(x)=2,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}h(x)=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f,g,$ and $h.$\""] [:db/cas 17592186396680 :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[2{e}^{(k\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)/n}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{n}].\n$$" "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[2{e}^{(k\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)/n}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{n}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186396680 :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=0.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{n}=\\frac{b-0}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\n$$\n\nConsequently,  ${x}_{k}=\\frac{k\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=2{e}^{{x}_{k}}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}2{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[2{e}^{(k\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)/n}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)=2{e}^{x}$ and $b=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$" "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=0.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{n}=\\frac{b-0}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\n$$\n\nConsequently,  ${x}_{k}=\\frac{k\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=2{e}^{{x}_{k}}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}2{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[2{e}^{(k\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)/n}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)=2{e}^{x}$ and $b=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186396792 :question/problem "Consider the following statement. \n\nIf $f(x)=3x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x,$ then $f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\nA proof of the statement is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,3x=O(3x)$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{L2}:\\,{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{2x}\\right)=1\n$$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=O(2x)$ as $x\\to 0$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,f=O(5x)$ as $x\\to 0$ \n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L1}$ follows from {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from lines \n{{field-2}} and \n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from line {{field-4}}  and \n{{field-5}}." "Consider the following statement. \n\nIf $f(x)=3x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x,$ then $f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\nA proof of the statement is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,3x=O(3x)$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{L2}:\\,\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{2x}\\right)=1\n$$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=O(2x)$ as $x\\to 0$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,f=O(5x)$ as $x\\to 0$ \n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L1}$ follows from {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from lines \n{{field-2}} and \n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from line {{field-4}}  and \n{{field-5}}."] [:db/cas 17592186396818 :question/problem "Consider the following statement. \n\nIf $f(x)=2+{e}^{-x},$ then $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nA proof of the statement is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,f=O(2+{e}^{-x})$  \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2}=0$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,{e}^{-x}=O(2)$  \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,f=O(2)$  \n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,f=O(1)$  \n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L1}$ follows from  \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from lines  \n{{field-2}}  \nand  \n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from line  \n{{field-4}}  \nand  \n{{field-5}}." "Consider the following statement. \n\nIf $f(x)=2+{e}^{-x},$ then $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nA proof of the statement is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,f=O(2+{e}^{-x})$  \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2}=0$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,{e}^{-x}=O(2)$  \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,f=O(2)$  \n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,f=O(1)$  \n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L1}$ follows from  \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from lines  \n{{field-2}}  \nand  \n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from line  \n{{field-4}}  \nand  \n{{field-5}}."] [:db/cas 17592186397587 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ is not continuous on $x\\in (-2,2)$ because $\\frac{1}{x}\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to {0}^{+}.$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/8e/8e5eec10427a7061a05c0bd1228ce1146234bf2b09d75bb86ba83b28fbe178d9.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ is not piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2)$ because ${F}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{false}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}(x+2)=-2+2=0\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{1}{x}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, ${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statements I and II are false.\n\n**Note:** The $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/4a/4afcdf86f777b9c7afc8af31b36cde4b80cfe394fd908db1ccd47ef48dff1617.png)" "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ is not continuous on $x\\in (-2,2)$ because $\\frac{1}{x}\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to {0}^{+}.$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/8e/8e5eec10427a7061a05c0bd1228ce1146234bf2b09d75bb86ba83b28fbe178d9.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ is not piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2)$ because ${F}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{false}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}(x+2)=-2+2=0\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{1}{x}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, $\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statements I and II are false.\n\n**Note:** The $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/4a/4afcdf86f777b9c7afc8af31b36cde4b80cfe394fd908db1ccd47ef48dff1617.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186398834 :question/worked-solution "The intermediate value theorem can be applied to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nThe function is discontinuous at $x=1.$ To see this, we note that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=2\\ne 3={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe conclude that the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain the point $x=1.$ \n\nFrom the given options, only the interval $[-2,0]$ is allowed.  The intervals $(1,3]$ and $[-2,1)$ are not closed, $[-2,1]$ and $[0,3]$ contain the point $x=1.$" "The intermediate value theorem can be applied to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nThe function is discontinuous at $x=1.$ To see this, we note that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=2\\ne 3=\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe conclude that the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain the point $x=1.$ \n\nFrom the given options, only the interval $[-2,0]$ is allowed.  The intervals $(1,3]$ and $[-2,1)$ are not closed, $[-2,1]$ and $[0,3]$ contain the point $x=1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186399440 :question/worked-solution "For $y=f(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $f(4)={4}^{2}-10=6$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}5=5\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}({x}^{2}-10)={4}^{2}-10=6.\n$$\n\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal. $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=4.$\n\nNow, for $y=g(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $g(4)=5-2(4)=-3$ exists  $\\,\\,✓$ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}(5-2x)=5-2(4)=-3\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}(x+3)=4+3=7.\n$$\n\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal. $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=4.$\n\nFinally, for $y=h(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $h(4)=5$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}h(x)={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}5x=5(4)=20\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}h(x)={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}5x=5(4)=20.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to 4}h(x)=20.$\n\n- Finally, we check that the value of the limit equals the value of the function:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 4}h(x)=20\\ne h(4)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo the value of the limit does not equal the value of the function.\n\nTherefore, $h(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=4.$" "For $y=f(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $f(4)={4}^{2}-10=6$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}5=5\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}({x}^{2}-10)={4}^{2}-10=6.\n$$\n\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal. $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=4.$\n\nNow, for $y=g(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $g(4)=5-2(4)=-3$ exists  $\\,\\,✓$ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}g(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}(5-2x)=5-2(4)=-3\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}g(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}(x+3)=4+3=7.\n$$\n\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal. $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=4.$\n\nFinally, for $y=h(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $h(4)=5$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}h(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}5x=5(4)=20\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}h(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}5x=5(4)=20.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and $\\lim_{x\\to 4}h(x)=20.$\n\n- Finally, we check that the value of the limit equals the value of the function:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 4}h(x)=20\\ne h(4)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo the value of the limit does not equal the value of the function.\n\nTherefore, $h(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=4.$"] [:db/cas 17592186402402 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-{x}^{4}+3).$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(-{x}^{4}+3).$"] [:db/cas 17592186402497 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=0,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=1" "\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=0,\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=1"] [:db/cas 17592186402498 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=1" "\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=1"] [:db/cas 17592186402499 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" "\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}"] [:db/cas 17592186402500 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=0,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" "\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=0,\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}"] [:db/cas 17592186402501 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" "\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}"] [:db/cas 17592186402662 :question/worked-solution "For $x=0,$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $f(0)={0}^{6}+1=1$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}{(x-1)}^{3}=(0-1)^{3}=-1\n$$\n\nwhile \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}({x}^{6}+1)={0}^{6}+1=1.\n$$\n\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal. $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ \n\nHence, “It has a removable discontinuity at $x=0.$” is false, “It has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$” is true and “It's continuous at $x=0.$” is false." "For $x=0,$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $f(0)={0}^{6}+1=1$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}{(x-1)}^{3}=(0-1)^{3}=-1\n$$\n\nwhile \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}({x}^{6}+1)={0}^{6}+1=1.\n$$\n\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal. $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ \n\nHence, “It has a removable discontinuity at $x=0.$” is false, “It has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$” is true and “It's continuous at $x=0.$” is false."] [:db/cas 17592186402675 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(4{x}^{2}-5x+16).$" "Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(4{x}^{2}-5x+16).$"] [:db/cas 17592186402881 :question/worked-solution "The function is continuous at $x=-1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(-1)=0$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=0$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=f(-1)$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\nOn the other hand, the function has a discontinuity at $x=1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(1)=1$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=0$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)\\ne f(1)$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=1$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, “It has a removable discontinuity at $x=1.$” is true while “It has a removable discontinuity at $x=-1.$” and “There are removable discontinuities at both $x=-1$ and $x=1.$” are false." "The function is continuous at $x=-1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(-1)=0$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=0$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=f(-1)$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\nOn the other hand, the function has a discontinuity at $x=1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(1)=1$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)=0$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)\\ne f(1)$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=1$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, “It has a removable discontinuity at $x=1.$” is true while “It has a removable discontinuity at $x=-1.$” and “There are removable discontinuities at both $x=-1$ and $x=1.$” are false."] [:db/cas 17592186402888 :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity at $x=0.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(0)=1$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)=2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)\\ne f(0)$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=0$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nIn the same way, the function has a discontinuity at $x=3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(3)=1$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=-2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)\\ne f(3)$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=3$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, “It has a removable discontinuity at $x=0.$” and “It has removable discontinuities at both $x=0$ and $x=3.$” are true while “It is continuous at $x=3.$” is false." "The function has a discontinuity at $x=0.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(0)=1$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)=2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)\\ne f(0)$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=0$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nIn the same way, the function has a discontinuity at $x=3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(3)=1$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)=-2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)\\ne f(3)$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=3$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, “It has a removable discontinuity at $x=0.$” and “It has removable discontinuities at both $x=0$ and $x=3.$” are true while “It is continuous at $x=3.$” is false."] [:db/cas 17592186402936 :question/worked-solution "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-1.$ Checking our conditions, we see that\n\n- $f(-1)=2$ exists, $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-1$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, “It has a jump discontinuity at $x=-1.$” is true, and “It has a removable discontinuity at $x=-1.$” and “It is continuous at $x=-1.$” are false." "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-1.$ Checking our conditions, we see that\n\n- $f(-1)=2$ exists, $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -1}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,\\,\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-1$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, “It has a jump discontinuity at $x=-1.$” is true, and “It has a removable discontinuity at $x=-1.$” and “It is continuous at $x=-1.$” are false."] [:db/cas 17592186402943 :question/worked-solution "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-2.$ Checking our conditions, we see that\n\n- $f(-2)=2$ exists, $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -2}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-2$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, “It has a jump discontinuity at $x=-2.$” is true, and “It has a point discontinuity at $x=-2.$” and “It is continuous at $x=-2.$” are false." "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-2.$ Checking our conditions, we see that\n\n- $f(-2)=2$ exists, $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -2}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,\\,\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-2$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, “It has a jump discontinuity at $x=-2.$” is true, and “It has a point discontinuity at $x=-2.$” and “It is continuous at $x=-2.$” are false."] [:db/cas 17592186403020 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}f(x)" "\\lim_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}f(x)"] [:db/cas 17592186403022 :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist" "$\\lim_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist"] [:db/cas 17592186403023 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)\\ne f(-1)" "\\lim_{x\\to -1}f(x)\\ne f(-1)"] [:db/