we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}x=-2\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}3=3.\n$$\n\nSo, ${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statements I and III are false.\n\n**Note:** The $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/1a/1ac6956ec22cfed2d304cc5c2352cf1fdf4c795ea423e7a19c8e3a3e2dc843ed.png)" "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/7a/7afa339233c7e21eda233f5b67ccf8be9e05fb4ce9484c98eaf59eb790e651ab.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{false}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}x=-2\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}3=3.\n$$\n\nSo, $\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statements I and III are false.\n\n**Note:** The $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/1a/1ac6956ec22cfed2d304cc5c2352cf1fdf4c795ea423e7a19c8e3a3e2dc843ed.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186425677 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-2,2).$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/0a/0a29065f008fac7d3fd32d52b2ddc0b7d9360ffd75475f6c169eeb26582d22d2.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{true}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}-4x=-4(-2)=8\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}{x}^{3}=(2)^{3}=8.\n$$\n\nSo, ${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{true}$ since statements I, II, and III are all true.\n\n**Note:** The $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/d7/d750ad2f9ea2a85446b94f7b19009ef74eeea25b4ccd1bf8ff4f408a5df381e2.png)" "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-2,2).$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/0a/0a29065f008fac7d3fd32d52b2ddc0b7d9360ffd75475f6c169eeb26582d22d2.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{true}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}-4x=-4(-2)=8\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}{x}^{3}=(2)^{3}=8.\n$$\n\nSo, $\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{true}$ since statements I, II, and III are all true.\n\n**Note:** The $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/d7/d750ad2f9ea2a85446b94f7b19009ef74eeea25b4ccd1bf8ff4f408a5df381e2.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186425678 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/2d/2d30d760181c95dd0764b9959ca91febb34e94618bb147d8022dae5745c38a1a.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{true}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}((x+2)^{3}+3)=(0)^{3}+3=3\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}(x+1)=2+1=3.\n$$\n\nSo, ${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statement I is false.\n\n**Note:** The $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/fc/fcdf5654322a6deff9d7598590b4e0e1ace6150020b4944ad844976fb0f70230.png)" "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/2d/2d30d760181c95dd0764b9959ca91febb34e94618bb147d8022dae5745c38a1a.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{true}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}((x+2)^{3}+3)=(0)^{3}+3=3\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}(x+1)=2+1=3.\n$$\n\nSo, $\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statement I is false.\n\n**Note:** The $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/fc/fcdf5654322a6deff9d7598590b4e0e1ace6150020b4944ad844976fb0f70230.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186425680 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-3,3).$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/11/11f9718c9384ad2fea0bf3f5cc7da720559c512a5b3cebbc11ca966d5ec0f28b.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{false}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}2x=2(-3)=-6\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}{x}^{2}=(3)^{2}=9.\n$$\n\nSo, ${lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statement III is false.\n\n**Note:** The $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/d0/d0212ece76aba288e88b0c4d9f2e0f6163f4fd1f594cee30e9ea5f764ebc5f69.png)" "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-3,3).$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/11/11f9718c9384ad2fea0bf3f5cc7da720559c512a5b3cebbc11ca966d5ec0f28b.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{false}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}2x=2(-3)=-6\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}{x}^{2}=(3)^{2}=9.\n$$\n\nSo, $\\lim_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statement III is false.\n\n**Note:** The $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/d0/d0212ece76aba288e88b0c4d9f2e0f6163f4fd1f594cee30e9ea5f764ebc5f69.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186436123 :question/problem "Find  ${lim}_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xz+xy+yz}{x+z}.$" "Find  $\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xz+xy+yz}{x+z}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186436124 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{zx+zy}{\\sqrt{x+y+4}-2}=$" "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{zx+zy}{\\sqrt{x+y+4}-2}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186436125 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{x(xy-1)}{y}.$" "Find $\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{x(xy-1)}{y}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186436126 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to (-2,-2)}\\sqrt{xy}.$" "Find $\\lim_{x\\to (-2,-2)}\\sqrt{xy}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186436127 :question/problem "Determine ${lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}}.$" "Determine $\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186436128 :question/problem "Determine ${lim}_{x\\to (1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{x+y}.$" "Determine $\\lim_{x\\to (1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{x+y}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186436129 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to (4,-4,-2)}\\frac{\\s