rac{2}{n}]"] [:db/cas 17592186504609 :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\cdot \\,\\frac{\\pi }{6n}$" "What is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\cdot \\,\\frac{\\pi }{6n}$"] [:db/cas 17592186504613 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}/3}(12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+\\pi ).$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}/3}(12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+\\pi ).$"] [:db/cas 17592186504615 :question/problem "What is the value of ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }(\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)+1)^{3}?$" "What is the value of $\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi }(\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)+1)^{3}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186504619 :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{log}_{2}\\,(5(3+\\frac{4k}{n}))\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{log}_{2}\\,(5(3+\\frac{4k}{n}))\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}"] [:db/cas 17592186504619 :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=3.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{4}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{n}=\\frac{b-3}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=7.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=3+\\frac{4k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})={log}_{2}\\,(5{x}_{k}).$ By replacing the sample point ${x}_{k}$ with the variable $x,$ we find the original function.\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{7}{log}_{2}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{log}_{2}\\,(5(3+\\frac{4k}{n}))\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)={log}_{2}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{and}\\,b=7.\n$$" "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=3.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{4}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{n}=\\frac{b-3}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=7.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=3+\\frac{4k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})={log}_{2}\\,(5{x}_{k}).$ By replacing the sample point ${x}_{k}$ with the variable $x,$ we find the original function.\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{7}{log}_{2}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{log}_{2}\\,(5(3+\\frac{4k}{n}))\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)={log}_{2}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{and}\\,b=7.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186504622 :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=3.$ Note that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=3.$\n\nThere are also discontinuities at $x=-2$ and $x=0$  but they are not discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes." "The function has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=3.$ Note that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=3.$\n\nThere are also discontinuities at $x=-2$ and $x=0$  but they are not discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes."] [:db/cas 17592186504631 :question/worked-solution "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if $\\frac{1}{x-3}$ grows without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\nWe compute the vertical asymptotes of $\\frac{1}{x-3}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx-3=0\\,⟹\\,x=3.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $x>3,$ we have that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}\\left(\\frac{1}{x-3}\\right)=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=3$ is indeed an infinite discontinuity.\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/13/13817b066347391aae800b751c50a7a411dc06372d346e0d9b87e6f3fe3ca1ba.png)" "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if $\\frac{1}{x-3}$ grows without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\nWe compute the vertical asymptotes of $\\frac{1}{x-3}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx-3=0\\,⟹\\,x=3.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $x>3,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}\\left(\\frac{1}{x-3}\\right)=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=3$ is indeed an infinite discontinuity.\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/13/13817b066347391aae800b751c50a7a411dc06372d346e0d9b87e6f3fe3ca1ba.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186504643 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)+6}{2x+3}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)+6}{2x+3}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186504648 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}\\frac{7\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}{{x}^{2}+5}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}\\frac{7\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}{{x}^{2}+5}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186504654 :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[7{(1+\\frac{2k}{n})}^{-3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[7{(1+\\frac{2k}{n})}^{-3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}"] [:db/cas 17592186504654 :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=1.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{2}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{n}=\\frac{b-1}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=3.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=1+\\frac{2k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=7{x}_{k}^{-3}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{3}7{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[7{(1+\\frac{2k}{n})}^{-3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)=7{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{and}\\,b=3.\n$$" "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=1.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{2}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{n}=\\frac{b-1}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=3.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=1+\\frac{2k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=7{x}_{k}^{-3}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{3}7{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[7{(1+\\frac{2k}{n})}^{-3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)=7{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{and}\\,b=3.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186504658 :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{(1+\\frac{4k}{n})+1}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{(1+\\frac{4k}{n})+1}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}"] [:db/cas 17592186504658 :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=1.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{4}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{n}=\\frac{b-1}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=5.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=1+\\frac{4k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=\\sqrt{{x}_{k}+1}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{(1+\\frac{4k}{n})+1}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{and}\\,b=5.\n$$" "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=1.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{4}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{n}=\\frac{b-1}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=5.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=1+\\frac{4k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=\\sqrt{{x}_{k}+1}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{(1+\\frac{4k}{n})+1}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{and}\\,b=5.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186504667 :question/worked-solution "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if either ${x}^{2}-1$ or $\\frac{2}{x-1}$ grows without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\n- We first compute the asymptotes of ${x}^{2}-1.$ Since ${x}^{2}-1$ is a polynomial, therefore it cannot grow without bound due to a vertical asymptote. So this function cannot have an infinite discontinuity.\n\n- Next, we compute the asymptotes of $\\frac{2}{x-1}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx-1=0\\,⟹\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $x>1$, we have that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\left(\\frac{2}{x-1}\\right)=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=1$ is indeed an infinite discontinuity.\n\nTherefore, the only point where the function $y=f(x)$ has an infinite discontinuity is  $x=1.$\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/6c/6cba4d1e7cab2340e24d176cee3e03c6a103cf0c69fbf15209c5f4b92a4993e2.png)" "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if either ${x}^{2}-1$ or $\\frac{2}{x-1}$ grows without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\n- We first compute the asymptotes of ${x}^{2}-1.$ Since ${x}^{2}-1$ is a polynomial, therefore it cannot grow without bound due to a vertical asymptote. So this function cannot have an infinite discontinuity.\n\n- Next, we compute the asymptotes of $\\frac{2}{x-1}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx-1=0\\,⟹\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $x>1$, we have that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\left(\\frac{2}{x-1}\\right)=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=1$ is indeed an infinite discontinuity.\n\nTherefore, the only point where the function $y=f(x)$ has an infinite discontinuity is  $x=1.$\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/6c/6cba4d1e7cab2340e24d176cee3e03c6a103cf0c69fbf15209c5f4b92a4993e2.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186504671 :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=3.$ Note that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=3.$\n\nThere is also a discontinuity at $x=-2,$ but this is not a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote." "The function has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=3.$ Note that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=3.$\n\nThere is also a discontinuity at $x=-2,$ but this is not a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote."] [:db/cas 17592186504672 :question/problem "Express $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{5}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+2)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ as the limit of a left Riemann sum.\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{5}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+2)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$)+2)\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]$" "Express $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{5}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+2)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ as the limit of a left Riemann sum.\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{5}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+2)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$)+2)\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]$"] [:db/cas 17592186504674 :question/worked-solution "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if either $\\frac{1}{5-x}$ or $\\frac{1}{8-x}$ grow without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\n- We first compute the asymptotes of $\\frac{1}{5-x}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\n5-x=0\\,⟹\\,x=5.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $x<5,$ which excludes $x=5,$ we have that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}\\left(\\frac{1}{5-x}\\right)=+\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=5$ is an infinite discontinuity.\n\n- Next, we compute the asymptotes of $\\frac{1}{8-x}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\n8-x=0\\,⟹\\,x=8.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $5\\le x<8$ and $x>8,$ which excludes $x=8,$ we have that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {8}^{-}}\\left(\\frac{1}{8-x}\\right)=\\infty \\,,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\left(\\frac{1}{8-x}\\right)=-\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=8$ is an infinite discontinuity.\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the function $y=f(x)$ has infinite discontinuities at \n\n$$\nx=5\\,\\text{and}\\,x=8.\n$$\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/a7/a7773030c42d9e26a737ad1ed0208900101ed291189c7a1bc1430a808659ba33.png)" "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if either $\\frac{1}{5-x}$ or $\\frac{1}{8-x}$ grow without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\n- We first compute the asymptotes of $\\frac{1}{5-x}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\n5-x=0\\,⟹\\,x=5.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $x<5,$ which excludes $x=5,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {5}^{-}}\\left(\\frac{1}{5-x}\\right)=+\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=5$ is an infinite discontinuity.\n\n- Next, we compute the asymptotes of $\\frac{1}{8-x}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\n8-x=0\\,⟹\\,x=8.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $5\\le x<8$ and $x>8,$ which excludes $x=8,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {8}^{-}}\\left(\\frac{1}{8-x}\\right)=\\infty \\,,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\left(\\frac{1}{8-x}\\right)=-\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=8$ is an infinite discontinuity.\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the function $y=f(x)$ has infinite discontinuities at \n\n$$\nx=5\\,\\text{and}\\,x=8.\n$$\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/a7/a7773030c42d9e26a737ad1ed0208900101ed291189c7a1bc1430a808659ba33.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186504678 :question/worked-solution "The function has discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes at $x=\\pm 2.$ Note that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=-2.$ Similarly\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=2.$\n\nThere is also a discontinuity at $x=0,$ but this is not a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote." "The function has discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes at $x=\\pm 2.$ Note that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=-2.$ Similarly\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=2.$\n\nThere is also a discontinuity at $x=0,$ but this is not a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote."] [:db/cas 17592186504686 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }6\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3-2x))=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }6\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3-2x))=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186504686 :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3-2x):$ \n\n![](images/87/873a6fb793d2ae68eab172132e467a48a43ab84733ad92155fe6cc012f516382.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3-2x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3-2x)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }6\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3-2x))=6\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=3\\pi .\n$$" "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3-2x):$ \n\n![](images/87/873a6fb793d2ae68eab172132e467a48a43ab84733ad92155fe6cc012f516382.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3-2x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3-2x)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }6\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3-2x))=6\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=3\\pi .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186504689 :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x):$ \n\n![](images/0f/0fa9338fbefb3682249a585b85d287a25868a8f137547830adf6085dae85139a.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)=\\pi .\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\pi }(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x))=\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot (\\pi )=1.\n$$" "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x):$ \n\n![](images/0f/0fa9338fbefb3682249a585b85d287a25868a8f137547830adf6085dae85139a.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)=\\pi .\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\pi }(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x))=\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot (\\pi )=1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186504700 :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+2)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+2)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[(\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{\\,3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]$" "What is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+2)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+2)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[(\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{\\,3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]$"] [:db/cas 17592186504705 :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=0.$ Note that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=0.$ \n\nThere are also discontinuities at $x=1$ and $x=3,$ but they are not discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes." "The function has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=0.$ Note that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that t