{x\\to (-5)}f(x)=-\\infty $" "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4x-5},$ which of the following statements is correct?\n\nI. $\\lim_{x\\to (-5)^{+}}f(x)=+\\infty $\n\nII. $\\lim_{x\\to (-5)^{-}}f(x)=+\\infty $\n\nIII. $\\lim_{x\\to (-5)}f(x)=-\\infty $"] [:db/cas 17592186511570 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{{x}^{2}+4x+3}.$" "Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{{x}^{2}+4x+3}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186511581 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{x}{6+x-{x}^{2}},$ which of the following statements is correct?\n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty $\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty $\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$" "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{x}{6+x-{x}^{2}},$ which of the following statements is correct?\n\nI. $\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty $\n\nII. $\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty $\n\nIII. $\\lim_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$"] [:db/cas 17592186512867 :question/problem "![](images/06/06e792c5b74eda036b55ac3728122737da22c9824f89f982b50657ab07391269.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above? \n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=2$\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$" "![](images/06/06e792c5b74eda036b55ac3728122737da22c9824f89f982b50657ab07391269.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above? \n\nI. $\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$\n\nII. $\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=2$\n\nIII. $\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$"] [:db/cas 17592186512867 :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. As $x$ approaches $0$ from the left and right we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=0,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=1\\,.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x),$ and hence ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. As $x$ approaches $1$ from the left and right we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=2,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\\,.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x),$ and hence ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We see from the graph that \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\n$$\n\n We can't say that the limits are equal because we can't compare undefined values.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true." "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. As $x$ approaches $0$ from the left and right we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=0,\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=1\\,.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x),$ and hence $\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. As $x$ approaches $1$ from the left and right we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=2,\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\\,.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x),$ and hence $\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We see from the graph that \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\n$$\n\n We can't say that the limits are equal because we can't compare undefined values.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true."] [:db/cas 17592186512873 :question/problem "![](images/51/51b119021be889ff440ec31d901187c3ee9072a507336a8e1a1854f729f2734f.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{-2f(x)}{3{x}^{2}}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above." "![](images/51/51b119021be889ff440ec31d901187c3ee9072a507336a8e1a1854f729f2734f.png)\n\nFind $\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{-2f(x)}{3{x}^{2}}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above."] [:db/cas 17592186514366 :question/problem "![](images/f8/f8b33f31c4b569da24aa8235421698859e74b144e783f0fb917a5f4b3679d834.png)