x)=1.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x).\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true."] [:db/cas 17592186514370 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{10x-3}}{{x}^{2}-3}.$" "Find $\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{10x-3}}{{x}^{2}-3}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186514371 :question/problem "![](images/a4/a45bf16dabedfdd0c8f4569289c56a5f89c127e240f57a8b9259429be86a9cde.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$ for the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above." "![](images/a4/a45bf16dabedfdd0c8f4569289c56a5f89c127e240f57a8b9259429be86a9cde.png)\n\nFind $\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$ for the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above."] [:db/cas 17592186514371 :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $2,$ the value of $f(x)$ approaches $0.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=0\\,.\n$$" "As $x$ approaches $2,$ the value of $f(x)$ approaches $0.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=0\\,.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186514399 :question/problem "![](images/b1/b12ba04b803d58c6cacf4fc781db5c7032c3aedad80506c101bf084109ebd797.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to -2}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{4(f(x))^{3}-3{x}^{2}}{2}}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above." "![](images/b1/b12ba04b803d58c6cacf4fc781db5c7032c3aedad80506c101bf084109ebd797.png)\n\nFind $\\lim_{x\\to -2}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{4(f(x))^{3}-3{x}^{2}}{2}}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above."] [:db/cas 17592186514416 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to -3}\\frac{({x}^{3}-6x+6)^{3}}{(2x+3)^{4}}.$" "Find $\\lim_{x\\to -3}\\frac{({x}^{3}-6x+6)^{3}}{(2x+3)^{4}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186514434 :question/problem "![](images/22/22acfe14709e4992ddadf6d1d4fcd0e2a533c93573c32dd4134ec3498b0ea805.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$ for the function above.\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "![](images/22/22acfe14709e4992ddadf6d1d4fcd0e2a533c93573c32dd4134ec3498b0ea805.png)\n\nFind $\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$ for the function above.\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186514434 :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $2,$ the value of $f(x)$ approaches $-3.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=-3.\n$$\n\nRemember, it does not matter that the actual value of $f(2)$ is $-2.$ All that matters is that as $x$ approaches $2,$ the function $f(x)$ *looks like* it is getting closer and closer to $-3.$" "As $x$ approaches $2,$ the value of $f(x)$ approaches $-3.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=-3.\n$$\n\nRemember, it does not matter that the actual value of $f(2)$ is $-2.$ All that matters is that as $x$ approaches $2,$ the function $f(x)$ *looks like* it is getting closer and closer to $-3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186514440 :question/problem "![](images/7d/7defdf7c31a164fdbe702c1f5e87567f5da868d7530143493f9713ff88de98bc.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\frac{3}{2}}f(x)$ for the function above." "![](images/7d/7defdf7c31a164fdbe702c1f5e87567f5da868d7530143493f9713ff88de98bc.png)\n\nFind $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-\\frac{3}{2}}f(x)$ for the function above."] [:db/cas 17592186514440 :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $-\\frac{3}{2},$ the value of $f(x)$ approaches $2.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\frac{3}{2}}f(x)=2\\,.\n$$\n\nRemember, it does not matter that the actual value of $f\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)$ is $0.$ All that matters is that as $x$ approaches $-\\frac{3}{2},$ the function $f(x)$ *looks like* it is getting closer and closer to $2.$" "As $x$ approaches $-\\frac{3}{2},$ the value of $f(x)$ approaches $2.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-\\frac{3}{2}}f(x)=2\\,.\n$$\n\nRemember, it does not matter that the actual value of $f\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)$ is $0.$ All that matters is that as $x$ approaches $-\\frac{3}{2},$ the function $f(x)$ *looks like* it is getting closer and closer to $2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186514441 :question/problem "![](images/c3/c336dbdbda8ea15f5d2e86eb75493690d11ad0080c3ceeac9f71ab48cb63737b.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)$ for the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "![](images/c3/c336dbdbda8ea15f5d2e86eb75493690d11ad0080c3ceeac9f71ab48cb63737b.png)\n\nFind $\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)$ for the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186514441 :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $1,$ the value of $f(x)$ approaches $3.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=3\\,.\n$$" "As $x$ approaches $1,$ the value of $f(x)$ approaches $3.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=3\\,.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186514469 :question/problem "![](images/67/6769c6590446a9599786758ba9cfaf17690f401d864b3ce5f2c3825d9ca432cd.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above?\n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=1$\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=0$\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$" "![](images/67/6769c6590446a9599786758ba9cfaf17690f401d864b3ce5f2c3825d9ca432cd.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above?\n\nI. $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=1$\n\nII. $\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=0$\n\nIII. $\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$"] [:db/cas 17592186514469 :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. As $x$ approaches $-1,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=1.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. As $x$ approaches $0,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=0.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ approaches $1,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=1,$ and consequently \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n \nSimilarly, as $x$ approaches $2,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=0,$ and consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=0.\n$$\n\n As a result,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x).\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true." "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. As $x$ approaches $-1,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=1.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. As $x$ approaches $0,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=0.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ approaches $1,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=1,$ and consequently \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n \nSimilarly, as $x$ approaches $2,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=0,$ and consequently, \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=0.\n$$\n\n As a result,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x).\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true."] [:db/cas 17592186514479 :question/problem "![](images/93/93fc83d79679ca9f2b58729af1e3e87c2a01c19c12bc97670fbff398d63d061d.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$ for the function above.\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "![](images/93/93fc83d79679ca9f2b58729af1e3e87c2a01c19c12bc97670fbff398d63d061d.png)\n\nFind $\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$ for the function above.\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186514479 :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $2,$ the value of $f(x)$ approaches $0.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=0.\n$$\n\nRemember, it does not matter that the actual value of $f(2)$ is $-2.$ All that matters is that as $x$ approaches $2,$ the function $f(x)$ *looks like* it is getting closer and closer to $0.$" "As $x$ approaches $2,$ the value of $f(x)$ approaches $0.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=0.\n$$\n\nRemember, it does not matter that the actual value of $f(2)$ is $-2.$ All that matters is that as $x$ approaches $2,$ the function $f(x)$ *looks like* it is getting closer and closer to $0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186518112 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{t\\to 2}\\frac{{t}^{2}+t-6}{{t}^{2}-9t+14}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{t\\to 2}\\frac{{t}^{2}+t-6}{{t}^{2}-9t+14}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186518149 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{3x+12}{{x}^{3}+64}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to -4}\\frac{3x+12}{{x}^{3}+64}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186518167 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{y\\to 0}\\frac{7{y}^{6}-3{y}^{4}}{5{y}^{5}+10{y}^{4}}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{y\\to 0}\\frac{7{y}^{6}-3{y}^{4}}{5{y}^{5}+10{y}^{4}}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186518196 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{y\\to 4}\\frac{{y}^{2}-16}{{y}^{2}-4y}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\lim_{y\\to 4}\\frac{{y}^{2}-16}{{y}^{2}-4y}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186519062 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" "\\lim_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=\\text{DNE}"] [:db/cas 17592186519120 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}" "\\lim_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}"] [:db/cas 17592186519181 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=3" "\\lim_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=3"] [:db/cas 17592186519223 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=-\\frac{5}{2}" "\\lim_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=-\\frac{5}{2}"] [:db/cas 17592186519314 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=0" "\\lim_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=0"] [:db/cas 17592186519516 :question/problem "![](images/1e/1e40e0ffa7880b3b3b63070f063de207dcfafbdbed5fdf02464e180feba715e2.png)\n\nThe function  $y=f(x)$ is defined on $x\\in (-\\frac{5}{2},3).$ Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,3}f(x).$" "![](images/1e/1e40e0ffa7880b3b3b63070f063de207dcfafbdbed5fdf02464e180feba715e2.png)\n\nThe function  $y=f(x)$ is defined on $x\\in (-\\frac{5}{2},3).$ Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,3}f(x).$"] [:db/cas 17592186519516 :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we see that it is not possible to approach $x=3$ from the right. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nWhen we approach $x=3$ from the left, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{-}}f(x)=0\\,.\n$$\n\nSince ${lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x),$ we  conclude  that\n \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\\,.\n$$" "From the graph, we see that it is not possible to approach $x=3$ from the right. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{3}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nWhen we approach $x=3$ from the left, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{3}^{-}}f(x)=0\\,.\n$$\n\nSince $\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x),$ we  conclude  that\n \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\\,.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186519532 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{y\\to 3}\\frac{{y}^{2}+3y-18}{{y}^{2}-3y}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{y\\to 3}\\frac{{y}^{2}+3y-18}{{y}^{2}-3y}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186519542 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -2}\\frac{{x}^{2}-5x-14}{x+2}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to -2}\\frac{{x}^{2}-5x-14}{x+2}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186519551 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{-2{x}^{4}+6{x}^{2}-4}{{x}^{2}-1}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{-2{x}^{4}+6{x}^{2}-4}{{x}^{2}-1}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186519589 :question/problem "![](images/39/39c2c86e1fca1dbcf80435237ba31fc4aed9ed8213578b4c5f3971408da59f2d.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above? \n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=3$\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=3$\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$" "![](images/39/39c2c86e1fca1dbcf80435237ba31fc4aed9ed8213578b4c5f3971408da59f2d.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above? \n\nI. $\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=3$\n\nII. $\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=3$\n\nIII. $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$"] [:db/cas 17592186519589 :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As $x$ approaches $0$ from the left and right we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=3,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. We see from the graph that \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)=3\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.$\n\n- Statement III is true.  We see from the graph that \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{+}}f(x)=2\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=2.$ Meanwhile, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)=2\n$$\n\n \nHence ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=2.$ Therefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x).$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is true." "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As $x$ approaches $0$ from the left and right we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=3,\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. We see from the graph that \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)=3\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.$\n\n- Statement III is true.  We see from the graph that \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{+}}f(x)=2\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=2.$ Meanwhile, \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)=2\n$$\n\n \nHence $\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=2.$ Therefore, $\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}f(x).$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is true."] [:db/cas 17592186520015 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\left(\\frac{3{x}^{2}+3(2)^{2}}{x-2}\\right)" "\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\left(\\frac{3{x}^{2}+3(2)^{2}}{x-2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186520061 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\left(\\frac{3{x}^{2}-3(2)^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\right)" "\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\left(\\frac{3{x}^{2}-3(2)^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186520107 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\left(\\frac{3{x}^{2}-3(2)^{2}}{x-2}\\right)" "\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\left(\\frac{3{x}^{2}-3(2)^{2}}{x-2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186520194 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\left(\\frac{3{x}^{2}-3(2)^{2}}{x+2}\\right)" "\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\left(\\frac{3{x}^{2}-3(2)^{2}}{x+2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186520312 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\left(\\frac{3{x}^{2}-3(2)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}\\right)" "\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\left(\\frac{3{x}^{2}-3(2)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186520482 :question/worked-solution "The definition of ${f}^{′}(2)$ is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\left(\\frac{f(x)-f(a)}{x-a}\\right).\n$$\n\nLet $a=2.$ Then, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\left(\\frac{3{x}^{2}-3(2)^{2}}{x-2}\\right).\n$$" "The definition of ${f}^{′}(2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\left(\\frac{f(x)-f(a)}{x-a}\\right).\n$$\n\nLet $a=2.$ Then, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\left(\\frac{3{x}^{2}-3(2)^{2}}{x-2}\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186521537 :question/problem "![](images/3c/3cc4f4447ee6fb212222b28c7e4c31cca435c542e3d2f25ae3f3331a3b98da2b.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graphs of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x).$ Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{f(x)+2g(x)}{1+xg(x)}.$" "![](images/3c/3cc4f4447ee6fb212222b28c7e4c31cca435c542e3d2f25ae3f3331a3b98da2b.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graphs of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x).$ Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{f(x)+2g(x)}{1+xg(x)}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186521544 :question/problem "![](images/b5/b563936f37e82c3f6a422f80b448a7902a656a2749a7abced6c7bb9225ecf2bc.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of $f(x),$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -4}(2{x}^{2}+1)f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}." "![](images/b5/b563936f37e82c3f6a422f80b448a7902a656a2749a7abced6c7bb9225ecf2bc.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of $f(x),$ then\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -4}(2{x}^{2}+1)f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186521555 :question/problem "![](images/6d/6dd62794ee072530cd2b6c8028101b58ae617d6757d29f79de90d1a439f6529b.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(6x-1)f(x)}{5}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above." "![](images/6d/6dd62794ee072530cd2b6c8028101b58ae617d6757d29f79de90d1a439f6529b.png)\n\nFind $\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(6x-1)f(x)}{5}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above."] [:db/cas 17592186522695 :question/problem "Find the right-sided limit of $f(x)$ at $x=-2.$\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x)\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" "Find the right-sided limit of $f(x)$ at $x=-2.$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x)\\,=\\,\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186522699 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to -1}f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -1}f(x)\\,=\\,\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186522712 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={x}^{2}+3x$ for $x<-4,$ compute the value of ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-5}\\frac{f(x)-f(-5)}{x-(-5)}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,-5}\\frac{f(x)-f(-5)}{x-(-5)}\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" "Given that $f(x)={x}^{2}+3x$ for $x<-4,$ compute the value of $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-5}\\frac{f(x)-f(-5)}{x-(-5)}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-5}\\frac{f(x)-f(-5)}{x-(-5)}\\,=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186522712 :question/worked-solution "First, comparing\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-5}\\frac{f(x)-f(-5)}{x-(-5)}\n$$\n\nwith the formula for the instantaneous rate of change,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={lim}_{x\\to \\,a}\\frac{f(x)-f(a)}{x-a},\n$$\n\nwe see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-5}\\frac{f(x)-f(-5)}{x-(-5)}={f}^{′}(-5).\n$$\n\nNow, computing the derivative of $f$ for $x<-1,$ we have \n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=2x+3,\n$$\n\nand therefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(-5)=2(-5)+3=-7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-5}\\frac{f(x)-f(-5)}{x-(-5)}=-7.\n$$" "First, comparing\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-5}\\frac{f(x)-f(-5)}{x-(-5)}\n$$\n\nwith the formula for the instantaneous rate of change,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,a}\\frac{f(x)-f(a)}{x-a},\n$$\n\nwe see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-5}\\frac{f(x)-f(-5)}{x-(-5)}={f}^{′}(-5).\n$$\n\nNow, computing the derivative of $f$ for $x<-1,$ we have \n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=2x+3,\n$$\n\nand therefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(-5)=2(-5)+3=-7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-5}\\frac{f(x)-f(-5)}{x-(-5)}=-7.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186522734 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)\\,=\\,\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186522796 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)\\,=\\,\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186524973 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}\\frac{3f(x)-g(x)}{x+2}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}\\frac{3f(x)-g(x)}{x+2}\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-2}\\frac{3f(x)-g(x)}{x+2}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-2}\\frac{3f(x)-g(x)}{x+2}\\,=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186529726 :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{4}^{+}}f(x)=-1,$ ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=3$" "$\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{4}^{+}}f(x)=-1,$ $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=3$"] [:db/cas 17592186529857 :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{4}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},$ ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$" "$\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{4}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},$ $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$"] [:db/cas 17592186530049 :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{4}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},$ ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=3$" "$\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{4}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},$ $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=3$"] [:db/cas 17592186530080 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{4}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=-1" "\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{4}^{+}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=-1"] [:db/cas 17592186530170 :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{4}^{+}}f(x)=-1,$ ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$" "$\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{4}^{+}}f(x)=-1,$ $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$"] [:db/cas 17592186530433 :question/problem "![](images/78/781d755a292a18934af5b28436593262070527aa41da3bda092f979d388ef1f7.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of $f(x),$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{-}}f(x)=$  \n{{field-1}},\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x)=$  \n{{field-2}}." "![](images/78/781d755a292a18934af5b28436593262070527aa41da3bda092f979d388ef1f7.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of $f(x),$ then\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{-}}f(x)=\n$$  \n{{field-1}},\n$$\n\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x)=\n$$  \n{{field-2}}."] [:db/cas 17592186530433 :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $-2$ from the *left*, the function $f(x)$ approaches $0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{-}}f(x)=0.\n$$\n\nAs $x$ approaches $-2$ from the *right*, the function $f(x)$ approaches $2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{-}}f(x)=0,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x)=2.\n$$" "As $x$ approaches $-2$ from the *left*, the function $f(x)$ approaches $0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{-}}f(x)=0.\n$$\n\nAs $x$ approaches $-2$ from the *right*, the function $f(x)$ approaches $2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{-}}f(x)=0,\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x)=2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186530442 :question/problem "![](images/21/21cf8b9a6a94900b68a565a2d0915ba849379a134009c37db64bd5ff1eea2ac7.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above? \n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x)$\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)$\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)$" "![](images/21/21cf8b9a6a94900b68a565a2d0915ba849379a134009c37db64bd5ff1eea2ac7.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above? \n\nI. $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x)$\n\nII. $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)$\n\nIII. $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)$"] [:db/cas 17592186530442 :question/worked-solution "First, let's compute the limits in question. \n\nAs $x$ approaches $-2$ from the left, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=2,$ and consequently\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{-}}f(x)=2\\,.\n$$\n\nAs $x$ approaches $-2$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=-2,$ and consequently\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x)=-2\\,.\n$$\n\nWe cannot approach $x=1$ from the right because the function is not defined to the right of $x=1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\\,.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we cannot approach $2$ from the left because the function is not defined to the left of $x=2.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\\,.\n$$\n\nAs $x$ approaches $1$ from the left, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=1,$ and consequently\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=1\\,.\n$$\n\nAs $x$ approaches $2$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=1,$ and consequently\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)=1\\,.\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- I. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x)$ becomes $2=-2,$ which is false.\n\n- II. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)$ is false because neither one-sided limit exists.\n\n- III. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)$ becomes $1=1,$ which is true.\n\nThus, only statement III is true." "First, let's compute the limits in question. \n\nAs $x$ approaches $-2$ from the left, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=2,$ and consequently\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{-}}f(x)=2\\,.\n$$\n\nAs $x$ approaches $-2$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=-2,$ and consequently\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x)=-2\\,.\n$$\n\nWe cannot approach $x=1$ from the right because the function is not defined to the right of $x=1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\\,.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we cannot approach $2$ from the left because the function is not defined to the left of $x=2.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\\,.\n$$\n\nAs $x$ approaches $1$ from the left, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=1,$ and consequently\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=1\\,.\n$$\n\nAs $x$ approaches $2$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=1,$ and consequently\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)=1\\,.\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- I. $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x)$ becomes $2=-2,$ which is false.\n\n- II. $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)$ is false because neither one-sided limit exists.\n\n- III. $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)$ becomes $1=1,$ which is true.\n\nThus, only statement III is true."] [:db/cas 17592186530458 :question/problem "![](images/44/441d472f60a9cc7415799032f66d2a618bf8419336f52aefb47f3b1ae7640875.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of $f(x),$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{-}}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}$,$\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{+}}f(x)=$\n{{field-2}}." "![](images/44/441d472f60a9cc7415799032f66d2a618bf8419336f52aefb47f3b1ae7640875.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of $f(x),$ then\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{3}^{-}}f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}$,$\n$$\n\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\,{3}^{+}}f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-2}}."] [:db/cas 17592186530458 :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $3$ from the *left*, the function $f(x)$ approaches $2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{-}}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\nAs $x$ approaches $3$ from the *right*, the function $f(x)$ approaches $2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{+}}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{+}}f(x)=2.\n$$" "As $x$ approaches $3$ from the *left*, the function $f(x)$ approaches $2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{3}^{-}}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\nAs $x$ approaches $3$ from the *right*, the function $f(x)$ approaches $2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{3}^{+}}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{3}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,{3}^{+}}f(x)=2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186530522 :question/problem "![](images/bd/bd7ee98596021320d0f24d5b813de476ffabaa48156316091e5f23fed9e20d30.png)\n\nThe function $y=g(x)$ (shown above) is defined on $x\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$ Find the left-sided limit at $x=-1$ and the right-sided limit at $x=2.$\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}g(x)=$\n{{field-1}}$,$\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}g(x)=$\n{{field-2}}." "![](images/bd/bd7ee98596021320d0f24d5b813de476ffabaa48156316091e5f23fed9e20d30.png)\n\nThe function $y=g(x)$ (shown above) is defined on $x\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$ Find the left-sided limit at $x=-1$ and the right-sided limit at $x=2.$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}g(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}$,$\n$$\n\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}g(x)=\n$$\n{{field-2}}."] [:db/cas 17592186530522 :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $-1$ from the left, the function $g(x)$ approaches $1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}g(x)=1.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, as $x$ approaches $2$ from the right, the function $g(x)$ approaches $0.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$" "As $x$ approaches $-1$ from the left, the function $g(x)$ approaches $1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}g(x)=1.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, as $x$ approaches $2$ from the right, the function $g(x)$ approaches $0.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186530531 :question/problem "![](images/f0/f01058e0460ac0de134f36ce8a83aada0a719565f78c640b713526ec1b1a3a99.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above?  \n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{3}^{+}}f(x)=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)$" "![](images/f0/f01058e0460ac0de134f36ce8a83aada0a719565f78c640b713526ec1b1a3a99.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above?  \n\nI. $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{3}^{+}}f(x)=0$\n\nII. $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$\n\nIII. $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)$"] [:db/cas 17592186530531 :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. As $x$ approaches $-3$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=0,$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{3}^{+}}f(x)=0\\,.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. As $x$ approaches $-1$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ is undefined, Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\\,.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ approaches $-1$ from the left, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=-2$, and consequently\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x)=-2\\,.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, as $x$ approaches $1$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=0$, and consequently\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)=0\\,.\n$$\n\n Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true." "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. As $x$ approaches $-3$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=0,$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{3}^{+}}f(x)=0\\,.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. As $x$ approaches $-1$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ is undefined, Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\\,.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ approaches $-1$ from the left, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=-2$, and consequently\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x)=-2\\,.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, as $x$ approaches $1$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=0$, and consequently\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)=0\\,.\n$$\n\n Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true."] [:db/cas 17592186530542 :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $-4$ from the *right*, the function $f(x)$ approaches $-1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{4}^{+}}f(x)=-1\\,.\n$$\n\nLikewise, as $x$ approaches $1$ from the *left*, the function $f(x)$ approaches $3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=3\\,.\n$$" "As $x$ approaches $-4$ from the *right*, the function $f(x)$ approaches $-1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{4}^{+}}f(x)=-1\\,.\n$$\n\nLikewise, as $x$ approaches $1$ from the *left*, the function $f(x)$ approaches $3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=3\\,.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186530547 :question/problem "![](images/11/11e1c9a550944fc8435fc0d6f17be7d172e0c6d8f82fc1af950896f6ec8176b6.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above?  \n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=1$\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x)$" "![](images/11/11e1c9a550944fc8435fc0d6f17be7d172e0c6d8f82fc1af950896f6ec8176b6.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above?  \n\nI. $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$\n\nII. $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=1$\n\nIII. $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{+}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x)$"] [:db/cas 17592186530547 :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As $x$ approaches $0$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$  approaches $y=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. As $x$ approaches $2$ from the left, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=1$, Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ approaches $-1$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ is undefined. Consequently,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nInstead, as $x$ approaches $-1$ from the left, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=1$, and consequently\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{+}}f(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As $x$ approaches $0$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$  approaches $y=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. As $x$ approaches $2$ from the left, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=1$, Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ approaches $-1$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ is undefined. Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nInstead, as $x$ approaches $-1$ from the left, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=1$, and consequently\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{+}}f(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."] [:db/cas 17592186534002 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x-y}-4}{16-2x+y}=$" "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x-y}-4}{16-2x+y}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186534979 :question/problem "Calculate $L={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ for the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{5}^{n}{n}^{n+1}}{(9+5n)^{n}}.$ According to the root test, does $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ converge or diverge?" "Calculate $L=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ for the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{5}^{n}{n}^{n+1}}{(9+5n)^{n}}.$ According to the root test, does $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ converge or diverge?"] [:db/cas 17592186534994 :question/problem "Calculate $L={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ for the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{11}^{n}}{{7}^{n}\\cdot n!}.$ Does $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ converge or diverge?" "Calculate $L=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ for the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{11}^{n}}{{7}^{n}\\cdot n!}.$ Does $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ converge or diverge?"] [:db/cas 17592186534998 :question/problem "Calculate $L={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ for the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{n/2}}{3{n}^{{n}^{2}}}.$ Does $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ converge or diverge?" "Calculate $L=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ for the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{n/2}}{3{n}^{{n}^{2}}}.$ Does $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ converge or diverge?"] [:db/cas 17592186535055 :question/problem "Calculate $L={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ for the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(10n)^{n}n}{(8n+1)^{n}}.$ According to the root test, does $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ converge or diverge?" "Calculate $L=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ for the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(10n)^{n}n}{(8n+1)^{n}}.$ According to the root test, does $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ converge or diverge?"] [:db/cas 17592186535069 :question/problem "Calculate $L={lim}_{n\\to