ve\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true.\n\n- Statement II is false.\n\n- Statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true."] [:db/cas 17592186548066 :question/problem "![](images/e3/e30b6d047b119e5beb5f0d5cf5e013628e7c9836e0ca264ee2da92d1e589a0fc.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Therefore\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=$\n\n{{field-1}}." "![](images/e3/e30b6d047b119e5beb5f0d5cf5e013628e7c9836e0ca264ee2da92d1e589a0fc.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186548066 :question/worked-solution "We see from the graph that as $x$ approaches $1$ from the left, the function $f(x)$ approaches $-\\infty .$ So, the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty \\,.\n$$\n\nLikewise, as $x$ approaches $1$ from the right, the function $f(x)$ again approaches $-\\infty .$ So, the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty \\,.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=-\\infty \\,.\n$$" "We see from the graph that as $x$ approaches $1$ from the left, the function $f(x)$ approaches $-\\infty .$ So, the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty \\,.\n$$\n\nLikewise, as $x$ approaches $1$ from the right, the function $f(x)$ again approaches $-\\infty .$ So, the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty \\,.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)=-\\infty \\,.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186548071 :question/problem "![](images/0c/0cdb3e9c74a36e5ef13436b7b18968064b278f6d8e0dffc27820f618f7f4fee9.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Therefore\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}." "![](images/0c/0cdb3e9c74a36e5ef13436b7b18968064b278f6d8e0dffc27820f618f7f4fee9.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Theref