t in part II.*" "Consider the polar curve $r={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ].$ Given that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\n$$\n\nand ${y}^{′}(\\theta )=0$ at $\\theta =0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${x}^{′}(0)=0$\n\nII. $\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0$\n\nIII. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =0$\n\n*Hint: It is not necessary to use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*"] [:db/cas 17592186549025 :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r=8{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta -8{sin}^{5}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=16{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1),\n$$\n\nand ${y}^{′}(0)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${x}^{′}(0)=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\infty $\n\nIII. The curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta =0$" "Consider the polar curve $r=8{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta -8{sin}^{5}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=16{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1),\n$$\n\nand ${y}^{′}(0)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${x}^{′}(0)=0$\n\nII. $\\lim_{\\theta \\to {0}^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\infty $\n\nIII. The curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta =0$"] [:db/cas 17592186550468 :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(3+\\frac{2i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]" "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(3+\\frac{2i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]"] [:db/cas 17592186550497 :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(3+\\frac{2i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(3+\\frac{2i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]"] [:db/cas 17592186550562 :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(1+\\frac{2i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(1+\\frac{2i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]"] [:db/cas 17592186550616 :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(1+\\frac{i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(1+\\frac{i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]"] [:db/cas 17592186550978 :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(1+\\frac{4i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]" "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(1+\\frac{4i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]"] [:db/cas 17592186551235 :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[4{(2+\\frac{k}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}].\n$$" "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[4{(2+\\frac{k}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186551235 :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=2.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{1}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{n}=\\frac{b-2}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=3.\n$$\n\nConsequently,  ${x}_{k}=2+\\frac{k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=4{x}_{k}^{3}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{3}4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[4{(2+\\frac{k}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)=4{x}^{3}$ and $b=3.$" "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=2.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{1}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{n}=\\frac{b-2}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=3.\n$$\n\nConsequently,  ${x}_{k}=2+\\frac{k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=4{x}_{k}^{3}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{3}4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[4{(2+\\frac{k}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)=4{x}^{3}$ and $b=3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186551285 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-2,2).$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/64/64b557365c3642ae7a4206794080804bf2bbccb07230dfa46a4f415570f85329.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{true}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}-5x=-5(-2)=10\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}=\\frac{5}{2}(2)^{2}=10.\n$$\n\nSo, ${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{true}$ since statements I, II, and III are all true.\n\n**Note:** The $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/cd/cd37979eada411b68755dfa85ec3b1e22585df2bff5c4bdd4b27cf06463706d6.png)" "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-2,2).$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/64/64b557365c3642ae7a4206794080804bf2bbccb07230dfa46a4f415570f85329.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{true}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}-5x=-5(-2)=10\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}=\\frac{5}{2}(2)^{2}=10.\n$$\n\nSo, $\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{true}$ since statements I, II, and III are all true.\n\n**Note:** The $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/cd/cd37979eada411b68755dfa85ec3b1e22585df2bff5c4bdd4b27cf06463706d6.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186551347 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-3,3).$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/66/66b91f732d36469ded18df5404b3f12dddad7474168efd47ddf038835b67b831.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{false}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}(-2x+1)=-2(-3)+1=7\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}({x}^{2}+1)=(3)^{2}+1=10.\n$$\n\nSo, ${lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statement III is false.\n\n**Note:** The $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/88/888053858d845bd47a56b953a5eb9a6f7f6b4ddffc0ecc70fa280e1a7d543f24.png)" "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-3,3).$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/66/66b91f732d36469ded18df5404b3f12dddad7474168efd47ddf038835b67b831.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{false}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}(-2x+1)=-2(-3)+1=7\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}({x}^{2}+1)=(3)^{2}+1=10.\n$$\n\nSo, $\\lim_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statement III is false.\n\n**Note:** The $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/88/888053858d845bd47a56b953a5eb9a6f7f6b4ddffc0ecc70fa280e1a7d543f24.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186552420 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to -5}\\frac{4x}{x+1}=${{field-1}}" "$\\lim_{x\\to -5}\\frac{4x}{x+1}=${{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186552439 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}(5f(x)+6g(x)+3{x}^{3}).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}(5f(x)+6g(x)+3{x}^{3})=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)}(5f(x)+6g(x)+3{x}^{3}).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)}(5f(x)+6g(x)+3{x}^{3})=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186552441 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }g(x).$ If a limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }g(x)=$\n{{field-2}}" "Find $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)$ and $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }g(x).$ If a limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }g(x)=\n$$\n{{field-2}}"] [:db/cas 17592186552441 :question/worked-solution "On the left side of the graph, we see that as the values of $x$ decrease and approach $-\\infty ,$ the graph of the function $g(x)$ moves closer and closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=-1.$\n\nConsequently, $g(x)$ approaches the value $-1$ as $x$ approaches $-\\infty ,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)=-1.\n$$\n\nOn the right side of the graph, as the values of $x$ increase and approach $\\infty ,$  the graph of the function $g(x)$ moves closer and closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=-1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }g(x)=-1.\n$$" "On the left side of the graph, we see that as the values of $x$ decrease and approach $-\\infty ,$ the graph of the function $g(x)$ moves closer and closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=-1.$\n\nConsequently, $g(x)$ approaches the value $-1$ as $x$ approaches $-\\infty ,$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)=-1.\n$$\n\nOn the right side of the graph, as the values of $x$ increase and approach $\\infty ,$  the graph of the function $g(x)$ moves closer and closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=-1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }g(x)=-1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186552447 :question/problem "Given that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}Cf(x)=8,$ where $C$ is a real constant, what is the value of $C?$\n\n$C=$\n{{field-1}}" "Given that $\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}Cf(x)=8,$ where $C$ is a real constant, what is the value of $C?$\n\n$C=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186552464 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x).$ If a limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x)=$\n{{field-2}}" "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x)$ and $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x).$ If a limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x)=\n$$\n{{field-2}}"] [:db/cas 17592186552464 :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $1$ from the left, the function $g(x)$ approaches $1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x)=1.\n$$\n\nLikewise, as $x$ approaches $1$ from the right, the function $g(x)$ approaches $0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x)=1,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$" "As $x$ approaches $1$ from the left, the function $g(x)$ approaches $1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x)=1.\n$$\n\nLikewise, as $x$ approaches $1$ from the right, the function $g(x)$ approaches $0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x)=1,\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186552495 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}g(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}g(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186552495 :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we see that as we approach $x=-1$ from the left, the $y$-value approaches $1.$ So the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)=1.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, when we approach $x=-1$ from the right, the $y$-value again approaches $1.$ So the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x)=1.\n$$\n\nSince both the left and right-hand limits exist and are equal to $1,$ we conclude that\n\n \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x)=1.\n$$" "From the graph, we see that as we approach $x=-1$ from the left, the $y$-value approaches $1.$ So the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)=1.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, when we approach $x=-1$ from the right, the $y$-value again approaches $1.$ So the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x)=1.\n$$\n\nSince both the left and right-hand limits exist and are equal to $1,$ we conclude that\n\n \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x)=1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186552511 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{1-{x}^{3}}=${{field-1}}" "$\\lim_{x\\to -1}\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{1-{x}^{3}}=${{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186552519 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}g(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}g(x)=$ \n{{field-1}}" "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}g(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}g(x)=\n$$ \n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186552519 :question/worked-solution "In part 1, we showed that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x)=1,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$\n\nSince ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x),$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}g(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$" "In part 1, we showed that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x)=1,\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$\n\nSince $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x),$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}g(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186552523 :question/problem "![](images/ea/ea804b5eb5c4af6aace19a66008dfbf9b130f1e1e38c2d8d9d811e9cad4c413c.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{({x}^{2}-1)}{f(x)}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above." "![](images/ea/ea804b5eb5c4af6aace19a66008dfbf9b130f1e1e38c2d8d9d811e9cad4c413c.png)\n\nFind $\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{({x}^{2}-1)}{f(x)}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above."] [:db/cas 17592186552523 :question/worked-solution "We find from the graph that ${lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)$ does not exist, therefore\n \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{({x}^{2}-1)}{f(x)}=\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 0}({x}^{2}-1)}{{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)}=\\text{DNE}.\n$$" "We find from the graph that $\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)$ does not exist, therefore\n \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{({x}^{2}-1)}{f(x)}=\\frac{\\lim_{x\\to 0}({x}^{2}-1)}{\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)}=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186558095 :answer/value "{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{-7(x+h)^{3}+7{x}^{3}}{h}\\right)" "\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{-7(x+h)^{3}+7{x}^{3}}{h}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186558137 :answer/value "{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{-7(x+h)^{3}-{