hat is $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(p(x)\\cdot q(x))?$"] [:db/cas 17592186563647 :question/problem "Find $f(5)$ given that  $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are two continuous vector-valued functions for which   $g(5)=⟨-1,1,-2⟩$ and \n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 5}[f(t)+3g(t)]=⟨1,3,1⟩\\,.\n$$" "Find $f(5)$ given that  $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are two continuous vector-valued functions for which   $g(5)=⟨-1,1,-2⟩$ and \n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to 5}[f(t)+3g(t)]=⟨1,3,1⟩\\,.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563652 :question/problem "Given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are two continuous vector-valued functions for which $f(-7)=⟨6,0,-2⟩$ and $g(-7)=⟨2,-1,0⟩,$ determine\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to -7}[\\frac{f(t)}{2}+3g(t)].\n$$" "Given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are two continuous vector-valued functions for which $f(-7)=⟨6,0,-2⟩$ and $g(-7)=⟨2,-1,0⟩,$ determine\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to -7}[\\frac{f(t)}{2}+3g(t)].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563660 :question/problem "Find the **exact** value of the limit.\n\n${lim}_{t\\to -1}(\\sqrt[7]{(2t+1)^{7}}-\\sqrt[3]{({t}^{2}+t+1)^{6}})=$\n{{field-1}}" "Find the **exact** value of the limit.\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to -1}(\\sqrt[7]{(2t+1)^{7}}-\\sqrt[3]{({t}^{2}+t+1)^{6}})=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186563661 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{t\\to 2}f(t),$ if $f(t)=⟨{e}^{t-2},\\,\\sqrt{6-t},\\,\\frac{3}{t+2}⟩.$" "Calculate $\\lim_{t\\to 2}f(t),$ if $f(t)=⟨{e}^{t-2},\\,\\sqrt{6-t},\\,\\frac{3}{t+2}⟩.$"] [:db/cas 17592186563662 :question/problem "Find the limit ${lim}_{t\\to 1}‖f(t)‖$, if $$\n\\displaystyle f(t)=\\frac{4+t}{5}\\,i+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1)}{{t}^{2}-1}\\,j+\\sqrt{9t}\\,k.\n$$" "Find the limit $\\lim_{t\\to 1}‖f(t)‖$, if $$\n\\displaystyle f(t)=\\frac{4+t}{5}\\,i+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1)}{{t}^{2}-1}\\,j+\\sqrt{9t}\\,k.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563676 :question/problem "![](images/94/94027c25ef78ae46d0deb4b577339daf878efd71b71029a9575f9c8ed9096e2c.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of the function $f(x),$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(x-1)^{2}\\,(f(x)-2)^{3}=$\n{{field-1}}" "![](images/94/94027c25ef78ae46d0deb4b577339daf878efd71b71029a9575f9c8ed9096e2c.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of the function $f(x),$ then\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}(x-1)^{2}\\,(f(x)-2)^{3}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186563690 :question/problem "Find $f(0)$ given that  $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are two continuous vector-valued functions for which   $g(0)=⟨-1,0,1⟩$ and \n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)-g(t)]=⟨-2,-3,0⟩\\,.\n$$" "Find $f(0)$ given that  $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are two continuous vector-valued functions for which   $g(0)=⟨-1,0,1⟩$ and \n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to 0}[f(t)-g(t)]=⟨-2,-3,0⟩\\,.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563691 :question/problem "Find $f(0)$ given that $f(t)$ is a continuous vector-valued function and $u(t)$ is continuous real-valued function for which $u(0)=3$ and \n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 0}[u(t)f(t)]=⟨-18,-21,6⟩\\,.\n$$" "Find $f(0)$ given that $f(t)$ is a continuous vector-valued function and $u(t)$ is continuous real-valued function for which $u(0)=3$ and \n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to 0}[u(t)f(t)]=⟨-18,-21,6⟩\\,.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186563714 :question/problem "Find the limit ${lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t),$ if $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+(t+2)^{2}\\,k.$" "Find the limit $\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t),$ if $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+(t+2)^{2}\\,k.$"] [:db/cas 17592186563738 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{s\\to 2}f(s)$, if $f(s)=⟨\\frac{5}{{e}^{s-2}},\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4-2s),\\,{s}^{3}-5⟩.$" "Calculate $\\lim_{s\\to 2}f(s)$, if $f(s)=⟨\\frac{5}{{e}^{s-2}},\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4-2s),\\,{s}^{3}-5⟩.$"] [:db/cas 17592186563742 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{3x}{\\sqrt[3]{2{x}^{4}-5}}.$" "Find $\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{3x}{\\sqrt[3]{2{x}^{4}-5}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186563752 :question/problem "Find the limit $\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}‖f(t)‖,$ if $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+(t+2)^{2}\\,k.$" "Find the limit $\\,\\lim_{t\\to 0}‖f(t)‖,$ if $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+(t+2)^{2}\\,k.$"] [:db/cas 17592186564471 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{log}_{3}⁡(-9x)=$" "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{log}_{3}⁡(-9x)=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186564471 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{3}⁡(-9x).$\n\n![](images/41/41378635430091e24674e22dbc45105bedd7af55410ba6069057f7a37b2025fc.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ decreases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{log}_{3}⁡(-9x)=\\infty \n$$" "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{3}⁡(-9x).$\n\n![](images/41/41378635430091e24674e22dbc45105bedd7af55410ba6069057f7a37b2025fc.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ decreases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{log}_{3}⁡(-9x)=\\infty \n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186564474 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}{log}_{2}⁡(x+2).$" "Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to (-2)}{log}_{2}⁡(x+2).$"] [:db/cas 17592186564474 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{2}⁡(x+2).$\n\n![](images/5c/5c4b062938cc4e3d64f11f9ad3bd8da9431581e9a1b0278a977a64c0d727c73d.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\le -2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}{log}_{2}⁡(x+2)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x>-2.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}{log}_{2}⁡(x+2)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are not equal, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -2}{log}_{2}⁡(x+2)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$" "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{2}⁡(x+2).$\n\n![](images/5c/5c4b062938cc4e3d64f11f9ad3bd8da9431581e9a1b0278a977a64c0d727c73d.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\le -2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}{log}_{2}⁡(x+2)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x>-2.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}{log}_{2}⁡(x+2)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are not equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -2}{log}_{2}⁡(x+2)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186564476 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}{log}_{3}⁡(4-2x)=$" "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}{log}_{3}⁡(4-2x)=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186564476 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{3}⁡(4-2x).$\n\n![](images/b1/b1d65d2df4e6a5a6b66e908ed8f776e2a1b8a4b24896506d3625e6569d9ea4e6.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\ge 2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}{log}_{3}⁡(4-2x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x<2.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}{log}_{3}⁡(4-2x)=-\\infty .\n$$" "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{3}⁡(4-2x).$\n\n![](images/b1/b1d65d2df4e6a5a6b66e908ed8f776e2a1b8a4b24896506d3625e6569d9ea4e6.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\ge 2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}{log}_{3}⁡(4-2x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x<2.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}{log}_{3}⁡(4-2x)=-\\infty .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186564478 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to -3}{log}_{7}⁡(x+3).$" "Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to -3}{log}_{7}⁡(x+3).$"] [:db/cas 17592186564478 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{7}⁡(x+3).$\n\n![](images/0b/0b45cd648830ad88c2f21865c1bfd704783a7b5d9ce3510646ae55e98d2d95ac.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\le -3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}{log}_{7}⁡(x+3)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x>-3.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}{log}_{7}⁡(x+3)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are not equal, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -3}{log}_{7}⁡(x+3)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$" "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{7}⁡(x+3).$\n\n![](images/0b/0b45cd648830ad88c2f21865c1bfd704783a7b5d9ce3510646ae55e98d2d95ac.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\le -3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}{log}_{7}⁡(x+3)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x>-3.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}{log}_{7}⁡(x+3)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are not equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -3}{log}_{7}⁡(x+3)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186564486 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 4}{log}_{2}⁡(4x)=$" "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 4}{log}_{2}⁡(4x)=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186564509 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6-2x)=$" "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6-2x)=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186564509 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6-2x).$\n\n![](images/bb/bba697be09903e54cffc4bf0c0e05cbd30cbfc11975cac5097e964415baca113.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x>3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6-2x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x<3.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6-2x)=-\\infty .\n$$" "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6-2x).$\n\n![](images/bb/bba697be09903e54cffc4bf0c0e05cbd30cbfc11975cac5097e964415baca113.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x>3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6-2x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x<3.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6-2x)=-\\infty .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186564521 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{log}_{5}⁡(x+1)=$" "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{log}_{5}⁡(x+1)=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186564521 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{log}⁡(x+1).$\n\n![](images/90/902c8394181aefb301c901b3792e89e909ba36ecba3a9b8113fc42b80fd08870.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{log}_{5}⁡(x+1)=\\infty \n$$" "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{log}⁡(x+1).$\n\n![](images/90/902c8394181aefb301c901b3792e89e909ba36ecba3a9b8113fc42b80fd08870.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{log}_{5}⁡(x+1)=\\infty \n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186564525 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x+8)}{2-x}=$\n\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x+8)}{2-x}=\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186565409 :question/problem "Compute ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\left(-\\frac{9}{5{x}^{8}}\\right).$" "Compute $\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\left(-\\frac{9}{5{x}^{8}}\\right).$"] [:db/cas 17592186565409 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\left(-\\frac{9}{5{x}^{8}}\\right)=-\\frac{9}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{8}}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall the graph of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{8}}:$\n\n![](images/ae/aeeed013b57f5fd8a554ca6613bf9e3fa9419ae2e0fd5920397c5b9cb0dd88fb.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{8}}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nFinally, the negative factor of $-\\frac{9}{5}$ changes the sign of the limit. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n-\\frac{9}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{8}}=-\\infty .\n$$" "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\left(-\\frac{9}{5{x}^{8}}\\right)=-\\frac{9}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{8}}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall the graph of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{8}}:$\n\n![](images/ae/aeeed013b57f5fd8a554ca6613bf9e3fa9419ae2e0fd5920397c5b9cb0dd88fb.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{8}}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nFinally, the negative factor of $-\\frac{9}{5}$ changes the sign of the limit. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n-\\frac{9}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{8}}=-\\infty .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186565411 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{-3}{{x}^{8}}\\right)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{-3}{{x}^{8}}\\right)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186565412 :question/problem "![](images/64/6469991ee6751110f55ce6462c4c3f2e406d8b61fabcb6c2d2cc1515ec12dd2c.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of $g(x),$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{g(x)}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-7}=$\n\n {{field-1}}." "![](images/64/6469991ee6751110f55ce6462c4c3f2e406d8b61fabcb6c2d2cc1515ec12dd2c.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of $g(x),$ then\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{g(x)}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-7}=$\n\n {{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186565415 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{7}{3x}=$" "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{7}{3x}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186565422 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186565422 :question/worked-solution "We recall the graph of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}:$\n\n![](images/ff/ffedd1b83fa2d80b924b0d2f68a3100236347e3222ec171bc60b299579a7af4e.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}=\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}=\\infty .\n$$" "We recall the graph of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}:$\n\n![](images/ff/ffedd1b83fa2d80b924b0d2f68a3100236347e3222ec171bc60b299579a7af4e.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}=\\infty ,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}=\\infty .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186565424 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to (3,1,2)}\\,\\frac{y-x+z}{\\sqrt{x-z}-\\sqrt{y}}=$" "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,2)}\\,\\frac{y-x+z}{\\sqrt{x-z}-\\sqrt{y}}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186565425 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}=$" "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186565432 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{xy}{2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}}=$" "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{xy}{2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186565438 :question/problem "Determine ${lim}_{x\\to (-2,0)}(2x-y+3).$" "Determine $\\lim_{x\\to (-2,0)}(2x-y+3).$"] [:db/cas 17592186565443 :question/problem "Compute ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\,\\frac{9}{12{x}^{4}}.$" "Compute $\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\,\\frac{9}{12{x}^{4}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186565444 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-4}{({x}^{2}-3)^{3}}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-4}{({x}^{2}-3)^{3}}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186565444 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite our limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-4}{({x}^{2}-3)^{3}}=-4\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}-3)^{3}}\n$$\n\nFor any non-constant polynomial $P(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\pm \\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{P(x)}\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $({x}^{2}-3)^{3}$ is a non-constant polynomial, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}-3)^{3}}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the original limit as follows:\n\n$$\n-4\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}-3)^{3}}=-4\\cdot 0=0\n$$" "First, we rewrite our limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-4}{({x}^{2}-3)^{3}}=-4\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}-3)^{3}}\n$$\n\nFor any non-constant polynomial $P(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\pm \\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{P(x)}\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $({x}^{2}-3)^{3}$ is a non-constant polynomial, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}-3)^{3}}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the original limit as follows:\n\n$$\n-4\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}-3)^{3}}=-4\\cdot 0=0\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186565447 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}\\left(-\\frac{9}{4(x-5)^{6}}\\right)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {5}^{-}}\\left(-\\frac{9}{4(x-5)^{6}}\\right)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186565447 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}\\left(-\\frac{9}{4(x-5)^{6}}\\right)\\,=\\,-\\frac{9}{4}\\,\\cdot \\,{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}\\frac{1}{(x-5)^{6}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{(x-5)^{6}}$ as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}.$\n\n2\\. Translate it to the right by $5$ units. This gives $y=\\frac{1}{(x-5)^{6}},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/57/574b270248f2acc2a2eb3403f5626e420f5f16abc6e9e01dcb147df83e5806b5.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}\\frac{1}{(x-5)^{6}}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nFinally, the negative factor of $-\\frac{9}{4}$ changes the sign of the limit. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n-\\frac{9}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}\\frac{1}{(x-5)^{6}}=-\\infty .\n$$" "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {5}^{-}}\\left(-\\frac{9}{4(x-5)^{6}}\\right)\\,=\\,-\\frac{9}{4}\\,\\cdot \\,\\lim_{x\\to {5}^{-}}\\frac{1}{(x-5)^{6}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{(x-5)^{6}}$ as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}.$\n\n2\\. Translate it to the right by $5$ units. This gives $y=\\frac{1}{(x-5)^{6}},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/57/574b270248f2acc2a2eb3403f5626e420f5f16abc6e9e01dcb147df83e5806b5.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {5}^{-}}\\frac{1}{(x-5)^{6}}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nFinally, the negative factor of $-\\frac{9}{4}$ changes the sign of the limit. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n-\\frac{9}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}\\frac{1}{(x-5)^{6}}=-\\infty .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186565463 :question/problem "Determine ${lim}_{x\\to (9,1,3)}\\frac{x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2}}{\\sqrt{x}-yz}.$" "Determine $\\lim_{x\\to (9,1,3)}\\frac{x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2}}{\\sqrt{x}-yz}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186565476 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\left(\\frac{7}{9{x}^{7}}\\right)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\left(\\frac{7}{9{x}^{7}}\\right)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186565484 :question/problem "![](images/d7/d7f1d5de28c6479a2e9b8b33a6da25a4091b71b58f6dc0c535ef390c4cb97799.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of $g(x),$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{g(x)}{{x}^{2}}=$\n {{field-1}}." "![](images/d7/d7f1d5de28c6479a2e9b8b33a6da25a4091b71b58f6dc0c535ef390c4cb97799.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of $g(x),$ then\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{g(x)}{{x}^{2}}=$\n {{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186565485 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(-\\frac{7}{{x}^{5}}\\right)=$" "$\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(-\\frac{7}{{x}^{5}}\\right)=$"] [:db/cas 17592186569964 :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta$ for $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\infty$\n\nIII. The curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$" "Consider the polar curve $r={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta$ for $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0$\n\nII. $\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\infty$\n\nIII. The curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$"] [:db/cas 17592186569981 :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{3}⁡\\theta$ for $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )={cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,(5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta (5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3),\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0$\n\nIII. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n*Hint: It is not necessary to use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*" "Consider the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{3}⁡\\theta$ for $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )={cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,(5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta (5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3),\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0$\n\nII. $\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0$\n\nIII. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n*Hint: It is not necessary to use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*"] [:db/cas 17592186570016 :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r=4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{3}⁡\\theta$ for $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,(3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=3{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)$ is undefined\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0$\n\nIII. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n*Hint: It is not necessary to use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*" "Consider the polar curve $r=4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{3}⁡\\theta$ for $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,(3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=3{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)$ is undefined\n\nII. $\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0$\n\nIII. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n*Hint: It is not necessary to use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*"] [:db/cas 17592186570042 :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta$ for $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0$\n\nIII. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n*Hint: It is not necessary to use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*" "Consider the polar curve $r={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta$ for $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0$\n\nII. $\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0$\n\nIII. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n*Hint: It is not necessary to use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*"] [:db/cas 17592186570149 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)=2" "\\lim_{x\\to -1}f(x)=2"] [:db/cas 17592186570160 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)=-1" "\\lim_{x\\to -1}f(x)=-1"] [:db/cas 17592186570163 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to -2}f(x)=-1" "\\lim_{x\\to -2}f(x)=-1"] [:db/cas 17592186570177 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)=\\infty" "\\lim_{x\\to -1}f(x)=\\infty"] [:db/cas 17592186570257 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to -2}f(x)=\\infty" "\\lim_{x\\to -2}f(x)=\\infty"] [:db/cas 17592186570360 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to -2}f(x)=-2" "\\lim_{x\\to -2}f(x)=-2"] [:db/cas 17592186570364 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" "\\lim_{x\\to -1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}"] [:db/cas 17592186570542 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to -2}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" "\\lim_{x\\to -2}f(x)=\\text{DNE}"] [:db/cas 17592186570544 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=-\\infty" "\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)=-\\infty"] [:db/cas 17592186570573 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)=-\\infty" "\\lim_{x\\to -1}f(x)=-\\infty"] [:db/cas 17592186570702 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}\\frac{8-4\\sqrt{x}}{x-4}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}\\frac{8-4\\sqrt{x}}{x-4}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186570720 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{x-1}{\\sqrt{3x+2}-\\sqrt{4x+1}}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{x-1}{\\sqrt{3x+2}-\\sqrt{4x+1}}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186570736 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{3-\\sqrt{3x}}{x-3}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{3-\\sqrt{3x}}{x-3}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186570740 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1).$" "Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1).$"] [:db/cas 17592186570740 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1).$\n\n![](images/52/52817b9c0ede04e8e920de8a52ebe025916f314cc937097c7fb91ea612e18f1b.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\le -1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x>-1.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are not equal, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$" "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1).$\n\n![](images/52/52817b9c0ede04e8e920de8a52ebe025916f314cc937097c7fb91ea612e18f1b.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\le -1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x>-1.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are not equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186570745 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-1}{x-1}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-1}{x-1}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186570765 :question/problem "![](images/cc/cc2ea14272a30271bb022e9deb5e8b506ed0c2725d6633e7308b3786cb09d84c.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x).$" "![](images/cc/cc2ea14272a30271bb022e9deb5e8b506ed0c2725d6633e7308b3786cb09d84c.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x).$"] [:db/cas 17592186570765 :question/worked-solution "We see from the graph that as $x$ approaches $-1$ from the left, the function $f(x)$ approaches $-\\infty .$ So, the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty \\,.\n$$\n\nLikewise, as $x$ approaches $-1$ from the right, the function $f(x)$ again approaches $-\\infty .$ So, the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty \\,.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)=-\\infty \\,.\n$$" "We see from the graph that as $x$ approaches $-1$ from the left, the function $f(x)$ approaches $-\\infty .$ So, the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty \\,.\n$$\n\nLikewise, as $x$ approaches $-1$ from the right, the function $f(x)$ again approaches $-\\infty .$ So, the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty \\,.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -1}f(x)=-\\infty \\,.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186570767 :question/problem "![](images/8c/8c0a344d98dc0ca3a6e4c0fdc3c98074bc2de23301300e663a02ea6f4dff3035.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Therefore\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,6}f(x)=$\n\n{{field-1}}." "![](images/8c/8c0a344d98dc0ca3a6e4c0fdc3c98074bc2de23301300e663a02ea6f4dff3035.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,6}f(x)=\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186570767 :question/worked-solution "We see from the graph that as $x$ approaches $6$ from the left, the function $f(x)$ approaches $\\infty .$ So, the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {6}^{-}}f(x)=\\infty \\,.\n$$\n\nLikewise, as $x$ approaches $6$ from the right, the function $f(x)$ again approaches $\\infty .$ So, the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {6}^{+}}f(x)=\\infty \\,.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 6}f(x)=\\infty \\,.\n$$" "We see from the graph that as $x$ approaches $6$ from the left, the function $f(x)$ approaches $\\infty .$ So, the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {6}^{-}}f(x)=\\infty \\,.\n$$\n\nLikewise, as $x$ approaches $6$ from the right, the function $f(x)$ again approaches $\\infty .$ So, the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {6}^{+}}f(x)=\\infty \\,.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 6}f(x)=\\infty \\,.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186570768 :question/problem "![](images/84/84490ed5dee30eb93ae322d0d4ca20109672a80083bf9eabb252ae49c7501036.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\,5}f(x).$" "![](images/84/84490ed5dee30eb93ae322d0d4ca20109672a80083bf9eabb252ae49c7501036.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\,5}f(x).$"] [:db/cas 17592186570768 :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $5$ from the left, the function value $f(x)$ goes to $-\\infty$, and therefore\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is not defined for $x>5,$ and consequently, we cannot approach $x=5$ from the right. So we have \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n  \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 5}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$" "As $x$ approaches $5$ from the left, the function value $f(x)$ goes to $-\\infty$, and therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {5}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is not defined for $x>5,$ and consequently, we cannot approach $x=5$ from the right. So we have \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {5}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n  \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 5}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186570771 :question/problem "![](images/d1/d1e5821252dd0634c6033a35247af033bcda8a96fa3733b65be52565a60ea3a6.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\,-2}f(x).$" "![](images/d1/d1e5821252dd0634c6033a35247af033bcda8a96fa3733b65be52565a60ea3a6.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\,-2}f(x).$"] [:db/cas 17592186570771 :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $-2$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ grows to infinity, and therefore\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}f(x)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHowever, as $x$ approaches $-2$ from the left, the function value $f(x)$ goes to $-\\infty$, and therefore\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -2}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$" "As $x$ approaches $-2$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ grows to infinity, and therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}f(x)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHowever, as $x$ approaches $-2$ from the left, the function value $f(x)$ goes to $-\\infty$, and therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -2}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186572110 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}\\frac{1}{x-3}=$" "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}\\frac{1}{x-3}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186572110 :question/worked-solution "We can sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{x-3}$ as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n2\\. Translate it to the right by $3$ units. This gives $y=\\frac{1}{x-3},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/e3/e3308ad7f61359df7fb4e1b0f32f1d373c703be34b353ffa100ae57c6c115e0b.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}\\frac{1}{x-3}=\\infty ,\n$$" "We can sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{x-3}$ as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n2\\. Translate it to the right by $3$ units. This gives $y=\\frac{1}{x-3},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/e3/e3308ad7f61359df7fb4e1b0f32f1d373c703be34b353ffa100ae57c6c115e0b.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}\\frac{1}{x-3}=\\infty ,\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186572168 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{7}{4{(x-2)}^{3}}\\,=$\n {{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{7}{4{(x-2)}^{3}}\\,=\n$$\n {{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186572168 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite our limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{7}{4{(x-2)}^{3}}=\\frac{7}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{(x-2)}^{3}}\n$$\n\nFor any non-constant polynomial $P(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\pm \\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{P(x)}\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${(x-2)}^{3}$ is a non-constant polynomial, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{3}}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the original limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{(x-2)}^{3}}=\\frac{7}{4}\\cdot 0=0\n$$" "First, we rewrite our limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{7}{4{(x-2)}^{3}}=\\frac{7}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{(x-2)}^{3}}\n$$\n\nFor any non-constant polynomial $P(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\pm \\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{P(x)}\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${(x-2)}^{3}$ is a non-constant polynomial, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{3}}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the original limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{(x-2)}^{3}}=\\frac{7}{4}\\cdot 0=0\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186572200 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-12}{9{(x+5)}^{4}}\\,=$\n {{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-12}{9{(x+5)}^{4}}\\,=\n$$\n {{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186572200 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite our limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-12}{9{(x+5)}^{4}}=\\frac{-12}{9}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{(x+5)}^{4}}=-\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{(x+5)}^{4}}\n$$\n\nFor any non-constant polynomial $P(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\pm \\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{P(x)}\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${(x+5)}^{4}$ is a non-constant polynomial, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{(x+5)^{4}}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the original limit as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{(x+5)}^{4}}=-\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot 0=0\n$$" "First, we rewrite our limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-12}{9{(x+5)}^{4}}=\\frac{-12}{9}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{(x+5)}^{4}}=-\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{(x+5)}^{4}}\n$$\n\nFor any non-constant polynomial $P(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\pm \\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{P(x)}\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${(x+5)}^{4}$ is a non-constant polynomial, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{(x+5)^{4}}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the original limit as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{(x+5)}^{4}}=-\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot 0=0\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186572665 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x-3}{{x}^{2}-1}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to -1}\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x-3}{{x}^{2}-1}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186572697 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{y\\to 3}\\frac{{y}^{3}-9y}{{y}^{2}-5y+6}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{y\\to 3}\\frac{{y}^{3}-9y}{{y}^{2}-5y+6}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186572728 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{9{x}^{3}-4x}{3{x}^{2}+2x}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{9{x}^{3}-4x}{3{x}^{2}+2x}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186573256 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{n\\to 0}{(1+n)}^{4/7n}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to 0}{(1+n)}^{4/7n}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186573260 :question/problem "Find the value of $a$ given that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{a+h}-\\frac{1}{a})}{h}=-\\frac{1}{4}\\,.\n$$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" "Find the value of $a$ given that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{a+h}-\\frac{1}{a})}{h}=-\\frac{1}{4}\\,.\n$$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186573268 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}}cos\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x))=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}}cos\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x))=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186573868 :question/problem "If $r(x)=-2{x}^{8}$ and $s(x)=\\frac{11}{{x}^{10}}$, then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{2}\\cdot r(x)\\cdot s(x))=$  \n{{field-1}}." "If $r(x)=-2{x}^{8}$ and $s(x)=\\frac{11}{{x}^{10}}$, then\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{2}\\cdot 