e\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)=2=f(1).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=1$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186583631 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi }f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi }f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186583635 :question/problem "![](images/dc/dce065da5fb6e0ebb09537b27e108801d61c80c4905dcc8ae54f169dfdbd4f0f.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $g(h)$ is shown above, where\n\n$$\ng(h)=\\frac{4}{h}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{h}{2}\\right)-1).\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, what is ${lim}_{h\\to \\,0}g(h)?$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{h\\to \\,0}g(h)=$\n{{field-1}}" "![](images/dc/dce065da5fb6e0ebb09537b27e108801d61c80c4905dcc8ae54f169dfdbd4f0f.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $g(h)$ is shown above, where\n\n$$\ng(h)=\\frac{4}{h}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{h}{2}\\right)-1).\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, what is $\\lim_{h\\to \\,0}g(h)?$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h\\to \\,0}g(h)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186583635 :question/worked-solution "The graph shows that as we approach $h=0$ from the left, the $y$ value approaches $0.$ So, the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{h\\to \\,{0}^{-}}g(h)=0.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, when we approach $h=0$ from the right, the $y$ value approaches $0.$ So, the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{h\\to \\,{0}^{+}}g(h)=0.\n$$\n\nSince both the left and right-hand limits exist and are equal to $0,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{h\\to \\,0}g(h)=0.\n$$" "The graph shows that as we approach $h=0$ from the left, the $y$ value approaches $0.$ So, the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h\\to \\,{0}^{-}}g(h)=0.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, when we approach $h=0$ from the right, the $y$ value approaches $0.$ So, the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h\\to \\,{0}^{+}}g(h)=0.\n$$\n\nSince both the left and right-hand limits exist and are equal to $0,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h\\to \\,0}g(h)=0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186583642 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186583642 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=f(x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right).$\n\n![](images/44/446f0fd704e09106f34a70fcbd3adb2806bfea13664114bd5d704fd3c8519001.png)\n\nAs $x$ decreases to $-\\infty$, the graph oscillates between the values $y=-4$ and $y=4$ indefinitely. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)=DNE.\n$$" "Let's sketch the graph of $y=f(x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right).$\n\n![](images/44/446f0fd704e09106f34a70fcbd3adb2806bfea13664114bd5d704fd3c8519001.png)\n\nAs $x$ decreases to $-\\infty$, the graph oscillates between the values $y=-4$ and $y=4$ indefinitely. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)=DNE.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186585049 :question/problem "The financial director predicts that, in the future, the profit will have an overall behavior similar to the function $\\sqrt[3]{4x-4}.$ Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[3]{4x-4}.$\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[3]{4x-4}=$\n{{field-1}}" "The financial director predicts that, in the future, the profit will have an overall behavior similar to the function $\\sqrt[3]{4x-4}.$ Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[3]{4x-4}.$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[3]{4x-4}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186585049 :question/worked-solution "Let $k(x)=\\sqrt[3]{4x-4}.$ Let's sketch the graph of $y=k(x):$\n\n![](images/c5/c5216901041a8042a8ec3b6d444c37ab95488414715dbca923c008ef3ccc0bf0.png)\n\nThe graph shows that the function increases without bound as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }k(x)=\\infty .\n$$" "Let $k(x)=\\sqrt[3]{4x-4}.$ Let's sketch the graph of $y=k(x):$\n\n![](images/c5/c5216901041a8042a8ec3b6d444c37ab95488414715dbca923c008ef3ccc0bf0.png)\n\nThe graph shows that the function increases without bound as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }k(x)=\\infty .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186587299 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{s\\to 1}||f(s)||$, if $$\n\\displaystyle f(s)=⟨\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{s}^{2}}{s-1},\\frac{6s-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(s-1)}{3s-2},\\frac{{s}^{3}+4s-2}{{s}^{2}+s-1}⟩.\n$$" "Calculate $\\lim_{s\\to 1}||f(s)||$, if $$\n\\displaystyle f(s)=⟨\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{s}^{2}}{s-1},\\frac{6s-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(s-1)}{3s-2},\\frac{{s}^{3}+4s-2}{{s}^{2}+s-1}⟩.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186587308 :question/problem "The economist predicts that, in the future, the inflation will have an overall behavior similar to the function $\\sqrt[3]{x-4}.$ Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[3]{x-4}.$\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[3]{x-4}=$\n{{field-1}}" "The economist predicts that, in the future, the inflation will have an overall behavior similar to the function $\\sqrt[3]{x-4}.$ Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[3]{x-4}.$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[3]{x-4}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186587308 :question/worked-solution "Let $k(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x-4}.$ Let's sketch the graph of $y=k(x):$\n\n![](images/22/22d381f28b74d851369a40ef1c1f5bb6ef7fb22c11a7afb0145587480c0a7ccb.png)\n\nThe graph shows that the function increases without bound as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }k(x)=\\infty .\n$$" "Let $k(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x-4}.$ Let's sketch the grap