 ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)$ is undefined\n\nII. $\\lim_{\\theta \\to 3\\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0$\n\nIII. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$\n\n*Hint: It is not necessary to use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*"] [:db/cas 17592186591667 :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )$ for $$\n\\displaystyle \\theta \\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+1),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}(0)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}(0)=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\infty$\n\nIII. The curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta =0$" "Consider the polar curve $r=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )$ for $$\n\\displaystyle \\theta \\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+1),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}(0)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}(0)=0$\n\nII. $\\lim_{\\theta \\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\infty$\n\nIII. The curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta =0$"] [:db/cas 17592186591718 :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta$ for $\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}(\\theta )=0$ at $\\theta =0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}(0)=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0$\n\nIII. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =0$\n\n*Hint: Use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*" "Consider the polar curve $r=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta$ for $\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}(\\theta )=0$ at $\\theta =0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}(0)=0$\n\nII. $\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0$\n\nIII. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =0$\n\n*Hint: Use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*"] [:db/cas 17592186592281 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 16}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-4}{x-16}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to 16}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-4}{x-16}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186593386 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=0" "\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=0"] [:db/cas 17592186593431 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" "\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=\\text{DNE}"] [:db/cas 17592186593507 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" "\\lim_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=\\text{DNE}"] [:db/cas 17592186593526 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,-3}f(x)=-1" "\\lim_{x\\to \\,-3}f(x)=-1"] [:db/cas 17592186593696 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=1" "\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=1"] [:db/cas 17592186593699 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,-3}f(x)=-2" "\\lim_{x\\to \\,-3}f(x)=-2"] [:db/cas 17592186593715 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=0" "\\lim_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=0"] [:db/cas 17592186593719 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,-3}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" "\\lim_{x\\to \\,-3}f(x)=\\text{DNE}"] [:db/cas 17592186593832 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,-3}f(x)=2" "\\lim_{x\\to \\,-3}f(x)=2"] [:db/cas 17592186593855 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=-2" "\\lim_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=-2"] [:db/cas 17592186593873 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=2" "\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=2"] [:db/cas 17592186593890 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=-1" "\\lim_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=-1"] [:db/cas 17592186593915 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=1" "\\lim_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=1"] [:db/cas 17592186593939 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,-3}f(x)=1" "\\lim_{x\\to \\,-3}f(x)=1"] [:db/cas 17592186593954 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=-2" "\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=-2"] [:db/cas 17592186594152 :question/problem "![](images/c5/c5e03bbe06b44f27a9573e9dbfb8efbf0f1f1ee8efe6e8093dfbe64bb431d790.png)\n\nThe function  $y=f(x)$ is defined on $x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$ Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x).$" "![](images/c5/c5e03bbe06b44f27a9573e9dbfb8efbf0f1f1ee8efe6e8093dfbe64bb431d790.png)\n\nThe function  $y=f(x)$ is defined on $x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$ Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}f(x).$"] [:db/cas 17592186594152 :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we see that it is not possible to approach $x=0$ from the right. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nWhen we approach $x=0$ from the left, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=0.\n$$\n\nSince ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x),$ we  conclude  that\n \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$" "From the graph, we see that it is not possible to approach $x=0$ from the right. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nWhen we approach $x=0$ from the left, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=0.\n$$\n\nSince $\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x),$ we  conclude  that\n \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186594171 :question/problem "![](images/f9/f98551299f73f55e9c1a07ef513723f068f66576a514d1895c07f317c10ae262.png)\n\nThe function  $y=f(x)$ is defined on $x\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup (0,\\infty ).$ Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x).$" "![](images/f9/f98551299f73f55e9c1a07ef513723f068f66576a514d1895c07f317c10ae262.png)\n\nThe function  $y=f(x)$ is defined on $x\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup (0,\\infty ).$ Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x).$"] [:db/cas 17592186594171 :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we see that it is not possible to approach $x=-2$ from the right. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nWhen we approach $x=-2$ from the left, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{-}}f(x)=-1.\n$$\n\nSince ${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}f(x),$ we  conclude  that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$" "From the graph, we see that it is not possible to approach $x=-2$ from the right. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nWhen we approach $x=-2$ from the left, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{-}}f(x)=-1.\n$$\n\nSince $\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}f(x),$ we  conclude  that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186594187 :question/problem "![](images/88/884ec8c4d7e079915b7b6a21a2008514bc201580b447dcfc0f9c4007e60d00dc.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above? \n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=-2$\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=-1$" "![](images/88/884ec8c4d7e079915b7b6a21a2008514bc201580b447dcfc0f9c4007e60d00dc.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above? \n\nI. $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=-2$\n\nII. $\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$\n\nIII. $\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=-1$"] [:db/cas 17592186594187 :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We see from the graph that \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x)=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=-2.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Both limits ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)$ do not exist because the function $f(x)$ is not given in the interval $[0,2).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ approaches $2$ from the left and right we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x).$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true." "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We see from the graph that \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x)=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=-2.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Both limits $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)$ and $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)$ do not exist because the function $f(x)$ is not given in the interval $[0,2).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ approaches $2$ from the left and right we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x).$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true."] [:db/cas 17592186594197 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{3{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}-2}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{3{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}-2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186594197 :question/worked-solution "For any non-constant polynomial $P(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\pm \\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{P(x)}\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $3{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}-2$ is a non-constant polynomial, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{3{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}-2}=0.\n$$" "For any non-constant polynomial $P(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\pm \\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{P(x)}\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $3{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}-2$ is a non-constant polynomial, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{3{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}-2}=0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186594219 :question/problem "![](images/d6/d651c43faf3d85411e3aefa905278ff64e45b178f5625a0b92c7c89c3b5eb66f.png)\n\nThe function  $y=f(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-3,2)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$ Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-3}f(x).$" "![](images/d6/d651c43faf3d85411e3aefa905278ff64e45b178f5625a0b92c7c89c3b5eb66f.png)\n\nThe function  $y=f(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-3,2)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$ Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-3}f(x).$"] [:db/cas 17592186594219 :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we see that it is not possible to approach $x=-3$ from the left. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{3}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, when we approach $x=-3$ from the right, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{3}^{+}}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\nSince ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{3}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,-{3}^{+}}f(x),$ we  conclude  that\n \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-3}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$" "From the graph, we see that it is not possible to approach $x=-3$ from the left. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{3}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, when we approach $x=-3$ from the right, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{3}^{+}}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\nSince $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{3}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,-{3}^{+}}f(x),$ we  conclude  that\n \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-3}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186594395 :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=2$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {(\\pi /2)}^{+}}f(x)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$" "$\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=2$ and $\\lim_{x\\to {(\\pi /2)}^{+}}f(x)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$"] [:db/cas 17592186594411 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {(\\pi /2)}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" "\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {(\\pi /2)}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}"] [:db/cas 17592186594437 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,{lim}_{x\\to {(\\pi /2)}^{+}}f(x)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}" "\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,\\lim_{x\\to {(\\pi /2)}^{+}}f(x)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] [:db/cas 17592186594471 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {(\\pi /2)}^{+}}f(x)=0" "\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {(\\pi /2)}^{+}}f(x)=0"] [:db/cas 17592186594543 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=2,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {(\\pi /2)}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" "\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=2,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {(\\pi /2)}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}"] [:db/cas 17592186594670 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)=$" "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186594670 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3).$\n\n![](images/65/65420a63daa7a7c4f7a4c061bfc4e7fd223d28cc90f7fbc7e02d4d6f066ec968.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)=\\infty .\n$$" "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3).$\n\n![](images/65/65420a63daa7a7c4f7a4c061bfc4e7fd223d28cc90f7fbc7e02d4d6f066ec968.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)=\\infty .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186594671 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}{log}_{8}⁡(4-x)=$" "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}{log}_{8}⁡(4-x)=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186594671 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{8}⁡(4-x).$\n\n![](images/0b/0b3cd9b5d4110b8b76b3e165d75ee4d8019cc9cc17192f38e9f2751350e5ddc7.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\ge 4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}{log}_{8}⁡(4-x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x<4.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}{log}_{8}⁡(4-x)=-\\infty .\n$$" "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{8}⁡(4-x).$\n\n![](images/0b/0b3cd9b5d4110b8b76b3e165d75ee4d8019cc9cc17192f38e9f2751350e5ddc7.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\ge 4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}{log}_{8}⁡(4-x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x<4.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}{log}_{8}⁡(4-x)=-\\infty .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186594673 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{({x}^{2}-3)^{2}-8x+15}{{x}^{2}-4x+4}=$" "$\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{({x}^{2}-3)^{2}-8x+15}{{x}^{2}-4x+4}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186594677 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 16}\\frac{{x}^{2}-20x+64}{\\sqrt{x}-4}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,\\lim_{x\\to 16}\\frac{{x}^{2}-20x+64}{\\sqrt{x}-4}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186594701 :question/problem "Find the right-sided limit at $x=2$ for the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-16\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le 2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2x+5}-\\sqrt{2x}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Find the right-sided limit at $x=2$ for the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-16\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le 2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2x+5}-\\sqrt{2x}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186594707 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to 4}{log}_{5}⁡(4-x).$" "Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to 4}{log}_{5}⁡(4-x).$"] [:db/cas 17592186594707 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{5}⁡(4-x).$\n\n![](images/4c/4c8119ac59d4fe8102a4d78e10f32f1b3f0116366001b6a78d256b3928867943.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\ge 4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}{log}_{5}⁡(4-x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x<4.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}{log}_{5}⁡(4-x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are not equal, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 4}{log}_{5}⁡(4-x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$" "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{5}⁡(4-x).$\n\n![](images/4c/4c8119ac59d4fe8102a4d78e10f32f1b3f0116366001b6a78d256b3928867943.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\ge 4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}{log}_{5}⁡(4-x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x<4.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}{log}_{5}⁡(4-x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are not equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 4}{log}_{5}⁡(4-x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186594715 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{8x}-{e}^{5x}-3x}{{x}^{2}}=$" "$\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{8x}-{e}^{5x}-3x}{{x}^{2}}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186594719 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{ln\\,(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x))}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{ln\\,(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x))}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186595927 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to (-5/2)}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+5).$" "Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to (-5/2)}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+5).$"] [:db/cas 17592186595927 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+5)$\n\n![](images/f4/f43a774fdfe638719972b74163adc61dc1099ab0117bda1c5bcc648912fd7b25.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\le -\\frac{5}{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -5/{2}^{-}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+5)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x>-\\frac{5}{2}.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -5/{2}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+5)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are not equal, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -5/2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+5)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$" "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+5)$\n\n![](images/f4/f43a774fdfe638719972b74163adc61dc1099ab0117bda1c5bcc648912fd7b25.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\le -\\frac{5}{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -5/{2}^{-}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+5)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x>-\\frac{5}{2}.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -5/{2}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+5)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are not equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -5/2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+5)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186595955 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)}{(x+1)^{2}}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)}{(x+1)^{2}}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186595955 :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty$ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since $(x+1)^{2}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)}{(x+1)^{2}}=0.\n$$" "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty$ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since $(x+1)^{2}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)}{(x+1)^{2}}=0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186595957 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}{log}_{5}⁡(x-2).$" "Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}{log}_{5}⁡(x-2).$"] [:db/cas 17592186595957 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{5}⁡(x-2).$\n\n![](images/42/42585a4ff6db9c4848588f10ac50bc78aa4d902ddacdb201039aa7a558d3a3a9.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\le 2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}{log}_{5}⁡(x-2)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x>2.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}{log}_{5}⁡(x-2)=-\\infty .\n$$" "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{5}⁡(x-2).$\n\n![](images/42/42585a4ff6db9c4848588f10ac50bc78aa4d902ddacdb201039aa7a558d3a3a9.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\le 2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}{log}_{5}⁡(x-2)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x>2.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}{log}_{5}⁡(x-2)=-\\infty .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186595959 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{6x}{{e}^{x}}+3).$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{6x}{{e}^{x}}+3).$"] [:db/cas 17592186595959 :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty$ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{x}≫6x$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{6x}{{e}^{x}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{6x}{{e}^{x}}+3)=0+3=3.\n$$" "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty$ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{x}≫6x$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{6x}{{e}^{x}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{6x}{{e}^{x}}+3)=0+3=3.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186595964 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}+x}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}+x}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186595964 :question/worked-solution "The sine function is a bounded, oscillating function with\n\n$$\n|\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSo the numerator here is a bounded, oscillating function with \n\n$$\n1\\le 2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\le 3.\n$$\n\nHowever, the denominator ${x}^{2}+x$ grows without bound as $x\\to \\infty .$ Since the denominator grows much faster than the (bounded) numerator, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}+x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see this from the graph of $y=\\frac{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}+x},$ shown below:\n\n![](images/0e/0eb0389561fc6c754431fcf7fcb63f5b03ad80f4de6a34ab407dbbdc079e77c4.png)" "The sine function is a bounded, oscillating function with\n\n$$\n|\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSo the numerator here is a bounded, oscillating function with \n\n$$\n1\\le 2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\le 3.\n$$\n\nHowever, the denominator ${x}^{2}+x$ grows without bound as $x\\to \\infty .$ Since the denominator grows much faster than the (bounded) numerator, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}+x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see this from the graph of $y=\\frac{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}+x},$ shown below:\n\n![](images/0e/0eb0389561fc6c754431fcf7fcb63f5b03ad80f4de6a34ab407dbbdc079e77c4.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186595973 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x){e}^{-3x}+2).$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x){e}^{-3x}+2).$"] [:db/cas 17592186595980 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{3x}}+1)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{3x}}+1)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186596004 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)}+10)=$" "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)}+10)=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186596004 :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty$ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since $(x-3)^{2}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$ for large values of $x,$ the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)}=\\infty\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)}+10)=\\infty\n$$" "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty$ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since $(x-3)^{2}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$ for large values of $x,$ the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)}=\\infty\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)}+10)=\\infty\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186596005 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)}{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)}{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186596701 :question/problem "The function $g$ is defined as $$\n\\displaystyle g(x)={\\int }_{-5}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -5}\\,\\frac{g(x)}{{x}^{2}+12x+35}=$\n{{field-1}}." "The function $g$ is defined as $$\n\\displaystyle g(x)={\\int }_{-5}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to -5}\\,\\frac{g(x)}{{x}^{2}+12x+35}=$\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186599786 :question/problem "![](images/21/21d8823484fa5c17c1a0ea33a74a733116816100e169ca675c7f1216730e4328.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)$ for the function above." "![](images/21/21d8823484fa5c17c1a0ea33a74a733116816100e169ca675c7f1216730e4328.png)\n\nFind $\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)$ for the function above."] [:db/cas 17592186599786 :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $1,$ the value of $f(x)$ approaches $3.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=3\\,.\n$$\n\nRemember, it does not matter that the actual value of $f(1)$ is $2.$ All that matters is that as $x$ approaches $1,$ the function $f(x)$ *looks like* it is getting closer and closer to $3.$" "As $x$ approaches $1,$ the value of $f(x)$ approaches $3.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=3\\,.\n$$\n\nRemember, it does not matter that the actual value of $f(1)$ is $2.$ All that matters is that as $x$ approaches $1,$ the function $f(x)$ *looks like* it is getting closer and closer to $3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186601505 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}{log}_{6}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-2}{7}\\right)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}{log}_{6}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-2}{7}\\right)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186601505 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{6}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-2}{7}\\right).$\n\n![](images/9f/9f9d26034e3aa5010f6be4045393874de0268ac50226675ad8233dedb1cabefd.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\le 0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}{log}_{6}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-2}{7}\\right)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x>0.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}{log}_{6}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-2}{7}\\right)=-\\infty .\n$$" "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{6}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-2}{7}\\right).$\n\n![](images/9f/9f9d26034e3aa5010f6be4045393874de0268ac50226675ad8233dedb1cabefd.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\le 0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}{log}_{6}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-2}{7}\\right)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x>0.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}{log}_{6}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-2}{7}\\right)=-\\infty .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186601940 :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[4{(1+\\frac{k}{n})}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[4{(1+\\frac{k}{n})}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}"] [:db/cas 17592186601940 :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=1.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{1}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{n}=\\frac{b-1}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=2.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=1+\\frac{k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=4{x}_{k}^{4}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{2}4{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[4{(1+\\frac{k}{n})}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)=4{x}^{4}\\,\\text{and}\\,b=2.\n$$" "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=1.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{1}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{n}=\\frac{b-1}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=2.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=1+\\frac{k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=4{x}_{k}^{4}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{2}4{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[4{(1+\\frac{k}{n})}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)=4{x}^{4}\\,\\text{and}\\,b=2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186602406 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}\\,(-1-x-{x}^{2})=${{field-1}}" "$\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}\\,(-1-x-{x}^{2})=${{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186602410 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{4}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-2}{62+f(x)}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,{4}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-2}{62+f(x)}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{4}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-2}{62+f(x)}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{4}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-2}{62+f(x)}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186602422 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186602430 :question/problem "${f}^{′}(x)={lim}_{h\\to 0}(-3{x}^{2}-3xh-{h}^{2})=${{field-1}}" "${f}^{′}(x)=\\lim_{h\\to 0}(-3{x}^{2}-3xh-{h}^{2})=${{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186602437 :question/problem "The instantaneous rate of change of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(1)={lim}_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{f(x)-f(1)}{x-1}={lim}_{x\\to 1}(-1\\,-\n$$\n{{field-1}} \n$-\\,{x}^{2}).$" "The instantaneous rate of change of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(1)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{f(x)-f(1)}{x-1}=\\lim_{x\\to 1}(-1\\,-\n$$\n{{field-1}} \n$-\\,{x}^{2}).$"] [:db/cas 17592186602457 :question/problem "The derivative of $f(x)$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}={lim}_{h\\to \\,0}\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}={lim}_{h\\to \\,0}(-3{x}^{2}-\n$$\n{{field-1}} \n$-\\,{h}^{2}).$" "The derivative of $f(x)$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\lim_{h\\to \\,0}\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}=\\lim_{h\\to \\,0}(-3{x}^{2}-\n$$\n{{field-1}} \n$-\\,{h}^{2}).$"] [:db/cas 17592186603006 :question/problem "Write down an expression for the area $A$ as a definite integral, and express the integral as the limit of a left Riemann sum.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle A={\\int }_{2}^{5}\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}\n$$\n{{field-2}}" "Write down an expression for the area $A$ as a definite integral, and express the integral as the limit of a left Riemann sum.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle A={\\int }_{2}^{5}\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}\n$$\n{{field-2}}"] [:db/cas 17592186603039 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{2}-1}=${{field-1}}" "$\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{2}-1}=${{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186604024 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}{{x}^{4}}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}{{x}^{4}}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186604024 :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty$ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${x}^{4}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}{{x}^{4}}=0.\n$$" "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty$ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${x}^{4}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}{{x}^{4}}=0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186604025 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)}{{e}^{x}}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)}{{e}^{x}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186604025 :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty$ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{x}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)}{{e}^{x}}=0.\n$$" "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty$ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{x}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)}{{e}^{x}}=0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186604060 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{5}{{x}^{4}}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{5}{{x}^{4}}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186604080 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6x)\\,{e}^{-4x}-1)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6x)\\,{e}^{-4x}-1)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186605097 :question/problem "Evaluate the limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{|10x+7|}{2x+1}=$\n\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{|10x+7|}{2x+1}=\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186605110 :question/problem "![](images/70/701cd49e5926f07a4c3a9a145660aaa205119da1f4ccf8b174706fc8d70dec24.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. \n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{6}^{-}}f(x)$ is finite\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{6}^{+}}f(x)$ is **not** finite\n\nIII. $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-6$" "![](images/70/701cd49e5926f07a4c3a9a145660aaa205119da1f4ccf8b174706fc8d70dec24.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. \n\nI. $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{6}^{-}}f(x)$ is finite\n\nII. $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{6}^{+}}f(x)$ is **not** finite\n\nIII. $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-6$"] [:db/cas 17592186605110 :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is false. We get ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{6}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty ,$ which is not finite.\n\n- Statement II is true. We get ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{6}^{+}}f(x)=\\infty .$\n\n- Statement III is true. The graph of the function has a vertical asymptote exactly at $x=-6$ and it's impossible to draw its graph without taking our pencil off of the paper." "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is false. We get $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{6}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty ,$ which is not finite.\n\n- Statement II is true. We get $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{6}^{+}}f(x)=\\infty .$\n\n- Statement III is true. The graph of the function has a vertical asymptote exactly at $x=-6$ and it's impossible to draw its graph without taking our pencil off of the paper."] [:db/cas 17592186605113 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(9{x}^{9})=${{field-1}}" "$\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(9{x}^{9})=${{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186605134 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{u\\to -\\infty }{(3{u}^{3}-u)}^{5}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{u\\to -\\infty }{(3{u}^{3}-u)}^{5}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186605142 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(6{x}^{9})=$" "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(6{x}^{9})=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186605147 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{z\\to -\\infty }{(4{z}^{3}-3)}^{3}.$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{z\\to -\\infty }{(4{z}^{3}-3)}^{3}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186605854 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nTo find the limit, we calculate\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡n}{{e}^{n/3}}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡n}{{e}^{n/3}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges, and its limit is $0.$" "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nTo find the limit, we calculate\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡n}{{e}^{n/3}}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡n}{{e}^{n/3}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges, and its limit is $0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186605861 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(14n)}{n}-3)=-3+{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(14n)}{n}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(14n)}{n}=0.\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n-3+{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(14n)}{n}=-3+0=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges and its limit is $-3.$" "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(14n)}{n}-3)=-3+\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(14n)}{n}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(14n)}{n}=0.\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n-3+\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(14n)}{n}=-3+0=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges and its limit is $-3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186605886 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{n}}{4{n}^{2}}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{n}}{4{n}^{2}}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence diverges." "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{n}}{4{n}^{2}}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{n}}{4{n}^{2}}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence diverges."] [:db/cas 17592186610950 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\sqrt[3]{2-3x}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\sqrt[3]{2-3x}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186611600 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{6x}{\\sqrt{9x+1}-\\sqrt{6x+1}}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{6x}{\\sqrt{9x+1}-\\sqrt{6x+1}}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186611648 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1/2}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)).$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\,1/2}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)).$"] [:db/cas 17592186613214 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to (-1,-1)}x\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}.$" "Find $\\lim_{x\\to (-1,-1)}x\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186615373 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{y(x)-1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}=${{field-1}}" "$\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{y(x)-1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}=${{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186616147 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=$" "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186616147 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\n![](images/44/44ba48c2eebd00aac41e09413f328906eea3148be2fcec2ed4700852d7bd4802.png)\n\nAs $x$ decreases, the values of $y$ range between $-\\infty$ and $\\infty$ periodically with period $\\pi .$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\text{DNE.}\n$$" "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\n![](images/44/44ba48c2eebd00aac41e09413f328906eea3148be2fcec2ed4700852d7bd4802.png)\n\nAs $x$ decreases, the values of $y$ range between $-\\infty$ and $\\infty$ periodically with period $\\pi .$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\text{DNE.}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186619722 :question/worked-solution "To determine whether $f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=c,$ we need to check whether the left-sided limit is equal to the function value.\n\nLet's check this for $x=-3$, at $x=1$ and at  $x=4.$\n\n- At $x=-3$ the function is left-continuous because\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-3)^{-}}f(x)=-2=f(-3).\n$$\n\n- At $x=1$ the function is left-continuous because\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=-2=f(1).\n$$\n\n- At $x=4$ the function is not left-continuous because it's undefined at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=-3$ and $x=1$ only." "To determine whether $f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=c,$ we need to check whether the left-sided limit is equal to the function value.\n\nLet's check this for $x=-3$, at $x=1$ and at  $x=4.$\n\n- At $x=-3$ the function is left-continuous because\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-3)^{-}}f(x)=-2=f(-3).\n$$\n\n- At $x=1$ the function is left-continuous because\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=-2=f(1).\n$$\n\n- At $x=4$ the function is not left-continuous because it's undefined at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=-3$ and $x=1$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186619758 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=3$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)=-2,$ then what is ${lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}(f(x)-f(3))?$" "If $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=3$ and $\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)=-2,$ then what is $\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}(f(x)-f(3))?$"] [:db/cas 17592186619781 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{2x}{3}\\right).$" "Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{2x}{3}\\right).$"] [:db/cas 17592186619786 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x.$" "Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x.$"] [:db/cas 17592186619814 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to 0}2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" "Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to 0}2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$"] [:db/cas 17592186620817 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{5}+4}{3x-6}.$" "Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{5}+4}{3x-6}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186629292 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{6x}=$" "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{6x}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186629313 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nTo find the limit, we calculate\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{n}}{{log}_{4}⁡n}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{n}}{{log}_{4}⁡n}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence diverges." "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nTo find the limit, we calculate\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{n}}{{log}_{4}⁡n}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{n}}{{log}_{4}⁡n}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence diverges."] [:db/cas 17592186630260 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=-1$ and $f(-1)=16,$ then what is \n${lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}\\sqrt{4f(x)}?$" "If $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=-1$ and $f(-1)=16,$ then what is \n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}\\sqrt{4f(x)}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186634587 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{-2n/3}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{-2n/3}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186634644 :question/problem "${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{-\\pi n/2}=$" "$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{-\\pi n/2}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186634665 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{4n}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{4n}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186638264 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x}+3).$" "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x}+3).$"] [:db/cas 17592186638264 :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty$ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{3x}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x$ for large values of $x,$ the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x}+3)=\\infty .\n$$" "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty$ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{3x}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x$ for large values of $x,$ the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x}+3)=\\infty .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186638638 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=\\frac{1}{8}" "\\lim_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=\\frac{1}{8}"] [:db/cas 17592186638678 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)=3" "\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)=3"] [:db/cas 17592186638695 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=-1" "\\lim_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=-1"] [:db/cas 17592186638697 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" "\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)=\\text{DNE}"] [:db/cas 17592186638763 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)=\\frac{2}{3}" "\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)=\\frac{2}{3}"] [:db/cas 17592186638829 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)=2" "\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)=2"] [:db/cas 17592186638853 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" "\\lim_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=\\text{DNE}"] [:db/cas 17592186638946 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=0" "\\lim_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=0"] [:db/cas 17592186638976 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)=0" "\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)=0"] [:db/cas 17592186638993 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=-2" "\\lim_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=-2"] [:db/cas 17592186639256 :question/worked-solution "To compute ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(1/2)}f(x),$ we must compute the left and right-sided limits separately.\n\nHowever, the left-sided limit ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(1/2)^{-}}f(x)$ does not exist since $f(x)$ is not defined on the interval $x\\in (-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$ \n\nConsequently, the overall limit does not exist either:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$" "To compute $\\lim_{x\\to \\,(1/2)}f(x),$ we must compute the left and right-sided limits separately.\n\nHowever, the left-sided limit $\\lim_{x\\to \\,(1/2)^{-}}f(x)$ does not exist since $f(x)$ is not defined on the interval $x\\in (-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$ \n\nConsequently, the overall limit does not exist either:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186639267 :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the left, we have $x<0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=x.$ We get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}x=0\\,.\n$$\n\nApproaching from the right, we have $x>0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=3.$ We get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}3=3\\,.\n$$\n\nSince the two limits are different, we can conclude that \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$" "Approaching from the left, we have $x<0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}x=0\\,.\n$$\n\nApproaching from the right, we have $x>0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}3=3\\,.\n$$\n\nSince the two limits are different, we can conclude that \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186647763 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7x)}{(x-1)^{4}}-2)=$" "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7x)}{(x-1)^{4}}-2)=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186647763 :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty$ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since $(x-1)^{4}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7x)$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7x)}{(x-1)^{4}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7x)}{(x-1)^{4}}-2)=0-2=-2\n$$" "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty$ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since $(x-1)^{4}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7x)$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7x)}{(x-1)^{4}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7x)}{(x-1)^{4}}-2)=0-2=-2\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186650331 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\left(\\frac{5}{3{x}^{4}}\\right)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\left(\\frac{5}{3{x}^{4}}\\right)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186654751 :question/problem "Calculate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n{x}^{3}/3}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Calculate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n{x}^{3}/3}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186654792 :question/problem "Calculate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{nx/y}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Calculate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{nx/y}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186655762 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,(\\pi /2)^{+}}f(x)=3" "\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\,(\\pi /2)^{+}}f(x)=3"] [:db/cas 17592186655855 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,(\\pi /2)^{+}}f(x)=1" "\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,(\\pi /2)^{+}}f(x)=1"] [:db/cas 17592186655943 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,(\\pi /2)^{+}}f(x)=3" "\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\,(\\pi /2)^{+}}f(x)=3"] [:db/cas 17592186656013 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,(\\pi /2)^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" "\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\,(\\pi /2)^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}"] [:db/cas 17592186656029 :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=0,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,(\\pi /2)^{+}}f(x)=1" "\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=0,\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\,(\\pi /2)^{+}}f(x)=1"] [:db/cas 17592186660350 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 36}\\frac{x-36}{\\sqrt{x}-6}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 36}\\frac{x-36}{\\sqrt{x}-6}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186664092 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{4{n}^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡n}+2)=2+{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{n}^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡n}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{n}^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡n}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{4{n}^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡n}+2)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence diverges" "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{4{n}^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡n}+2)=2+\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{n}^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡n}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{n}^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡n}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{4{n}^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡n}+2)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence diverges"] [:db/cas 17592186667622 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{2}}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(6n)}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{2}}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(6n)}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence diverges" "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{2}}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(6n)}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{2}}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(6n)}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence diverges"]]
